{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj96kqpK1NCS9UdFZhCa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0181tsrAnLJe25CRJSiW1GhH","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"sed -n '318,356p;462,524p' root-README.md","description":"Read the hub README's friends and session sections"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25072,"cache_read_input_tokens":47551,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25072},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"55c989f0-7e81-4778-8972-5fef7bef9393","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:14.049Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj96kaBVCGgFpZVsj8eg","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_0181tsrAnLJe25CRJSiW1GhH":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work/_scratch/hub && sed -n '318,356p;462,524p' root-README.md","description":"Read the hub README's friends and session sections"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_0181tsrAnLJe25CRJSiW1GhH":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work/_scratch/hub"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0181tsrAnLJe25CRJSiW1GhH","type":"tool_result","content":"when you switch, and so does a search, as far as it is plain words. Hidden\ngames stay hidden in all seven.\n\nPlaying with friends is never more than a glance away. Beside the account\ncontrol in every view's corner sits the one light button, **Play with\nfriends**, to the Friends view. Every game that plays in a session says so\nwhere you would play it: *With friends* on its cabinet, *Play with friends*\nunder Play in its details (the wall, the Atlas, the Timeline, the Catalogue's\nrecord), on the Couch's hero and game page, and on the Deck's card. Each of\nthem opens the game's page in the Friends view.\n\n### Friends — `/friends`\n\nThe hub as a game-night console, for playing with other people:\n\n- **The party.** *Host a game night* (Google sign-in, then the session's\n  lobby with an invite link), *Got an invite?* (paste the link, the code, or\n  a message with the link in it; it finds the ten-character code), and *Try a\n  session on this computer*, in up to four tabs with the hub's practice games.\n- **Made for game night**: the games built for sessions by hand (Blaze Cup,\n  Merry-Go-Kart 64, Grand Theft Motto III, Arwingdings, Brine Line 64,\n  Urethane, Slick & Skid; `SPOTLIGHT` in `assets/js/friends/together.js`),\n  else the newest shared worlds.\n- **How a session works**, in three steps, and the **ways to win**.\n- **Games to play together**: every game that plays in a session, filed by\n  how it plays — *One world for everyone* (shared world), *Race each other's\n  ghosts* (ghost), *Same start, side by side* (parallel) — and *Couch co-op*,\n  the games that seat several players on one screen by themselves (their\n  `players`). The toolbar narrows them by the party's size (2, 3, 4), the way\n  it plays, a session round's length and a search; each choice shows how\n  many games it leaves. A browser with no WebGPU is told how many of them need\n  it, and those cards say so.\n- **A game's page** (`/friends#/g/<slug>`): how it plays together, its goal,\n  controls, seats and round length, and the ways to start it: *Host with this\n  game* (a session that opens with it, then deals the rest), *Try it on this\n  computer*, and *Practise alone* in the hub's player. A couch co-op game has\n  *Play on this screen*.\n\n### The Couch — `/couch`\n## Playing together: AI Hub sessions\n\nEvery game can be played with up to three friends, each on their own device,\nthrough an **AI Hub session**: the hub deals the games (Random Mode), keeps\nthe clock, carries the network traffic and keeps score. There is no chat and\nno way for players to message each other; they talk in an app of their own.\n\nWhat a game must do is the **AI Hub Session Protocol**, specified in\n[`lib/hub/README.md`](lib/hub/README.md) and served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>. That page is the\nwhole interface: it is the one thing handed to whichever agent builds a game.\nThe page holds only the interface and the hub's facts: no example values,\nsuggested numbers or design advice, since an agent takes any of those as the\nway to build the game. A game declares one of three modes:\n\n- **Parallel**: everyone plays their own copy, from the same\n  start, on the same clock; the hub draws a live scoreboard from each copy's\n  progress. Every game with a score or a finish can do it, with no networking,\n  and it is how the existing library joins sessions.\n- **Ghost**: parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters while they are in the same area of the game, without them\n  touching its world.\n- **Shared world**: one world for everyone, run by the host's copy.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"6fc289dc-73e9-4ab6-84f1-fcf95709a251","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:14.074Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"when you switch, and so does a search, as far as it is plain words. Hidden\ngames stay hidden in all seven.\n\nPlaying with friends is never more than a glance away. Beside the account\ncontrol in every view's corner sits the one light button, **Play with\nfriends**, to the Friends view. Every game that plays in a session says so\nwhere you would play it: *With friends* on its cabinet, *Play with friends*\nunder Play in its details (the wall, the Atlas, the Timeline, the Catalogue's\nrecord), on the Couch's hero and game page, and on the Deck's card. Each of\nthem opens the game's page in the Friends view.\n\n### Friends — `/friends`\n\nThe hub as a game-night console, for playing with other people:\n\n- **The party.** *Host a game night* (Google sign-in, then the session's\n  lobby with an invite link), *Got an invite?* (paste the link, the code, or\n  a message with the link in it; it finds the ten-character code), and *Try a\n  session on this computer*, in up to four tabs with the hub's practice games.\n- **Made for game night**: the games built for sessions by hand (Blaze Cup,\n  Merry-Go-Kart 64, Grand Theft Motto III, Arwingdings, Brine Line 64,\n  Urethane, Slick & Skid; `SPOTLIGHT` in `assets/js/friends/together.js`),\n  else the newest shared worlds.\n- **How a session works**, in three steps, and the **ways to win**.\n- **Games to play together**: every game that plays in a session, filed by\n  how it plays — *One world for everyone* (shared world), *Race each other's\n  ghosts* (ghost), *Same start, side by side* (parallel) — and *Couch co-op*,\n  the games that seat several players on one screen by themselves (their\n  `players`). The toolbar narrows them by the party's size (2, 3, 4), the way\n  it plays, a session round's length and a search; each choice shows how\n  many games it leaves. A browser with no WebGPU is told how many of them need\n  it, and those cards say so.\n- **A game's page** (`/friends#/g/<slug>`): how it plays together, its goal,\n  controls, seats and round length, and the ways to start it: *Host with this\n  game* (a session that opens with it, then deals the rest), *Try it on this\n  computer*, and *Practise alone* in the hub's player. A couch co-op game has\n  *Play on this screen*.\n\n### The Couch — `/couch`\n## Playing together: AI Hub sessions\n\nEvery game can be played with up to three friends, each on their own device,\nthrough an **AI Hub session**: the hub deals the games (Random Mode), keeps\nthe clock, carries the network traffic and keeps score. There is no chat and\nno way for players to message each other; they talk in an app of their own.\n\nWhat a game must do is the **AI Hub Session Protocol**, specified in\n[`lib/hub/README.md`](lib/hub/README.md) and served at\n<https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md>. That page is the\nwhole interface: it is the one thing handed to whichever agent builds a game.\nThe page holds only the interface and the hub's facts: no example values,\nsuggested numbers or design advice, since an agent takes any of those as the\nway to build the game. A game declares one of three modes:\n\n- **Parallel**: everyone plays their own copy, from the same\n  start, on the same clock; the hub draws a live scoreboard from each copy's\n  progress. Every game with a score or a finish can do it, with no networking,\n  and it is how the existing library joins sessions.\n- **Ghost**: parallel, and each copy also shows the other players'\n  characters while they are in the same area of the game, without them\n  touching its world.\n- **Shared world**: one world for everyone, run by the host's copy.\n\nGames tagged with the older modes keep working: `race` plays as ghost, `co-op`\nand `versus` as a shared world.\n\nIn short: the `hub:session` metas in the head, the small `session.js` client\npasted in unmodified as the first script, a round reported with its stats and\nawards, and outside a session the game plays exactly as it always would. What\nthe hub does around a round (points, awards, formats, the deal, the\nscoreboard, what travels where) is in\n[`docs/session-hub.md`](docs/session-hub.md). The plan behind all of it, and\nwhy, is [`docs/multiplayer-plan.md`](docs/multiplayer-plan.md).\n\nA game that declares it shows a *Hub session* line in its details\n(`1–4 players · Parallel · about 4 min`), and the Catalogue finds them all\nwith `has:session`. Those fields never become rails. A parallel game is\nnot a multiplayer game on the wall: each player plays alone.\n\nGames that play in sessions include **Blaze Cup** (a shared world, in teams),\n**Arwingdings**, **Merry-Go-Kart 64**, **Brine Line 64** and **Urethane\nSwitchyard** (tagged race, so played as ghost), and **Grand Theft Motto III**\n(a shared world: the whole campaign, together).\n\nTo test a game against the protocol, open the harness, which loads 1–4 copies\nnext to each other on a simulated network:\n\n```\npnpm dev\nhttp://localhost:7417/lib/hub/harness.html?game=/games/mygame.html&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5\n```\n\n`node scripts/hub-check.mjs games/mygame.html` runs the automatic conformance\nchecks with Playwright: a parallel game at 1 and 2 players, a ghost game or a\nshared world at 2, 3 and 4 on a bad network (within the seats it declares),\neach with a player leaving, then the game on its own. `--record` writes the\nverdict into `data/overrides.json`, where a failing game gets `\"session\": \"0\"`\nand is never dealt. The three examples in `lib/hub/examples/` (**Starfall**,\nparallel; **Comet Dash**, ghost; **Star Arena**, a shared world) are the hub's\nreference games and pass it; the protocol page does not link them, so no agent\ncopies them.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_0181tsrAnLJe25CRJSiW1GhH","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgj96kqpK1NCS9UdFZhCa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01FmGWL141mxnGc13LhqDtDR","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"curl -fsS -o hub-check.mjs \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/scripts/hub-check.mjs\" && curl -fsS -o check3d.py \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/scripts/check3d.py\" && curl -fsS -o multiplayer-plan.md \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/docs/multiplayer-plan.md\"; wc -l hub-check.mjs check3d.py multiplayer-plan.md; sed -n 1,60p hub-check.mjs; echo ...; grep -n -iE 'couch|on its own|local|same screen|gamepads|keyboard' multiplayer-plan.md | head -40","description":"Download hub-check, check3d and the multiplayer plan and skim them","timeout":60000},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25072,"cache_read_input_tokens":47551,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25072},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"8e0d964c-a641-4328-9b5a-e6ec0ce65bd2","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:17.730Z","request_id":"req_011Cgj96kaBVCGgFpZVsj8eg","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01FmGWL141mxnGc13LhqDtDR":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work/_scratch/hub && curl -fsS -o hub-check.mjs \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/scripts/hub-check.mjs\" && curl -fsS -o check3d.py \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/scripts/check3d.py\" && curl -fsS -o multiplayer-plan.md \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/docs/multiplayer-plan.md\"; wc -l hub-check.mjs check3d.py multiplayer-plan.md; sed -n 1,60p hub-check.mjs; echo ...; grep -n -iE 'couch|on its own|local|same screen|gamepads|keyboard' multiplayer-plan.md | head -40","description":"Download hub-check, check3d and the multiplayer plan and skim them","timeout":60000}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01FmGWL141mxnGc13LhqDtDR":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work/_scratch/hub"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FmGWL141mxnGc13LhqDtDR","type":"tool_result","content":"  370 hub-check.mjs\n  455 check3d.py\n  605 multiplayer-plan.md\n 1430 total\n#!/usr/bin/env node\n/**\n * hub-check.mjs — does a game keep the AI Hub Session Protocol?\n *\n * Runs lib/hub/harness.html?auto=1 against each game, at the player counts its\n * hub:session-players allows: hello, ready under the hub's card with no key\n * press, the start, traffic, a player leaving, time-up and a valid result. Then\n * once more with the host leaving. Then the game on its own, outside a session:\n * it opens, Enter and Space are pressed, and it must draw its screen with no\n * errors, the session client inactive and Math.random left alone. Before any\n * game script runs, every frame in session mode gets alert, confirm, prompt,\n * window.open and socket constructors wrapped, so calling them is caught.\n * Needs Playwright, which is not a dependency (see shots.mjs).\n *\n * Parallel is played at 1 and 2 players: nothing is shared, so more copies\n * prove nothing more. Its checks expect Math.random seeded the same on every\n * copy, progress from every copy, and no game traffic.\n *\n * Ghost and shared world are played at 2, 3 and 4 players on a poor network\n * (150 ± 50 ms, 5% loss). Ghost is checked like parallel, except that every\n * copy sends its player. A shared world wants traffic both ways, and is\n * played once more at 2 players with its world hosted by one more copy that\n * has no player of its own, as a dedicated server would host it (README,\n * \"Shared world\"). That run is reported like any; --record does not hold it\n * against a game, since no server runs yet.\n *\n * --retrofit is for an existing game upgraded to parallel: it is played once,\n * at 2 players with one of them leaving. With --original <path>, errors the\n * original page already had are forgiven when it is opened on its own.\n * --draws-only does only that last part, for any page: the retrofit runs it on\n * a game before spending a model on it, since a page this browser cannot run\n * cannot be checked here. A WebGPU-only game without WebGPU is caught even\n * when its title screen shows: it asked for an adapter, got none, and drew\n * nothing with WebGL or a 2D canvas instead. --browser-args passes flags to\n * Chromium; on a machine without a GPU, SwiftShader gives such games a\n * software adapter:\n *   --browser-args \"--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader\"\n *\n * Every game's pasted client is also read from disk and must be exactly a\n * released one (scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs).\n *\n * --record writes the outcome into data/overrides.json, which is how the hub\n * keeps a failing game out of sessions: a game that fails gets \"session\": \"0\"\n * (it overrides the game's own hub:session meta, so Random Mode never deals\n * it) and a sessionNote naming what failed; a game that passes loses both.\n *\n * Usage:  node scripts/hub-check.mjs [game paths…] [--players 2,3,4] [--base URL] [--json] [--no-solo]\n *                                    [--retrofit [--original <path>]] [--draws-only] [--record] [--browser-args \"<flags>\"]\n *         (games default to the three examples in lib/hub/examples/)\n */\n\nimport { createServer } from 'node:http';\nimport { createRequire } from 'node:module';\nimport { mkdir, readdir, readFile, writeFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\nimport path from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n\nimport { staticHandler } from './lib/serve.mjs';\nimport { modeOf, SELF_REPORTED } from '../lib/hub/round.js';\nimport { findClients } from './lib/hub-client.mjs';\n...\n154:| 5 Couch and remote couch | Couch co-op on one screen stays each game's own (up to four controllers), unchanged. Remote couch co-op is **pinned**: friends far away would need the host's screen streamed to them as video |\n226:   progress and a seeded start expected; and the game on its own compared\n377:  a round (about 1,400 games use `localStorage`), and the real saves are\n398:  agent on its own copy of the game, spend capped, every failure put back);\n477:  (`localStorage['ai-hub:auth']`). Serving the frames from a second hostname\n479:  frames (the splash's pad buttons, Create + Options, the Couch's pad\n504:- Remote couch co-op: friends far away playing a game made for one screen, on\n507:  Couch co-op in one room is the game's own and is unaffected.\n529:- **Up to four players locally, controller first**: welcome. Couch co-op in\n530:  one room is the game's own; remote couch co-op is pinned.\n542:| 1 | Decisions 3 and 4 | Decided Oct 29: state, not video, and no video anywhere (remote couch pinned); dedicated servers prepared in the games now, built when wanted |\n574:- **Realtime**: 205 broadcasts in an 80 s, 2-round, 4-player local session","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"5e40657c-9e8c-43d9-ad73-a0b881d52bed","timestamp":"2026-11-05T04:21:18.374Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"  370 hub-check.mjs\n  455 check3d.py\n  605 multiplayer-plan.md\n 1430 total\n#!/usr/bin/env node\n/**\n * hub-check.mjs — does a game keep the AI Hub Session Protocol?\n *\n * Runs lib/hub/harness.html?auto=1 against each game, at the player counts its\n * hub:session-players allows: hello, ready under the hub's card with no key\n * press, the start, traffic, a player leaving, time-up and a valid result. Then\n * once more with the host leaving. Then the game on its own, outside a session:\n * it opens, Enter and Space are pressed, and it must draw its screen with no\n * errors, the session client inactive and Math.random left alone. Before any\n * game script runs, every frame in session mode gets alert, confirm, prompt,\n * window.open and socket constructors wrapped, so calling them is caught.\n * Needs Playwright, which is not a dependency (see shots.mjs).\n *\n * Parallel is played at 1 and 2 players: nothing is shared, so more copies\n * prove nothing more. Its checks expect Math.random seeded the same on every\n * copy, progress from every copy, and no game traffic.\n *\n * Ghost and shared world are played at 2, 3 and 4 players on a poor network\n * (150 ± 50 ms, 5% loss). Ghost is checked like parallel, except that every\n * copy sends its player. A shared world wants traffic both ways, and is\n * played once more at 2 players with its world hosted by one more copy that\n * has no player of its own, as a dedicated server would host it (README,\n * \"Shared world\"). That run is reported like any; --record does not hold it\n * against a game, since no server runs yet.\n *\n * --retrofit is for an existing game upgraded to parallel: it is played once,\n * at 2 players with one of them leaving. With --original <path>, errors the\n * original page already had are forgiven when it is opened on its own.\n * --draws-only does only that last part, for any page: the retrofit runs it on\n * a game before spending a model on it, since a page this browser cannot run\n * cannot be checked here. A WebGPU-only game without WebGPU is caught even\n * when its title screen shows: it asked for an adapter, got none, and drew\n * nothing with WebGL or a 2D canvas instead. --browser-args passes flags to\n * Chromium; on a machine without a GPU, SwiftShader gives such games a\n * software adapter:\n *   --browser-args \"--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader\"\n *\n * Every game's pasted client is also read from disk and must be exactly a\n * released one (scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs).\n *\n * --record writes the outcome into data/overrides.json, which is how the hub\n * keeps a failing game out of sessions: a game that fails gets \"session\": \"0\"\n * (it overrides the game's own hub:session meta, so Random Mode never deals\n * it) and a sessionNote naming what failed; a game that passes loses both.\n *\n * Usage:  node scripts/hub-check.mjs [game paths…] [--players 2,3,4] [--base URL] [--json] [--no-solo]\n *                                    [--retrofit [--original <path>]] [--draws-only] [--record] [--browser-args \"<flags>\"]\n *         (games default to the three examples in lib/hub/examples/)\n */\n\nimport { createServer } from 'node:http';\nimport { createRequire } from 'node:module';\nimport { mkdir, readdir, readFile, writeFile } from 'node:fs/promises';\nimport path from 'node:path';\nimport { fileURLToPath } from 'node:url';\n\nimport { staticHandler } from './lib/serve.mjs';\nimport { modeOf, SELF_REPORTED } from '../lib/hub/round.js';\nimport { findClients } from './lib/hub-client.mjs';\n...\n154:| 5 Couch and remote couch | Couch co-op on one screen stays each game's own (up to four controllers), unchanged. Remote couch co-op is **pinned**: friends far away would need the host's screen streamed to them as video |\n226:   progress and a seeded start expected; and the game on its own compared\n377:  a round (about 1,400 games use `localStorage`), and the real saves are\n398:  agent on its own copy of the game, spend capped, every failure put back);\n477:  (`localStorage['ai-hub:auth']`). Serving the frames from a second hostname\n479:  frames (the splash's pad buttons, Create + Options, the Couch's pad\n504:- Remote couch co-op: friends far away playing a game made for one screen, on\n507:  Couch co-op in one room is the game's own and is unaffected.\n529:- **Up to four players locally, controller first**: welcome. Couch co-op in\n530:  one room is the game's own; remote couch co-op is pinned.\n542:| 1 | Decisions 3 and 4 | Decided Oct 29: state, not video, and no video anywhere (remote couch pinned); dedicated servers prepared in the games now, built when wanted |\n574:- **Realtime**: 205 broadcasts in an 80 s, 2-round, 4-player local session","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01FmGWL141mxnGc13LhqDtDR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
