{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019m4nyRGtkawwHZfgZ3VaEe","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Director's Plan\n2\t\n3\tBuild the game from this document. Nobody can answer questions, so where the plan is silent, decide and move on. TASK.md stays the authority on what to deliver, how it is named, what to report and how you may test. This plan decides what the game is and how it is built. Paths are relative to your dev folder.\n4\t\n5\t**How to read the numbers.** ✔ means I ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with three@0.180.0 and @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3 and it behaved as described. No mark means a starting value: tune it with the dev sliders and the balance test (section 17).\n6\t\n7\t**Contents:** 1 The game · 2 Originality rules · 3 Controls · 4 The balance model · 5 Programme and progress · 6 Events · 7 Screens · 8 Cast and writing · 9 Art bible · 10 Animation · 11 Audio · 12 Multiplayer · 13 Attract mode · 14 Dev mode · 15 Touch-only · 16 Architecture and build · 17 Testing · 18 Build order and cut order · 19 Done · 20 What was and wasn't verified\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t## 1. The game\n12\t\n13\t**Pitch.** A wooden-toy wellness retreat stands on an island balanced on a single rock, and the island has been crooked for as long as anyone remembers. You arrive as a new guest, a jointed peg doll. Every morning a fussy talking spirit level named Trudy checks you in, asks something too personal and tells you your Wobble Age. Then you go to class: hold poses with a heron, do reps under a log for a walrus, step to city pop on the bandstand, catch fruit in a basket hat, stack stones on a slab over your head. Every class pours marbles into the counterweight jar, and the whole island visibly straightens, day by day, until the morning Trudy's bubble sits in the middle for the first time.\n14\t\n15\t**Title.** Do TASK.md's title step first, since it names your folder and file. The title is a joke on the source's name, never the name itself. Keep it in one constant (`src/data/config.js`). Nothing in the fiction depends on it: the resort is the Level Best Retreat, and the cast and events have their own names.\n16\t\n17\t**Genre:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression. **Perspective:** third person. Each event has its own framed stage camera. On the island it is a chase camera you can orbit.\n18\t\n19\t| What makes the source itself | Our version |\n20\t|---|---|\n21\t| You see your own balance as a dot in a target | The Balance Disc, in every event, driven by real physics |\n22\t| A rectangle given a personality, who asks awkward questions and notices when you skip days | Trudy, a talking spirit level whose bubble is her eye |\n23\t| Calm, slightly uncanny instructors; \"the trainer notices when you slack off\" | Ms. Stillwater (a heron) and Coach Bolster (a walrus), who both notice |\n24\t| A daily test that hands you an embarrassing age | The Check-In and your Wobble Age |\n25\t| Four kinds of training | Stillness, Strength, Cardio, Balance Play |\n26\t| Minutes trained fill a bank that opens new things | Marbles pour into a glass jar, physically |\n27\t| The family takes turns and laughs at each other | Group Class on one TV; an AI Hub class across devices |\n28\t| Absurd little games between the earnest ones | Fruit Hat, Springboard, Tippy Raft |\n29\t\n30\t**Pillars. When in doubt, serve these.**\n31\t1. **One verb: shift your weight.** Every event reads the same Balance Dot from the same physics. Learn it once and you can play everything.\n32\t2. **Physics tells the jokes.** Falling is the best moment in the game: clattering wooden limbs, a straight-faced line from Trudy, a spring back up. Failure is never punishing.\n33\t3. **A gentle ritual.** Check in, train, watch the marbles, go to bed. No skill gate ever stops the story.\n34\t4. **The world is the progress bar.** The lighthouse leans 9° on Day 1 and stands straight on the last day. Loose props slide to the low side. The music is slightly out of tune until the island is level.\n35\t5. **The room laughs.** It is played on a TV at 10 feet, and it should be funny to watch.\n36\t\n37\t**The first five minutes** (build this exact beat, it is the game's first impression):\n38\t1. Title screen at dawn. The crooked island fills the frame. The title's letters stand on a wooden beam that tilts with the island, and they slide a little. After 10 idle seconds, attract mode starts.\n39\t2. Press to start. \"Who's here today?\" Four empty mats. Pick one. The name spinner offers a generated name (\"Gentle Margaret\"); reroll or accept. Pick a paint colour. Under a minute.\n40\t3. You drop onto the island from above, clatter, spring up. Trudy hops over, bubble sliding to the high side. \"Oh! A person. Please step on. Not on me. On the island.\"\n41\t4. Trudy asks you to lean left. As you do, **the whole island tips under you** and every orange on the island rolls. No menu has taught this; the world did. Lean right, then forward. \"I can feel that, by the way.\"\n42\t5. First Check-In. Three short tests. The drum roll. \"Your Wobble Age is 74. Some of my favourite people are 74. Mostly the ones sitting down.\"\n43\t6. Walk to the Sun Deck (or pick it from Today's Plan) for your first pose with Ms. Stillwater, then Fruit Hat on the pier, where you get greedy, fall over, and laugh.\n44\t7. Marbles pour into the jar. The island creaks and comes up one notch. The lighthouse is visibly straighter. Bed.\n45\t\n46\t---\n47\t\n48\t## 2. Originality rules\n49\t\n50\tThis is an original game in the source's genre, with similar kinds of mechanics. It is not a recreation.\n51\t\n52\t- **Never use** the source's name (outside the title's joke and the About screen's Source line), its product or feature names (balance board, the words it uses for its test, age or bank), its characters, its avatar style, its music or its look. No white-and-pale-blue gym, no plain white-clad instructor, no Mii-style avatars (ours are jointed wooden peg dolls), no talking board, no piggy bank.\n53\t- **Never copy its activities.** No ski jump, slalom, soccer heading, table tilt with balls and holes, tightrope, hula hoop, penguin slide, river bubble, snowboard or boxing. The events in section 6 replace them with our own ideas. Add new ones in that spirit, not from that list.\n54\t- **Tone guard.** Trudy teases posture, knees, commitment and attendance. **Nothing in the game comments on body weight, body shape or food.** There is no scale, no BMI and no weight number. \"Weight\" only ever means where you are leaning. No real health claims.\n55\t- All art, music, text and voices are made for this game, in code.\n56\t\n57\t---\n58\t\n59\t## 3. Controls\n60\t\n61\tWeight comes from a stick on a controller and from the pointer with a mouse. Both are analog, both feel native. **Stick and mouse are always screen-relative:** up is away from the camera, right is screen right. Rotate the input by the camera's yaw before using it.\n62\t\n63\t| Verb | Controller (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n64\t|---|---|---|---|\n65\t| Shift weight (events) / walk (island) | Left stick, axes 0 and 1 | W A S D or arrow keys | Pointer's offset from screen centre, normalised by 33% of screen height, clamped to the unit disc |\n66\t| Left foot | L2, button 6, analog `.value` | Q | Left button |\n67\t| Right foot | R2, button 7, analog `.value` | E | Right button |\n68\t| Hop / both feet | Cross, button 0 | Space | Both buttons |\n69\t| Flail | Square, button 2 | F | Middle button |\n70\t| Breathe in (hold) | R1, button 5 | Left Shift | |\n71\t| Ask Trudy (island) | Triangle, button 3 | T | |\n72\t| Look (island only) | Right stick, axes 2 and 3 | | Hold right button and drag; wheel zooms |\n73\t| Use / confirm | Cross | Enter or E (island), Enter or Space (menus) | Left click |\n74\t| Back | Circle, button 1 | Esc or Backspace | Right click (menus only; suppress the context menu) |\n75\t| Pause | Options, button 9 | Esc | |\n76\t| Menu move | D-pad or left stick | Arrow keys or W A S D | Hover moves the focus |\n77\t\n78\t- **No pointer lock is needed**, which keeps the game simple inside the hub's frames.\n79\t- **Keyboard feel:** weight keys ramp to full over 0.45 s and fall back over 0.25 s, so tapping gives small corrections. Foot keys ramp 0 to 1 in 0.12 s.\n80\t- **On the island,** a hovered thing (pavilion, jar, board, Trudy) gets a thick outline and a label. Left click walks there and uses it. WASD and E work without the mouse. Hit-test by projecting each hotspot's anchor to screen and taking the nearest within a radius. It is simple and robust.\n81\t- **Prompts follow the last input.** Track `lastDevice` (`pad` or `kbm`): a pad counts when a stick passes 0.25 or a button is pressed; keyboard and mouse count on keydown, mousedown or 4+ px of movement. Prompts re-render the same frame. A Settings option switches pad glyphs between PlayStation shapes and Xbox letters (A B X Y, LT RT LB RB). Keyboard prompts show keycaps, mouse prompts show a mouse with the button lit. Draw all glyphs as inline SVG in code. Every glyph element carries a `data-glyph` attribute that tests can read.\n82\t- **On the island,** Cross and E use the focused thing if there is one; otherwise Cross and Space hop.\n83\t- **Create + Options belongs to the hub.** Ignore Options while Create (button 8) is held, everywhere.\n84\t- **If a player's pad disconnects mid-class,** pause and say so on screen.\n85\t- **Haptics:** `pad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on falls, catches, rising fatigue and near-tip. Guard it, with a Settings toggle.\n86\t\n87\t---\n88\t\n89\t## 4. The balance model\n90\t\n91\tThis is the heart of the game and it is the most tested part of this plan. Copy it, then tune it.\n92\t\n93\t**The doll's \"balance core\"** is one Rapier dynamic body on a ball joint at the foot. Everything in the game that stands uses it. Its colliders can carry props, and those props push back on it through real contacts, so a heavy hat makes you top-heavy without any special code.\n94\t\n95\t```js\n96\t// m = 1, h = 0.75 (foot to centre of mass), doll stands 1.5 m tall. Fixed step 1/120 s.\n97\tconst body = world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()\n98\t  .setTranslation(fx, fy + 0.75, fz).setCanSleep(false).setAdditionalSolverIterations(4));\n99\tworld.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.capsule(0.5, 0.25).setMass(1).setCollisionGroups(DOLL(lane)), body);\n100\tconst joint = world.createImpulseJoint(\n101\t  RAPIER.JointData.spherical({x:0,y:0,z:0}, {x:0,y:-0.75,z:0}), footBody, body, true);\n102\tjoint.setContactsEnabled(false);            // footBody is kinematicPositionBased\n103\t\n104\tconst G = 9.81 * 0.75, I = 0.75 * 0.75 + 0.06;   // gravity moment, inertia about the foot\n105\tconst K = G * (1 + margin), wn = Math.sqrt((K - G) / I), D = 2 * zeta * wn * I;\n106\t\n107\t// Each fixed step, before world.step():\n108\tconst r = 1 - Math.exp(-reflex * dt);\n109\twx += (wishX - wx) * r;  wz += (wishZ - wz) * r;       // wish = the input, on the unit disc\n110\tconst tx = wx * maxLean, tz = wz * maxLean;            // target lean in radians\n111\tconst q = body.rotation();\n112\tconst ux = 2*(q.x*q.y - q.w*q.z), uy = 1 - 2*(q.x*q.x + q.z*q.z), uz = 2*(q.y*q.z + q.w*q.x);\n113\tleanX = Math.atan2(ux, uy);  leanZ = Math.atan2(uz, uy);   // lean toward +x and +z\n114\tconst w = body.angvel();\n115\tlet Tx = -(G*Math.sin(tx) + K*(leanX - tx)) + D*w.z;    // gravity feed-forward, spring, damper\n116\tlet Tz = -(G*Math.sin(tz) + K*(leanZ - tz)) - D*w.x;\n117\tconst limit = ankleMax * (flailLeft > 0 ? flailBoost : 1);\n118\tconst m = Math.hypot(Tx, Tz);  if (m > limit) { Tx *= limit/m; Tz *= limit/m; }\n119\t// nx, nz are the disturbances (sway, tremor, gusts, shoves), in the same lean space, not clamped\n120\tbody.resetTorques(true);\n121\tbody.addTorque({ x: Tz + nz, y: -w.y * 0.6, z: -(Tx + nx) }, true);\n122\t```\n123\t\n124\t**Defaults ✔:** `maxLean` 18°, `margin` 0.45, `zeta` 0.40, `ankleMax` 4.6 N·m, `reflex` 10 /s, `flailBoost` 1.9, flail lasts 0.35 s with a 2 s cooldown, fall at 45° of lean.\n125\t\n126\t**What those numbers do ✔:**\n127\t- Full stick from rest reaches 90% in about 1 s, overshoots to about 23° and settles at 18°. It is a soft, wobbly, toy-like feel.\n128\t- Whipping the stick end to end at about 0.5 Hz survives. Slamming it from −18° to +18° peaks near 28° at `ankleMax` 4.2 and up, and falls at 3.8. This is why careful correction matters.\n129\t- The torque limit makes a natural point of no return at `asin(ankleMax / G)`, about 39° with no load. Hat and stone loads lower it automatically.\n130\t\n131\t**Gusts at the defaults ✔ (0.5 s long; these numbers are the difficulty dial):**\n132\t\n133\t| Gust | Do nothing | Flail as you pass about 30° | Stick against it once it has started | Braced: stick at 60% against it, 0.5 s before it hits |\n134\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n135\t| 2 N·m | safe, peaks 22° | | | |\n136\t| 2.5 N·m | safe, peaks 31° | | | |\n137\t| 3 N·m | **falls** | saved, peaks 38° | falls | safe, peaks 19° |\n138\t| 3.5 N·m | falls | saved only if timed near 32°, peaks 43° | falls | safe, peaks 29° |\n139\t| 4 N·m | falls | falls | falls | falls |\n140\t\n141\tSo a telegraph is worth a lot: **give a warning before every gust of 3 N·m or more.** And flail is a real last-moment save with timing in it: a flail used early, before you have moved much, wears off and you still fall. It is the moment people will shout at the TV.\n142\t\n143\t**The load rule ✔.** Every prop adds a gravity moment δ = mass × 9.81 × height. The doll grows unstable when total δ exceeds K − G (3.3 at the default margin). A prop's mass therefore decides how many you can carry. Too heavy and the doll tips over at once: with margin 0.45, 0.06 kg fruit tipped it at the 5th or 6th catch, while 0.015 kg works out to about 13. **Keep prop masses small** (0.01 to 0.03 kg) against the doll's 1 kg.\n144\t\n145\t**Slow sway is what makes holding still a game ✔.** A thumb is far steadier than a body on a board, so a player who simply parks the stick on a target and leaves it scored 100% in my first test. Fast tremor barely moves the dot. The fix is slow forcing that the player has to see and counter by hand. Add it to `nx`, `nz`:\n146\t\n147\t```js\n148\tnx = sway  *(Math.sin(t*0.9 + ph)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*1.73 + ph*2.1)*0.4)\n149\t   + tremor*(Math.sin(t*8.2 + 1.1)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*18.3)*0.4);\n150\tnz = sway  *(Math.sin(t*0.77 + ph*1.7)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*1.51 + ph*0.6)*0.4)\n151\t   + tremor*(Math.sin(t*7.1 + 2.3)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*15.7 + 0.7)*0.4);   // ph from the event's seed\n152\t```\n153\tSway units are N·m. Sway of 0.2 moves the dot about a quarter of the disc if ignored. Tremor is mostly for looks and rumble.\n154\t\n155\t**Traps I hit ✔:**\n156\t- **The doll's colliders must not touch the ground.** The capsule's foot rotating into the floor silently limits lean to about 8° and every event feels dead. Use collision groups: `(membership << 16) | filter`. Bits: `GROUND 0x0001`, props of lane *i* `0x0010 << i`, doll of lane *i* `0x0100 << i`, ragdoll parts `0x1000`. A doll meets only its own lane's props. A prop meets its own lane's doll, its own lane's props and the ground. Ragdoll parts meet only the ground. Lanes never touch each other.\n157\t- **Stacked things use cuboid colliders.** Convex-hull stones collapsed by themselves with no disturbance at all (5th stone, every seed). Plain cuboids held. Draw a rounded stone over a box collider.\n158\t- **No physical step bob.** With a kinematic foot bobbing 3 cm at walking pace, everything left the tray within 20 s. Bob the visual only. Ramp foot speed up over 1.5 s.\n159\t- Use `world.integrationParameters.numSolverIterations = 8` to `10` for events with props.\n160\t\n161\t**The Balance Disc.** `cob = (leanX, leanZ) / maxLean` is the dot, shown truthfully. It is the game's signature HUD: a large circular dial with rings, a target zone that turns green when you are inside, the solid dot, a faint ring showing where your stick is asking to go, and a short inked trail of the last half second, like a seismograph needle. Diameter is about 22% of screen height, bottom left. Trudy peeks over its rim and reacts. In Group Class each player gets a smaller one (14%) along the bottom edge.\n162\t\n163\t**Falling.** At 45° of lean the doll becomes a ragdoll (section 10) with a clatter of wooden clacks, a quip, a rumble and a comic \"CLACK!\" word in the world. After `recoverTime` (2.2 s) the parts snap back with a spring noise and the doll bounces upright. Only the current hold or rep is lost. Falls are counted in the Journal as Floor Exercises.\n164\t\n165\t**Flail.** One button, all devices. Arms windmill for 0.35 s and the ankle gets `flailBoost` strength. Cooldown is 2 s. It looks ridiculous on purpose. It is the save that makes near-falls exciting, and it only works if you time it (see the table above). A flail costs a small form penalty in Stillness events, and the instructor comments.\n166\t\n167\t**Breath.** In Stillness events a breath ring pulses: 4 s growing, 4 s shrinking. Hold Breathe while it grows, release while it shrinks. Over the last full cycle, 70%+ in sync multiplies sway and tremor by 0.6 and form gain by 1.25. Out of sync does nothing bad except miss the bonus. Accessibility toggle: auto-breathe.\n168\t\n169\t**Difficulty** (Settings): Gentle, Standard (default), Honest. It multiplies `ankleMax` by 1.25 / 1 / 0.9, disturbances by 0.65 / 1 / 1.2 and rhythm windows by 1.4 / 1 / 0.8.\n170\t\n171\t---\n172\t\n173\t## 5. Programme and progress\n174\t\n175\t**Days are game days, not calendar days.** A day ends when you go to bed. The real date is only used for absence jokes and calendar stamps."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"69ff7919-2ef3-43ff-8756-6997af61dd1e","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:09:04.578Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Director's Plan\n\nBuild the game from this document. Nobody can answer questions, so where the plan is silent, decide and move on. TASK.md stays the authority on what to deliver, how it is named, what to report and how you may test. This plan decides what the game is and how it is built. Paths are relative to your dev folder.\n\n**How to read the numbers.** ✔ means I ran it on this computer on 2026-11-04 with three@0.180.0 and @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3 and it behaved as described. No mark means a starting value: tune it with the dev sliders and the balance test (section 17).\n\n**Contents:** 1 The game · 2 Originality rules · 3 Controls · 4 The balance model · 5 Programme and progress · 6 Events · 7 Screens · 8 Cast and writing · 9 Art bible · 10 Animation · 11 Audio · 12 Multiplayer · 13 Attract mode · 14 Dev mode · 15 Touch-only · 16 Architecture and build · 17 Testing · 18 Build order and cut order · 19 Done · 20 What was and wasn't verified\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** A wooden-toy wellness retreat stands on an island balanced on a single rock, and the island has been crooked for as long as anyone remembers. You arrive as a new guest, a jointed peg doll. Every morning a fussy talking spirit level named Trudy checks you in, asks something too personal and tells you your Wobble Age. Then you go to class: hold poses with a heron, do reps under a log for a walrus, step to city pop on the bandstand, catch fruit in a basket hat, stack stones on a slab over your head. Every class pours marbles into the counterweight jar, and the whole island visibly straightens, day by day, until the morning Trudy's bubble sits in the middle for the first time.\n\n**Title.** Do TASK.md's title step first, since it names your folder and file. The title is a joke on the source's name, never the name itself. Keep it in one constant (`src/data/config.js`). Nothing in the fiction depends on it: the resort is the Level Best Retreat, and the cast and events have their own names.\n\n**Genre:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression. **Perspective:** third person. Each event has its own framed stage camera. On the island it is a chase camera you can orbit.\n\n| What makes the source itself | Our version |\n|---|---|\n| You see your own balance as a dot in a target | The Balance Disc, in every event, driven by real physics |\n| A rectangle given a personality, who asks awkward questions and notices when you skip days | Trudy, a talking spirit level whose bubble is her eye |\n| Calm, slightly uncanny instructors; \"the trainer notices when you slack off\" | Ms. Stillwater (a heron) and Coach Bolster (a walrus), who both notice |\n| A daily test that hands you an embarrassing age | The Check-In and your Wobble Age |\n| Four kinds of training | Stillness, Strength, Cardio, Balance Play |\n| Minutes trained fill a bank that opens new things | Marbles pour into a glass jar, physically |\n| The family takes turns and laughs at each other | Group Class on one TV; an AI Hub class across devices |\n| Absurd little games between the earnest ones | Fruit Hat, Springboard, Tippy Raft |\n\n**Pillars. When in doubt, serve these.**\n1. **One verb: shift your weight.** Every event reads the same Balance Dot from the same physics. Learn it once and you can play everything.\n2. **Physics tells the jokes.** Falling is the best moment in the game: clattering wooden limbs, a straight-faced line from Trudy, a spring back up. Failure is never punishing.\n3. **A gentle ritual.** Check in, train, watch the marbles, go to bed. No skill gate ever stops the story.\n4. **The world is the progress bar.** The lighthouse leans 9° on Day 1 and stands straight on the last day. Loose props slide to the low side. The music is slightly out of tune until the island is level.\n5. **The room laughs.** It is played on a TV at 10 feet, and it should be funny to watch.\n\n**The first five minutes** (build this exact beat, it is the game's first impression):\n1. Title screen at dawn. The crooked island fills the frame. The title's letters stand on a wooden beam that tilts with the island, and they slide a little. After 10 idle seconds, attract mode starts.\n2. Press to start. \"Who's here today?\" Four empty mats. Pick one. The name spinner offers a generated name (\"Gentle Margaret\"); reroll or accept. Pick a paint colour. Under a minute.\n3. You drop onto the island from above, clatter, spring up. Trudy hops over, bubble sliding to the high side. \"Oh! A person. Please step on. Not on me. On the island.\"\n4. Trudy asks you to lean left. As you do, **the whole island tips under you** and every orange on the island rolls. No menu has taught this; the world did. Lean right, then forward. \"I can feel that, by the way.\"\n5. First Check-In. Three short tests. The drum roll. \"Your Wobble Age is 74. Some of my favourite people are 74. Mostly the ones sitting down.\"\n6. Walk to the Sun Deck (or pick it from Today's Plan) for your first pose with Ms. Stillwater, then Fruit Hat on the pier, where you get greedy, fall over, and laugh.\n7. Marbles pour into the jar. The island creaks and comes up one notch. The lighthouse is visibly straighter. Bed.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Originality rules\n\nThis is an original game in the source's genre, with similar kinds of mechanics. It is not a recreation.\n\n- **Never use** the source's name (outside the title's joke and the About screen's Source line), its product or feature names (balance board, the words it uses for its test, age or bank), its characters, its avatar style, its music or its look. No white-and-pale-blue gym, no plain white-clad instructor, no Mii-style avatars (ours are jointed wooden peg dolls), no talking board, no piggy bank.\n- **Never copy its activities.** No ski jump, slalom, soccer heading, table tilt with balls and holes, tightrope, hula hoop, penguin slide, river bubble, snowboard or boxing. The events in section 6 replace them with our own ideas. Add new ones in that spirit, not from that list.\n- **Tone guard.** Trudy teases posture, knees, commitment and attendance. **Nothing in the game comments on body weight, body shape or food.** There is no scale, no BMI and no weight number. \"Weight\" only ever means where you are leaning. No real health claims.\n- All art, music, text and voices are made for this game, in code.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Controls\n\nWeight comes from a stick on a controller and from the pointer with a mouse. Both are analog, both feel native. **Stick and mouse are always screen-relative:** up is away from the camera, right is screen right. Rotate the input by the camera's yaw before using it.\n\n| Verb | Controller (standard mapping) | Keyboard | Mouse |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Shift weight (events) / walk (island) | Left stick, axes 0 and 1 | W A S D or arrow keys | Pointer's offset from screen centre, normalised by 33% of screen height, clamped to the unit disc |\n| Left foot | L2, button 6, analog `.value` | Q | Left button |\n| Right foot | R2, button 7, analog `.value` | E | Right button |\n| Hop / both feet | Cross, button 0 | Space | Both buttons |\n| Flail | Square, button 2 | F | Middle button |\n| Breathe in (hold) | R1, button 5 | Left Shift | |\n| Ask Trudy (island) | Triangle, button 3 | T | |\n| Look (island only) | Right stick, axes 2 and 3 | | Hold right button and drag; wheel zooms |\n| Use / confirm | Cross | Enter or E (island), Enter or Space (menus) | Left click |\n| Back | Circle, button 1 | Esc or Backspace | Right click (menus only; suppress the context menu) |\n| Pause | Options, button 9 | Esc | |\n| Menu move | D-pad or left stick | Arrow keys or W A S D | Hover moves the focus |\n\n- **No pointer lock is needed**, which keeps the game simple inside the hub's frames.\n- **Keyboard feel:** weight keys ramp to full over 0.45 s and fall back over 0.25 s, so tapping gives small corrections. Foot keys ramp 0 to 1 in 0.12 s.\n- **On the island,** a hovered thing (pavilion, jar, board, Trudy) gets a thick outline and a label. Left click walks there and uses it. WASD and E work without the mouse. Hit-test by projecting each hotspot's anchor to screen and taking the nearest within a radius. It is simple and robust.\n- **Prompts follow the last input.** Track `lastDevice` (`pad` or `kbm`): a pad counts when a stick passes 0.25 or a button is pressed; keyboard and mouse count on keydown, mousedown or 4+ px of movement. Prompts re-render the same frame. A Settings option switches pad glyphs between PlayStation shapes and Xbox letters (A B X Y, LT RT LB RB). Keyboard prompts show keycaps, mouse prompts show a mouse with the button lit. Draw all glyphs as inline SVG in code. Every glyph element carries a `data-glyph` attribute that tests can read.\n- **On the island,** Cross and E use the focused thing if there is one; otherwise Cross and Space hop.\n- **Create + Options belongs to the hub.** Ignore Options while Create (button 8) is held, everywhere.\n- **If a player's pad disconnects mid-class,** pause and say so on screen.\n- **Haptics:** `pad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on falls, catches, rising fatigue and near-tip. Guard it, with a Settings toggle.\n\n---\n\n## 4. The balance model\n\nThis is the heart of the game and it is the most tested part of this plan. Copy it, then tune it.\n\n**The doll's \"balance core\"** is one Rapier dynamic body on a ball joint at the foot. Everything in the game that stands uses it. Its colliders can carry props, and those props push back on it through real contacts, so a heavy hat makes you top-heavy without any special code.\n\n```js\n// m = 1, h = 0.75 (foot to centre of mass), doll stands 1.5 m tall. Fixed step 1/120 s.\nconst body = world.createRigidBody(RAPIER.RigidBodyDesc.dynamic()\n  .setTranslation(fx, fy + 0.75, fz).setCanSleep(false).setAdditionalSolverIterations(4));\nworld.createCollider(RAPIER.ColliderDesc.capsule(0.5, 0.25).setMass(1).setCollisionGroups(DOLL(lane)), body);\nconst joint = world.createImpulseJoint(\n  RAPIER.JointData.spherical({x:0,y:0,z:0}, {x:0,y:-0.75,z:0}), footBody, body, true);\njoint.setContactsEnabled(false);            // footBody is kinematicPositionBased\n\nconst G = 9.81 * 0.75, I = 0.75 * 0.75 + 0.06;   // gravity moment, inertia about the foot\nconst K = G * (1 + margin), wn = Math.sqrt((K - G) / I), D = 2 * zeta * wn * I;\n\n// Each fixed step, before world.step():\nconst r = 1 - Math.exp(-reflex * dt);\nwx += (wishX - wx) * r;  wz += (wishZ - wz) * r;       // wish = the input, on the unit disc\nconst tx = wx * maxLean, tz = wz * maxLean;            // target lean in radians\nconst q = body.rotation();\nconst ux = 2*(q.x*q.y - q.w*q.z), uy = 1 - 2*(q.x*q.x + q.z*q.z), uz = 2*(q.y*q.z + q.w*q.x);\nleanX = Math.atan2(ux, uy);  leanZ = Math.atan2(uz, uy);   // lean toward +x and +z\nconst w = body.angvel();\nlet Tx = -(G*Math.sin(tx) + K*(leanX - tx)) + D*w.z;    // gravity feed-forward, spring, damper\nlet Tz = -(G*Math.sin(tz) + K*(leanZ - tz)) - D*w.x;\nconst limit = ankleMax * (flailLeft > 0 ? flailBoost : 1);\nconst m = Math.hypot(Tx, Tz);  if (m > limit) { Tx *= limit/m; Tz *= limit/m; }\n// nx, nz are the disturbances (sway, tremor, gusts, shoves), in the same lean space, not clamped\nbody.resetTorques(true);\nbody.addTorque({ x: Tz + nz, y: -w.y * 0.6, z: -(Tx + nx) }, true);\n```\n\n**Defaults ✔:** `maxLean` 18°, `margin` 0.45, `zeta` 0.40, `ankleMax` 4.6 N·m, `reflex` 10 /s, `flailBoost` 1.9, flail lasts 0.35 s with a 2 s cooldown, fall at 45° of lean.\n\n**What those numbers do ✔:**\n- Full stick from rest reaches 90% in about 1 s, overshoots to about 23° and settles at 18°. It is a soft, wobbly, toy-like feel.\n- Whipping the stick end to end at about 0.5 Hz survives. Slamming it from −18° to +18° peaks near 28° at `ankleMax` 4.2 and up, and falls at 3.8. This is why careful correction matters.\n- The torque limit makes a natural point of no return at `asin(ankleMax / G)`, about 39° with no load. Hat and stone loads lower it automatically.\n\n**Gusts at the defaults ✔ (0.5 s long; these numbers are the difficulty dial):**\n\n| Gust | Do nothing | Flail as you pass about 30° | Stick against it once it has started | Braced: stick at 60% against it, 0.5 s before it hits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 N·m | safe, peaks 22° | | | |\n| 2.5 N·m | safe, peaks 31° | | | |\n| 3 N·m | **falls** | saved, peaks 38° | falls | safe, peaks 19° |\n| 3.5 N·m | falls | saved only if timed near 32°, peaks 43° | falls | safe, peaks 29° |\n| 4 N·m | falls | falls | falls | falls |\n\nSo a telegraph is worth a lot: **give a warning before every gust of 3 N·m or more.** And flail is a real last-moment save with timing in it: a flail used early, before you have moved much, wears off and you still fall. It is the moment people will shout at the TV.\n\n**The load rule ✔.** Every prop adds a gravity moment δ = mass × 9.81 × height. The doll grows unstable when total δ exceeds K − G (3.3 at the default margin). A prop's mass therefore decides how many you can carry. Too heavy and the doll tips over at once: with margin 0.45, 0.06 kg fruit tipped it at the 5th or 6th catch, while 0.015 kg works out to about 13. **Keep prop masses small** (0.01 to 0.03 kg) against the doll's 1 kg.\n\n**Slow sway is what makes holding still a game ✔.** A thumb is far steadier than a body on a board, so a player who simply parks the stick on a target and leaves it scored 100% in my first test. Fast tremor barely moves the dot. The fix is slow forcing that the player has to see and counter by hand. Add it to `nx`, `nz`:\n\n```js\nnx = sway  *(Math.sin(t*0.9 + ph)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*1.73 + ph*2.1)*0.4)\n   + tremor*(Math.sin(t*8.2 + 1.1)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*18.3)*0.4);\nnz = sway  *(Math.sin(t*0.77 + ph*1.7)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*1.51 + ph*0.6)*0.4)\n   + tremor*(Math.sin(t*7.1 + 2.3)*0.6 + Math.sin(t*15.7 + 0.7)*0.4);   // ph from the event's seed\n```\nSway units are N·m. Sway of 0.2 moves the dot about a quarter of the disc if ignored. Tremor is mostly for looks and rumble.\n\n**Traps I hit ✔:**\n- **The doll's colliders must not touch the ground.** The capsule's foot rotating into the floor silently limits lean to about 8° and every event feels dead. Use collision groups: `(membership << 16) | filter`. Bits: `GROUND 0x0001`, props of lane *i* `0x0010 << i`, doll of lane *i* `0x0100 << i`, ragdoll parts `0x1000`. A doll meets only its own lane's props. A prop meets its own lane's doll, its own lane's props and the ground. Ragdoll parts meet only the ground. Lanes never touch each other.\n- **Stacked things use cuboid colliders.** Convex-hull stones collapsed by themselves with no disturbance at all (5th stone, every seed). Plain cuboids held. Draw a rounded stone over a box collider.\n- **No physical step bob.** With a kinematic foot bobbing 3 cm at walking pace, everything left the tray within 20 s. Bob the visual only. Ramp foot speed up over 1.5 s.\n- Use `world.integrationParameters.numSolverIterations = 8` to `10` for events with props.\n\n**The Balance Disc.** `cob = (leanX, leanZ) / maxLean` is the dot, shown truthfully. It is the game's signature HUD: a large circular dial with rings, a target zone that turns green when you are inside, the solid dot, a faint ring showing where your stick is asking to go, and a short inked trail of the last half second, like a seismograph needle. Diameter is about 22% of screen height, bottom left. Trudy peeks over its rim and reacts. In Group Class each player gets a smaller one (14%) along the bottom edge.\n\n**Falling.** At 45° of lean the doll becomes a ragdoll (section 10) with a clatter of wooden clacks, a quip, a rumble and a comic \"CLACK!\" word in the world. After `recoverTime` (2.2 s) the parts snap back with a spring noise and the doll bounces upright. Only the current hold or rep is lost. Falls are counted in the Journal as Floor Exercises.\n\n**Flail.** One button, all devices. Arms windmill for 0.35 s and the ankle gets `flailBoost` strength. Cooldown is 2 s. It looks ridiculous on purpose. It is the save that makes near-falls exciting, and it only works if you time it (see the table above). A flail costs a small form penalty in Stillness events, and the instructor comments.\n\n**Breath.** In Stillness events a breath ring pulses: 4 s growing, 4 s shrinking. Hold Breathe while it grows, release while it shrinks. Over the last full cycle, 70%+ in sync multiplies sway and tremor by 0.6 and form gain by 1.25. Out of sync does nothing bad except miss the bonus. Accessibility toggle: auto-breathe.\n\n**Difficulty** (Settings): Gentle, Standard (default), Honest. It multiplies `ankleMax` by 1.25 / 1 / 0.9, disturbances by 0.65 / 1 / 1.2 and rhythm windows by 1.4 / 1 / 0.8.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Programme and progress\n\n**Days are game days, not calendar days.** A day ends when you go to bed. The real date is only used for absence jokes and calendar stamps.","numLines":175,"startLine":1,"totalLines":778}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_019m4nyRGtkawwHZfgZ3VaEe","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
