{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EHUqvauzogA6uRWMXtwuXZ","type":"tool_result","content":"356\t## 8. Cast and writing\n357\t\n358\t**Writing style.** Short sentences. Polite. Second person. Chirpy and calm, with something slightly off. Instructions are plain and exact, then a small, sincere, odd aside. Praise is mild and specific. Every failure gets a line that makes it fine. The joke is the straight face. Write at least 200 Trudy lines, 60 for each instructor, 60 for guests, in `src/data/dialogue.js`, by situation, picked without repeats.\n359\t\n360\t**Trudy, Head of Alignment.** A chunky spirit level on two stubby pegs. Her bubble is her eye: it slides to the high side of the world, and to wherever she is feeling. She has been crooked with the island since she was installed and thinks of this as normal. Fussy aunt energy. Keeps count of everything and insists she isn't. She gets attached to you and would die before saying so.\n361\t\n362\t- \"Oh! You came back. I wasn't counting. It's been one day, four hours and some minutes.\"\n363\t- \"Please step on. Not on me. On the island. I'm a level, not a bath mat.\"\n364\t- \"Lean left. Your other left. No, that was correct. I was testing you.\"\n365\t- \"That was nearly level! I got a little emotional. My bubble went sideways.\"\n366\t- \"Let's call that a floor exercise.\"\n367\t- \"You fell very consistently. Consistency is important.\"\n368\t- \"It's been three days. The island tilted a bit. I'm not saying those things are connected. They are connected.\"\n369\t- \"Your Wobble Age is 61. That's down from 74! If this continues, you will eventually be born.\"\n370\t- \"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\"\n371\t- *Personal questions, always Yes/No, always a reply either way.* \"Do you trust your knees?\" Yes: \"They speak highly of you too.\" No: \"They know.\" · \"Do you ever stand on one leg without knowing why?\" Yes: \"I thought so.\" No: \"That's what someone on one leg would say.\" · \"Have you been leaning on things? Be honest. I can tell.\" · \"When you walk into a room, which way do you lean first?\" Left / Right: \"Interesting. I'm writing that down.\"\n372\t\n373\t**Ms. Stillwater, Stillness.** A tall heron on one leg. Half-closed eyes. A voice like a slow door. She has never blinked on camera. She notices everything and is not bothered by any of it.\n374\t\n375\t- \"Breathe in. Breathe out. Not like that, but thank you for trying.\"\n376\t- \"This pose is called the Apologetic Pine. Feel your weight. Feel it apologise.\"\n377\t- \"If you are shaking, that is only your body leaving.\"\n378\t- \"A heron can stand like this for hours. A heron has also never been asked why.\"\n379\t- \"You got off the mat. I saw. It's all right. I see everything. It's all right.\"\n380\t\n381\t**Coach Bolster, Strength and Cardio.** A vast walrus in a sweatband with a whistle he always forgets to blow. Loves you unconditionally and loudly. Bad at counting, on purpose.\n382\t\n383\t- \"YOU'RE DOING IT. I don't know what it is, but you're doing it.\"\n384\t- \"That's the last one! ...THIS one's the last one. I've lied to you and I'd do it again.\"\n385\t- \"I believe in you as much as I believe in lunch.\"\n386\t- \"Your form is a form! That's what form is!\"\n387\t\n388\t**Guests,** all peg dolls in different paint and hats. They wander, watch your classes and react: Gentle Margaret, Retired Kevin, Both Sandras (two dolls, always together, always agreeing), Unsteady Paul, Young Gerald (92). **Gary** is a gull. He steals things and lands on you, turning up in several events. In the finale he sits on your head and Trudy says, \"He's part of it now.\" Nobody ever mentions that everyone is a wooden toy.\n389\t\n390\t---\n391\t\n392\t## 9. Art bible\n393\t\n394\tThe look is **sun-bleached Japanese resort postcard meets wooden toy box, drawn in ink.** I rendered this cast and these materials with the recipe below and they read clearly on a TV-sized frame ✔.\n395\t\n396\t**Palette ✔.** Ink `#1A1420` on everything. Seat colours are the hub's: `#E8443A`, `#3B82F6`, `#22C55E`, `#F5B700`.\n397\t\n398\t| Time | skyTop | skyMid | skyLow | sea | seaHi | sun, intensity | hemi sky, ground, intensity |\n399\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n400\t| dawn ✔ | 8FB4E0 | F6CFD8 | FFE3B5 | 3E8FB5 | A9E3E4 | FFE6D8, 2.6 | FCE8F0, 7C8BB5, 1.35 |\n401\t| noon | 6FC3E8 | BFE9F0 | FFF2D0 | 16A8B0 | 8CF0DD | FFFBE8, 3.2 | FFFFFF, A09080, 1.35 |\n402\t| golden ✔ | 7FB7C9 | F7D9A6 | F59E6C | 1F9EA3 | 71DCCB | FFF0D0, 3.1 | FFF4E0, 8A7FA0, 1.25 |\n403\t| dusk ✔ | 2B2F6B | A8558F | F58A5C | 24406F | C26A8C | FFB98A, 2.4 | B48AD0, 2B2F5F, 1.1 |\n404\t\n405\tLerp palette uniforms and lights over 3 s between steps.\n406\t\n407\t**Materials ✔.** `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color, gradientMap })` with a 3-step `DataTexture` ramp `[96, 178, 255]`, `NearestFilter`, `NoColorSpace`. `renderer.toneMapping = THREE.NoToneMapping` keeps the palette true. Sky and sea use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial` with a TSL `colorNode`. Anything that should get a hull outline must be a toon material.\n408\t\n409\t**Shadows ✔.** `renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true`, `PCFShadowMap`, one sun with a 2048 map, `shadow.bias = -0.0006`, `shadow.normalBias = 0.035`. Without those two, the toon surfaces are covered in acne.\n410\t\n411\t**The ink recipe ✔** (runs on WebGPU and on the WebGL2 fallback, tested in Chromium and WebKit):\n412\t\n413\t```js\n414\timport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\n415\timport { mrt, output, normalView, toonOutlinePass, color, float, vec2, vec4, uv, screenSize, Fn, abs, max,\n416\t  smoothstep, mix, uniform, perspectiveDepthToViewZ, time, length, hash, floor, oneMinus } from 'three/tsl';\n417\t\n418\texport function makeInk(renderer, scene, camera) {\n419\t  const INK = 0x1a1420;\n420\t  const u = { hull: uniform(0.0028), width: uniform(1.4), boil: uniform(0.5), fadeNear: uniform(30), fadeFar: uniform(90),\n421\t              grain: uniform(0.02), near: uniform(camera.near), far: uniform(camera.far), ink: uniform(new THREE.Color(INK)) };\n422\t  const scenePass = toonOutlinePass(scene, camera, color(INK), u.hull, float(1));\n423\t  scenePass.setMRT(mrt({ output, normal: normalView }));\n424\t  const colorTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('output'), normalTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('normal'),\n425\t        depthTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('depth');\n426\t  const post = new THREE.PostProcessing(renderer);\n427\t  post.outputNode = Fn(() => {\n428\t    const cell = floor(uv().mul(vec2(40, 23)));\n429\t    const wob = hash(floor(time.mul(8)).add(cell.x).add(cell.y.mul(17))).sub(0.5).mul(u.boil);   // hand-drawn line boil\n430\t    const px = u.width.add(wob).div(screenSize);\n431\t    const viewZ = (o) => perspectiveDepthToViewZ(depthTex.sample(uv().add(o)).r, u.near, u.far);\n432\t    const n = (o) => normalTex.sample(uv().add(o)).xyz;\n433\t    const dx = vec2(px.x, 0), dy = vec2(0, px.y);\n434\t    const z0 = viewZ(vec2(0, 0)).abs();\n435\t    const dz = max(abs(viewZ(dx).sub(viewZ(dx.negate()))), abs(viewZ(dy).sub(viewZ(dy.negate())))).div(z0.max(0.5));\n436\t    const dn = max(length(n(dx).sub(n(dx.negate()))), length(n(dy).sub(n(dy.negate()))));\n437\t    const e = max(smoothstep(0.05, 0.12, dz), smoothstep(0.7, 1.1, dn)).mul(oneMinus(smoothstep(u.fadeNear, u.fadeFar, z0)));\n438\t    const c = mix(colorTex.rgb, u.ink, e);\n439\t    const p = floor(uv().mul(screenSize));\n440\t    const g = hash(p.x.add(p.y.mul(1571))).sub(0.5).mul(u.grain);\n441\t    return vec4(c.add(g).mul(oneMinus(length(uv().sub(0.5)).mul(0.62).pow(2.2))), 1);\n442\t  })();\n443\t  return { post, u };\n444\t}\n445\t// Render with post.render(). Remember: camera far changes need u.far updated.\n446\t```\n447\tGotchas ✔:\n448\t- TSL `hash()` takes **one float**. Give it a vec2 and you get vertical streaks over the whole image.\n449\t- `fadeNear/fadeFar` removes depth-line noise at the horizon, which otherwise draws a thick black band. Make sure the sea disc is big (radius 590) and blends to `skyLow` with distance.\n450\t- Do not merge vertices on hard-edged props: smooth normals smear the toon shading. Plain `BoxGeometry` looks right, and the depth lines cover the hull's corners.\n451\t- `InstancedMesh` with `setColorAt` works with toon and the hull ✔. Use it for marbles, splash blobs, fruit crowds.\n452\t- Glass ✔: toon material, `transparent: true, opacity: 0.16, side: DoubleSide, depthWrite: false`, colour `#E8FBFF`, with a hull outline. Good jar.\n453\t- No TSL compute anywhere. It breaks the WebGL2 fallback. All particles are CPU-driven instances.\n454\t\n455\t**Sea ✔.** `length(positionWorld.xz)` into a `sin` with a small domain warp and `time`, then `step(0.86, wave)` between `sea` and `seaHi` for hard bands, mixed to `skyLow` by distance from the camera (60 to 260 units). Add a foam ring near the island by distance from its centre, plus rings that follow the tilt.\n456\t\n457\t**Sky ✔.** Inverted sphere radius 600, `mix` of the three colours on the view direction's y (`smoothstep(0, .14)` and `smoothstep(.1, .5)`). Add inked toon clouds as flattened spheres that drift.\n458\t\n459\t**Model recipes ✔ (all primitives, all parented to named joints).** Build in code from `SphereGeometry` (scaled), `CapsuleGeometry`, `CylinderGeometry`, `ConeGeometry`, `TorusGeometry`, `LatheGeometry`, `TubeGeometry` and `RoundedBoxGeometry` (from `three/addons/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js`).\n460\t\n461\t- **Peg doll, 1.5 m.** Feet: white flattened spheres r .1 scaled (1, .55, 1.5), at x ±.13. Legs: skin capsules r .08, length .3. Hips at y .46. Shorts: capsule r .2 (scaled 1.05, 1, .85). Shirt: capsule r .21, length .24 (z scale .82). Head: sphere r .23, centre .82 above hips. Headband: torus r .228. Eyes: ink spheres r .024 at (±.08, .85, .205) from hips. Cheeks: pink flattened spheres. Arms: visible darker wooden ball joints r .075 at shoulders (±.26, .5) and r .06 at elbows, skin capsule segments, white mitten spheres r .07. Skin `#F2D3A8`, joints `#D9A86C`, shorts `#2B4A6F`. Vary with paint, hats (visor, beret, bucket hat, perm of spheres, wizard hat, tiny crown), and small height and width changes for guests.\n462\t- **Trudy.** Rounded box (1.1, .42, .26), radius .1, `#F5B700`, on two stubby brown pegs. Dark window (.56, .2, .05) on the face holding a lime `#B9F26B` capsule vial r .075, length .36, lying on its side. Bubble: white flattened sphere with an ink pupil; it slides in the vial. Two ink marks on the vial like a level's centre lines. Round lime end vials at x ±.42 as cheeks. Ink torus arc for a mouth. She hops with squash and stretch. This design is very readable: keep it.\n463\t- **Ms. Stillwater.** One long orange leg (cylinder r .025, height 1.05), the other tucked. Body sphere r .34 scaled (.8, .92, 1.35), `#A9C2D4`. Wings: flattened ellipsoids `#7E9CB4`. S-curve neck: `TubeGeometry` of a Catmull-Rom curve, r .062, `#D6E3EC`, rising to y 2.2. Head sphere r .115. Beak: yellow cone. Ink crest cone. Half-closed eyes as ink torus arcs. A small `#C24D6A` scarf at the base of the neck. For animation, build the neck from 5 joint segments.\n464\t- **Coach Bolster.** Capsule r .72, length .5, scaled (1.06, 1, .9), `#B9806A`. Red sweatband torus at the top. Two pale muzzle spheres, cream cone tusks, tiny ink eyes, flattened flippers `#8D5F4F`, a white lanyard torus and a yellow whistle.\n465\t- **Island.** Sand disc r 22 over a grass disc r 15 (flush with the sand, or dolls sink into it: all walking surfaces share y = 0), big inverted rock cone below on its one pivot rock. Toy trees: peg trunk plus ball top. Lighthouse: stacked red and cream rings with a lamp and a blue cap. Pavilions: toy-block posts and rounded slab roofs in teal and orange. Sun Deck, Bandstand, Circus Green, Yard, Pier, Jar, Mirror pavilion, Notice Board, Gazebo, Bed Hut at fixed spots around the rim and centre. Every set in section 6 appears here as a landmark, and the class itself runs in its own world (section 16).\n466\t- **Comic touches.** Inked world-space words: CLACK!, SPLOSH!, WOBBLE. Canvas-texture sprites that pop and fade. Everyone squashes on landing. A small hit-stop on catches and big events. Gentle camera shake, off with reduce-shake.\n467\t\n468\t---\n469\t\n470\t## 10. Animation\n471\t\n472\tUse Three's `AnimationMixer` and `AnimationClip` as the base (TASK.md requires the engine's system). Rig each character as a hierarchy of named `THREE.Group` joints with the rigid parts parented to them: `root, hips, spine, head, shoulderL/R, elbowL/R, hipL/R, kneeL/R, ankleL/R` for dolls. No skinned meshes are needed for jointed toys. `poseClip(name, duration, keyframes)` in `src/anim/clips.js` compiles `{ t, pose: { shoulderL: [x, y, z in degrees], ... } }` into `QuaternionKeyframeTrack`s on `'shoulderL.quaternion'`. `AnimationMixer` plays them with `crossFadeTo`.\n473\t\n474\t- **Doll clips:** idle, breathe, walk, jog, hop, land, cheer, clap, shrug, bow, flail, sleep, one per pose (8), squat (scrubbed: `action.paused = true`, `action.time = depth × duration`), plank, sit cross-legged, lean-back tug, hip-circle (scrubbed by hoop phase), step-L, step-R, carry-tray, hold-slab, tuck, pike, dive-ready.\n475\t- **Trudy:** hop, idle wobble, gasp, sulk, faint, read clipboard; plus procedural squash and bubble.\n476\t- **Heron:** stand, slow demo versions of each pose, neck bow, head turn, pat. **Walrus:** bounce, clap, huge inhale, salute, catch log. **Gull:** fly, land, peck, squawk.\n477\t- **Layering order each frame:** `mixer.update(dt)`, then apply procedural lean from the balance core at `root`, head bobble, breath on spine. Then, while ragdolled, parts follow bodies instead.\n478\t\n479\t**Ragdoll from the rig ✔ (10 bodies, stable).** When a doll falls, create one dynamic body per segment at the segment's world transform (body origin at the joint, collider offset along the segment). Reparent the segment's visual to the scene (`scene.attach`) and copy each body's transform to it each step. Use spherical joints with `setContactsEnabled(false)` and the ragdoll collision bits. Tested: capsules torso (hh .2, r .22, m .45), head (.02, .2, .15), upper arm (.1, .07, .05), lower arm (.1, .07, .04), upper leg (.13, .09, .08), lower leg (.13, .08, .06), linear damping .05, angular damping .6, friction .7, restitution .15. Max speed 7 m/s, no NaN, settles in about 5.5 s; end it at `recoverTime`. Start it with the core's velocity and a small impulse on the head. On recovery, `attach` the segments back and restore their local transforms.\n480\t\n481\tGhost lanes do not ragdoll: they play a flop clip.\n482\t\n483\t---\n484\t\n485\t## 11. Audio\n486\t\n487\tEverything is synthesised with Web Audio. No files. All instruments take a `ctx` argument, so the same code runs on an `AudioContext` and on an `OfflineAudioContext` for tests.\n488\t\n489\t**Mix.** `buses (music, sfx, voice) → master compressor → destination`. Settings 0 to 100. **Music defaults to 10** (gain 0.10), sound 80, voice 80. Start the context on the first gesture; before that the game runs silently without error. In a hub session, stop all sound on `end`.\n490\t\n491\t**Music engine.** Look-ahead scheduler (25 ms tick, 150 ms ahead) on `currentTime`. Reverb from a `ConvolverNode` with a generated noise-decay impulse. Tracks are data: `{ bpm, swing, chords per bar, parts }` in `src/audio/tracks.js`. Instruments: FM electric piano (sine carrier with a 14:1 tine and a 1:1 body, fast index decay), slap bass (saw through an enveloped low-pass plus a click), marimba (sine plus 4× partial, fast decay), vibes (sine plus 4× partial and tremolo), pad (three detuned saws, low-pass, slow attack), FM brass stab, breathy flute (sine plus band-passed noise), kick (sine sweep 120→45 Hz), gated snare (noise into short reverb), brushes and shaker (filtered noise), woodblock, rim.\n492\t\n493\t**Score.** Original smooth city-pop and bossa, nothing like the source's music.\n494\t\n495\t| Track | Where | Feel | Chords per bar |\n496\t|---|---|---|---|\n497\t| Level Best | Title, menus, ending | 92 BPM light swing; e-piano, vibes lead, soft bass, brushes | D♭maj9 B♭m9 E♭m9 A♭13, twice; G♭maj9 Fm7 E♭m9 A♭13sus |\n498\t| Island | Hub | 84 BPM bossa; marimba, upright-ish bass, shaker | Fmaj7 Am7 B♭maj7 C9, Fmaj7 Dm9 Gm9 C13 |\n499\t| Stillness | Poses, Cairn, Still Water | 60 BPM, free; pad, bells on the breath cycle, flute | Dmaj9 Emaj9/D, slowly, Lydian |\n500\t| Strength | Log Reps, Plank, Tug | 100 BPM funk; slap bass, clav-like e-piano, brass stabs, gated snare | Em9 A13 |\n501\t| Cardio | Step Class, Plates, Loop | Bpm from the chart; four-on-the-floor, octave slap bass, e-piano, brass, gated snare | D♭maj7 Cm7 B♭m7 E♭sus |\n502\t| Balance | Fruit Hat, Room Service, Springboard, Raft | 118 BPM bouncy; marimba, pizzicato, woodblock, tuba-like bass | C6 Am7 Dm7 G7 |\n503\t| Level | The Grand Hold | Starts as bare e-piano of the title theme, then every instrument, slower, in tune | As Level Best |\n504\t| Stings | Results, reveal, unlock | Three seconds or less, one per medal, drum roll and reveal, little fanfare | |\n505\t\n506\t**Title theme motif** (use as written; MIDI, on a 16-step bar, `[step, note, length]`):\n507\t```\n508\tBar 1 D♭maj9: [0,77,3] [3,75,1] [4,73,2] [6,68,4] [12,70,2] [14,72,2]\n509\tBar 2 B♭m9:   [0,73,3] [3,72,1] [4,70,2] [6,65,6] [14,68,2]\n510\tBar 3 E♭m9:   [0,70,3] [3,68,1] [4,66,2] [6,63,4] [12,65,2] [14,66,2]\n511\tBar 4 A♭13:   [0,68,3] [3,70,1] [4,72,2] [6,75,6]\n512\t```\n513\t**Out-of-tune island.** While the rest tilt is above zero, detune every music note by `tilt° × 1.6 × sin(t × 0.7)` cents. At level, it is exactly in tune. The player will feel this before they notice it.\n514\t\n515\t**Voices.** Animalese-style gibberish. Each line is spoken from its own text: strip it to letters, walk the letters at the character's rate, and for each syllable-group fire a short blip: a pulse or saw oscillator at the character's pitch with slight random drift, through two band-pass filters at formants picked by vowel (a, e, i, o, u, each with a set F1 and F2), 60 ms envelope; consonants add a very short noise tick. Pitch rises on `?`, jumps up on `!`, falls at the end of a sentence. Occasional set exclamations in nonsense Simlish (\"Wobba-la!\", \"Oof-ah.\", \"Nee nee!\"). The text types out in time.\n516\t\n517\t| Voice | Base pitch | Rate | Character |\n518\t|---|---|---|---|\n519\t| Trudy | 420 Hz | 16 syll/s | squeaky, nervous, high formants |\n520\t| Ms. Stillwater | 170 Hz | 7 syll/s | breathy, adds noise, long gaps |\n521\t| Coach Bolster | 95 Hz | 11 syll/s | big, saw, loud, bursts |\n522\t| Guests | 200-320 Hz | 12 syll/s | varied per guest |\n523\t| Gary | | | FM squawk |\n524\t\n525\t**SFX** (`src/audio/sfx.js`, all parameterised, with small random variation): wood clack (three resonant noise bursts, tuned per limb), toy spring (rising sine with vibrato), splash (noise with falling low-pass, big or small), marble drop and roll (short high pings, pitch random, more as the jar fills), plate hum (sine following speed), plate crash, fruit thud, urchin spike, whistle, bell, breath in and out (filtered noise), wind gust (rising band-passed noise), wooden creak (for the island: slow saw through a resonant filter), UI move, confirm and back (woodblock tones), stamp thud, drum roll, foot click.\n526\t\n527\t---\n528\t\n529\t## 12. Multiplayer\n530\t\n531\t**Two modes, both required.** On one screen by itself (Group Class, as the hub's own docs say couch play stays the game's own, up to four controllers), and across devices in an AI Hub session. Both use the lane system, so they cost little beyond it.\n532\t\n533\t### Group Class: on its own, 1-4 on one TV\n534\t\n535\tFrom the main menu or the Gazebo. **Lobby:** four mats. Each pad's Cross joins it, Enter or a click joins keyboard and mouse as one player, Circle or Esc leaves. Seats use the hub's colours. The first to join drives menus. **Playlist:** Quick Class (three random) or pick three. All events with `couch: true`: Poses, Cairn, Log Reps, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, Springboard, Tippy Raft, Tug of Peace, Room Service. **Points:** 4, 3, 2, 1 per event. **Final podium:** a toy-block podium, winners on top, and it tips over.\n536\t\n537\tAll lanes use one camera that frames everyone; each event's camera has a wide setting for this. Room Service uses four parallel boardwalks with the camera following the group, and the leader's finish starts a 15 s clock for the rest. Each lane reads its own pad by gamepad index, and prompts on that player's disc follow that device.\n538\t\n539\t### AI Hub session: ghost mode, 1-4 devices\n540\t\n541\tThe contract is `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md`. Read it. I ran a minimal WebGPU ghost game built this way through the real harness and it passed all 16 checks at 1, 2 and 4 players, on the bad network, and with the host leaving ✔.\n542\t\n543\t**Why ghost.** Every copy runs its own physics, and the others appear as dolls in the next lanes doing the same class: it looks exactly like a fitness class. It is robust to latency, because nobody's balance depends on the network.\n544\t\n545\t**Head of the page, in this order** (the hub's check reads `name` then `content`, so keep that attribute order):\n546\t```html\n547\t<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n548\t<script>…vendor/session.js, verbatim, nothing before it…</script>\n549\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n550\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n551\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n552\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n553\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Stay balanced through the class: highest score wins\">\n554\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Lean: stick, WASD or mouse. Feet: L2 R2, Q E or clicks. Flail: Square or F\">\n555\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n556\t<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n557\t<meta name=\"hub:source\" content=\"Wii Fit\">\n558\t```\n559\tDo not set `hub:webgpu required`: the WebGL2 fallback works ✔, so the hub can deal this game to everyone. Fetch the client from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` into `vendor/session.js`. **The hub checks it by SHA-256** from its banner to its last line, with LF line endings: 1.4.0 is `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` ✔. Make the build fail if it doesn't match. If the hub has released a newer client, take its hash from `scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs` in that repo.\n560\t\n561\t**The boot path** (first statements of `main.js`, before anything heavy). Wrap it in an async function: an `iife` build rejects top-level `await`.\n562\t```js\n563\tasync function main() {\n564\t  if (AIHub.active) {\n565\t    const pConnect = AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true });   // hello must go out within 10 s\n566\t    await bootEngine();                       // renderer.init(), RAPIER.init(), build the world\n567\t    const info = await pConnect;\n568\t    if (info) runSession(info); else runStandalone();     // null: no hub after all\n569\t  } else { await bootEngine(); runStandalone(); }\n570\t}\n571\tmain().catch(showBootFailure);                // shows the message, calls AIHub.fail(message)\n572\t```\n573\t\n574\t**In `runSession(info)`:**\n575\t1. No title, menus or attract. Build the class stage, line up the dolls, then `AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press and without waiting for every shader. Register `AIHub.on` handlers before `ready()`.\n576\t2. **Your own card** (`card: true`). The class lined up on the deck, Trudy with a clipboard, the music. Overlay: the game's name, each player's name in their colour with a tick when ready (from `card.ready`), how to win, this player's controls with glyphs, time left from `card.left` (show \"Starting...\" when `card.starting`, \"Paused\" when `card.paused`). Enter or Cross or a tap: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. Triangle or M (name the key on the card) or a tap: `AIHub.answer('more')`. The harness presses Enter by dispatching a keydown on the page, so listen on `window`. Nothing on the card changes the round, and it never touches `Math.random`.\n577\t3. **The round runs on `AIHub.time()` alone,** so everyone is in the same class at the same moment. Count down from 3 while the time is negative. Three slots picked with `AIHub.rng('deal')` from the session set (Poses, Cairn, Log Reps, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, Springboard), no repeats: each is 6 s intro, 50 s play (`sessionConfig` makes each event fit exactly), 6 s score. Total 186 s, within the 270 s limit that 3 minutes gives ✔. If time jumps (hidden tab), go to the slot the clock says.\n578\t4. **Ghosts.** From the moment `AIHub.time()` reaches 0, including during intros, about 10 times a second, `AIHub.send({ t:'g', a:slotIndex, x, z, lx, lz, c:clipIndex, f:fallen?1:0, s:score }, { reliable:false })`. Keep it well under 1,200 bytes. Show a ghost only when its `a` equals your slot. Interpolate over about 150 ms. Ghosts are visual only: name tag (set with `textContent`, never HTML), seat colour on the shirt, a flop clip for `f`. They never touch your physics. Remove on `leave`.\n579\t5. **Scoring.** Each slot gives `round(form × 10)`. Call `AIHub.progress({ score, label: 'Fruit Hat 420' })` every second **from the moment time reaches 0, with score 0 during the first intro.** The harness checks for ghost traffic and progress about 4 seconds into play, well before the first class starts, and fails the game if there is none. `AIHub.result({ score, stats, awards })` once at the end, for example stats `{ 'Floor Exercises': 2, 'Best medal': 'Pleasant', 'Wobble Age': 41 }`, and an award from a small list (\"Suspiciously Still\"). Then a calm \"waiting for the others\" with Trudy.\n580\t6. `time-up`: send `result` at once if not already sent (grace is 5 s ✔). `end`: stop input, sending and all sound, and keep the last frame; the hub draws its results over the game.\n581\t7. Options opens a small overlay with volumes only, and it doesn't pause. No restart, no quit, no Dev Mode.\n582\t\n583\t**Session rules to keep:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; no other network; no chat, emotes or custom names (so no wave button in a session); names only from the hub; all gameplay random numbers from `AIHub.rng(label)`.\n584\t\n585\t**On its own:** `AIHub.active` is false and the game behaves as if the hub didn't exist. Pressing Enter and Space repeatedly on the title must be harmless and leave something drawn: the hub's check does exactly this.\n586\t\n587\t---\n588\t\n589\t## 13. Attract mode\n590\t\n591\tAfter 10 s with no input on the Title or Main menu, start a loop of bot-played vignettes, about 18 s each, using the real events with bot lanes and god mode on: island walk with the island tipping, Fruit Hat (a greedy bot that topples), The Cairn, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Springboard (a belly flop with the slow-mo replay), Tippy Raft with four bots, Room Service, Poses. Show as much as possible, and let the bots cheat. Overlay: the title, small, and \"DEMO · Press any button\" with the current device's glyph. Loop forever, returning to the title for 5 s between passes.\n592\t\n593\t**Any input exits and is swallowed:** a key, a button, a click, a stick past 0.3, or more than 8 px of mouse movement. It returns to the Title and does not act on that press. Audio plays if the context is already running; otherwise it is silent with no error. Never starts in a session.\n594\t\n595\t---\n596\t\n597\t## 14. Dev mode\n598\t\n599\tFrom the Pause menu. It is one screen with big controls, usable by pad, keyboard and mouse.\n600\t\n601\t**Tools:** jump to any event and tier; jump to any day or step; skip to Results or to the Ending; god mode (no falls); time scale 0.1× to 4×; single-step; free camera; physics debug lines (`world.debugRender()` into a `LineSegments`); stats (fps, step ms, bodies, draw calls); input monitor (both sticks, triggers, last device); force a gust, a fall, a fruit or a stone; play any dialogue line, music track or sting; set Wobble Age; add marbles; unlock all; toggle ink and pick palette; let the bot play; reset save; copy tuning as JSON (to the clipboard, falling back to a large on-screen text so nothing needs a dialog).\n602\t\n603\t**Live sliders with Reset to Defaults** (`src/data/tuning.js`: each entry `{ group, label, min, max, step, default }`; the whole game reads tuning live):\n604\t- *Balance:* maxLean, margin, zeta, ankleMax, fall angle, reflex, flail boost, flail cooldown, sway scale, tremor scale, recover time.\n605\t- *World:* gravity, island weight (° at rim), island rest tilt, island spring, walk speed.\n606\t- *Events:* fruit mass, fruit interval, landing radius, stone mass, stone interval, ring size at end, gust force, plate decay, plate gain, rhythm window, rep tempo, board gain, air torque.\n607\t- *Rewards:* marbles per minute, medal scale.\n608\t- *Ink:* hull, line width, boil, grain.\n609\t\n610\t**For later designers.** TASK.md wants the rest of the game buildable with normal designer tools. So events' tiers, poses, charts, dialogue, days, unlocks, palettes and tuning are all plain data tables in `src/data/`. A designer can add a tier, a day or a line of dialogue without touching systems, and test it from Dev Mode. Keep it that way.\n611\t\n612\t---\n613\t\n614\t## 15. Touch-only devices\n615\t\n616\t**Detect with media queries, not `maxTouchPoints`:** `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`. Playwright's iPhone profile reports `maxTouchPoints` 0 ✔.\n617\t\n618\tShow the title with \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a Settings button that works by tap. There are no touch controls anywhere else. Attract mode runs after 10 s as usual, and a tap returns to this screen. If a pad connects or a key is pressed, drop this state and carry on normally. In a session the card's Ready and More time accept taps, per the hub's rules.\n619\t\n620\t---\n621\t\n622\t## 16. Architecture and build\n623\t\n624\t**Stack.** `three@0.180.0` (import only from `three/webgpu` and `three/tsl`; addons from `three/addons/...` work ✔), `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/fredoka` (SIL OFL; put its name and licence in the credits). Web Audio and the Gamepad API directly. `npm i` these in the dev folder ✔; Playwright finds the browsers already here.\n625\t\n626\t```\n627\tpackage.json\n628\tvendor/session.js\n629\ttools/build.mjs  tools/test.mjs  tools/shots.mjs  tools/hub/{harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}\n630\tsrc/main.js\n631\tsrc/core/      loop.js  rng.js  save.js  script.js  tuning.js\n632\tsrc/input/     input.js  glyphs.js  bot.js\n633\tsrc/render/    renderer.js  ink.js  palette.js  sky.js  sea.js  cameraRig.js  particles.js  comicText.js\n634\tsrc/art/       materials.js  doll.js  trudy.js  heron.js  walrus.js  gull.js  island.js  props.js\n635\tsrc/anim/      clips.js  rig.js\n636\tsrc/physics/   world.js  balancer.js  ragdoll.js  island.js\n637\tsrc/audio/     engine.js  instruments.js  tracks.js  sequencer.js  voice.js  sfx.js\n638\tsrc/ui/        ui.css  focus.js  hud.js  disc.js  speech.js  screens/*.js\n639\tsrc/game/      director.js  programme.js  checkin.js  jar.js  hub.js  profiles.js  results.js\n640\t               attract.js  devmode.js  groupclass.js  session.js  ghost.js  ending.js\n641\tsrc/events/    base.js  poses.js  cairn.js  stillWater.js  logReps.js  plank.js  tug.js  stepClass.js\n642\t               plates.js  islandLoop.js  fruitHat.js  roomService.js  springboard.js  raft.js  grandHold.js\n643\tsrc/data/      config.js  events.js  programme.js  poses.js  charts.js  dialogue.js  tuning.js\n644\t               palettes.js  unlocks.js  names.js  about.js\n645\ttest/*.mjs\n646\t```\n647\t\n648\t**The loop.** Fixed 1/120 s simulation. `frame(real)`: `acc += min(real, 0.1) × timeScale`; run `game.fixed(1/120)` up to 12 times; `game.frame(real)`; `post.render()`. Input polls inside the fixed tick. **All game logic and UI flow run on sim time, never `setTimeout`,** via a `game.after(sec, fn)` scheduler and generator scripts. This is what makes a whole-game test take a minute. Use generators for sequences: `function* dayOne(d) { yield d.say('trudy', 'intro.step_on'); yield d.wait(1); ... }`, ticked by `src/core/script.js`.\n649\t\n650\t**Random numbers.** `src/core/rng.js` is the only file that may touch randomness. `rng(label)` returns `AIHub.rng(label)` in a session and a mulberry32 stream outside one. Cosmetics use a private stream. Never call `Math.random` directly. In a session the client seeds it for fairness, and stray draws don't matter, but nothing should depend on it.\n651\t\n652\t**Renderer ✔.** `new THREE.WebGPURenderer({ antialias: true })`, `await renderer.init()`, then check `renderer.backend.isWebGPUBackend`. It falls back to WebGL2 by itself when there's no adapter ✔ (a harmless warning). Pixel ratio up to 2. Wrap boot in try/catch: if nothing starts, show the message and call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Performance is out of scope, per TASK.md: add detail freely. On this computer a simple scene ran at about 59 fps at 720p under software WebGPU ✔.\n653\t\n654\t**Rapier ✔.** `import RAPIER from '@dimforge/rapier3d-compat'; await RAPIER.init();`. It works inlined with no extra file. It logs one harmless warning about deprecated init parameters. Fixed step: `world.timestep = 1/120`. Free each world with `world.free()`.\n655\t\n656\t**Build ✔.** One self-contained HTML with no external requests.\n657\t\n658\t```js\n659\t// tools/build.mjs (core; OUT is the file name and location TASK.md gives you)\n660\timport { build } from 'esbuild'; import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'; import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\n661\tconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js', 'utf8').replace(/\\r\\n/g, '\\n');\n662\tconst END = \"})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis);\";\n663\tconst hash = createHash('sha256').update(session.slice(session.indexOf('/*! aihub-session '), session.indexOf(END) + END.length)).digest('hex');\n664\tif (hash !== '529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a') throw new Error('not the released hub client');\n665\tconst r = await build({ entryPoints: ['src/main.js'], bundle: true, format: 'iife', target: 'es2022', minify: true,\n666\t  write: false, legalComments: 'none', loader: { '.css': 'text' } });\n667\tconst js = r.outputFiles[0].text.replace(/<\\/script/gi, '<\\\\/script');\n668\tconst font = (w) => readFileSync(`node_modules/@fontsource/fredoka/files/fredoka-latin-${w}-normal.woff2`).toString('base64');\n669\t// Assemble with an array join, not String.replace: the bundle contains $ patterns that replace would corrupt.\n670\twriteFileSync(OUT, ['<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><script>', session, '</script>',\n671\t  METAS, `<title>${TITLE}</title><style>@font-face{…data:font/woff2;base64,${font(500)}…}…</style></head><body>`,\n672\t  '<script>', js, '</script></body></html>'].join(''));\n673\t```\n674\tThe spike's bundle of Three and Rapier came to 3.2 MB ✔. Each Fredoka weight adds about 16 KB. Expect about 4-6 MB at the end. `format: 'iife'` means no dynamic imports. It opens from `file://` ✔.\n675\t\n676\t**Fresh world per event.** Events get a new Rapier world and a new `THREE.Group`, added under one scene that also holds sky, sea and lights. The island is hidden during events, or kept far in the background as scenery where a set wants it. Transitions hide the swap with the ink wipe.\n677\t\n678\t---\n679\t\n680\t## 17. Testing\n681\t\n682\tFollow TASK.md's rules: headless scripts first, one command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only what changed, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to review or judge. Keep every file in your folder. Keep to one or two browsers. If you see \"Target closed\" or \"page crashed\", memory ran out: run fewer browsers.\n683\t\n684\t**The command.** `node tools/test.mjs [group ...]`. Groups: `unit`, `flow`, `events`, `balance`, `input`, `audio`, `hub`, `ui`, `full`. It prints each failure with its reason, then one line: `N passed, M failed`. Rerun only the group your change touches.\n685\t\n686\t**Browser flags ✔ (use these exact sets):**\n687\t```js\n688\tconst WEBGPU = ['--enable-unsafe-webgpu', '--enable-features=Vulkan', '--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader'];  // ~60 fps; screenshots are black\n689\tconst SHOTS  = [...WEBGPU, '--use-vulkan=swiftshader', '--use-angle=swiftshader'];                         // real screenshots at ~6 fps\n690\t```\n691\t- **To see the game, use `SHOTS`.** With only `WEBGPU` the canvas is black in `page.screenshot` and blank from `toDataURL`. With `SHOTS`, screenshots show the true image ✔. Use them only for art passes, because of the frame rate.\n692\t- No flags at all: Chromium finds no adapter and falls back to WebGL2 (slowly) ✔. `channel: 'chromium'` new headless and headed Chromium under xvfb both aborted here with the WebGPU flags: don't bother.\n693\t- WebKit: WebGL2 fallback, works, screenshots are real ✔. Use it to test the fallback. Firefox: no WebGL2 when headless (`AllowWebgl2:false`); it does have WebGL2 when headed under `xvfb-run` ✔. Not needed for routine tests.\n694\t- On browser close you may see \"WebGPU Device Lost\" in the console: ignore it once closing has started. Also ignore Rapier's deprecated-parameters warning.\n695\t\n696\t**Test hooks.** With `?test=1`, the game exposes `window.__TEST__`:\n697\t- `state()`: JSON with `screen, attract, day, step, profile, marbles, tilt, wobbleAge, event: { id, tier, phase, t, score, lanes: [{ kind, leanX, leanZ, cob, fallen, form, props }] }, input: { lastDevice, glyphSet }, audio: { state, music, sfx, voice }, render: { backend, frames }, errors`.\n698\t- `advance(seconds)`: runs the fixed tick and the frame logic without rendering or waiting. All tests use it, except the ones checking real time and real frames.\n699\t- `bot(skill | null)`: `sharp`, `average`, `clumsy` play through the real input path: they write to the stubbed gamepad, not into the game's state.\n700\t- `goto(screen, args)`, `tuning`, `settings`."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"773e0f13-c2f9-4f5a-b864-3052337ad265","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:09:29.421Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work/PLAN.md","content":"## 8. Cast and writing\n\n**Writing style.** Short sentences. Polite. Second person. Chirpy and calm, with something slightly off. Instructions are plain and exact, then a small, sincere, odd aside. Praise is mild and specific. Every failure gets a line that makes it fine. The joke is the straight face. Write at least 200 Trudy lines, 60 for each instructor, 60 for guests, in `src/data/dialogue.js`, by situation, picked without repeats.\n\n**Trudy, Head of Alignment.** A chunky spirit level on two stubby pegs. Her bubble is her eye: it slides to the high side of the world, and to wherever she is feeling. She has been crooked with the island since she was installed and thinks of this as normal. Fussy aunt energy. Keeps count of everything and insists she isn't. She gets attached to you and would die before saying so.\n\n- \"Oh! You came back. I wasn't counting. It's been one day, four hours and some minutes.\"\n- \"Please step on. Not on me. On the island. I'm a level, not a bath mat.\"\n- \"Lean left. Your other left. No, that was correct. I was testing you.\"\n- \"That was nearly level! I got a little emotional. My bubble went sideways.\"\n- \"Let's call that a floor exercise.\"\n- \"You fell very consistently. Consistency is important.\"\n- \"It's been three days. The island tilted a bit. I'm not saying those things are connected. They are connected.\"\n- \"Your Wobble Age is 61. That's down from 74! If this continues, you will eventually be born.\"\n- \"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\"\n- *Personal questions, always Yes/No, always a reply either way.* \"Do you trust your knees?\" Yes: \"They speak highly of you too.\" No: \"They know.\" · \"Do you ever stand on one leg without knowing why?\" Yes: \"I thought so.\" No: \"That's what someone on one leg would say.\" · \"Have you been leaning on things? Be honest. I can tell.\" · \"When you walk into a room, which way do you lean first?\" Left / Right: \"Interesting. I'm writing that down.\"\n\n**Ms. Stillwater, Stillness.** A tall heron on one leg. Half-closed eyes. A voice like a slow door. She has never blinked on camera. She notices everything and is not bothered by any of it.\n\n- \"Breathe in. Breathe out. Not like that, but thank you for trying.\"\n- \"This pose is called the Apologetic Pine. Feel your weight. Feel it apologise.\"\n- \"If you are shaking, that is only your body leaving.\"\n- \"A heron can stand like this for hours. A heron has also never been asked why.\"\n- \"You got off the mat. I saw. It's all right. I see everything. It's all right.\"\n\n**Coach Bolster, Strength and Cardio.** A vast walrus in a sweatband with a whistle he always forgets to blow. Loves you unconditionally and loudly. Bad at counting, on purpose.\n\n- \"YOU'RE DOING IT. I don't know what it is, but you're doing it.\"\n- \"That's the last one! ...THIS one's the last one. I've lied to you and I'd do it again.\"\n- \"I believe in you as much as I believe in lunch.\"\n- \"Your form is a form! That's what form is!\"\n\n**Guests,** all peg dolls in different paint and hats. They wander, watch your classes and react: Gentle Margaret, Retired Kevin, Both Sandras (two dolls, always together, always agreeing), Unsteady Paul, Young Gerald (92). **Gary** is a gull. He steals things and lands on you, turning up in several events. In the finale he sits on your head and Trudy says, \"He's part of it now.\" Nobody ever mentions that everyone is a wooden toy.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Art bible\n\nThe look is **sun-bleached Japanese resort postcard meets wooden toy box, drawn in ink.** I rendered this cast and these materials with the recipe below and they read clearly on a TV-sized frame ✔.\n\n**Palette ✔.** Ink `#1A1420` on everything. Seat colours are the hub's: `#E8443A`, `#3B82F6`, `#22C55E`, `#F5B700`.\n\n| Time | skyTop | skyMid | skyLow | sea | seaHi | sun, intensity | hemi sky, ground, intensity |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| dawn ✔ | 8FB4E0 | F6CFD8 | FFE3B5 | 3E8FB5 | A9E3E4 | FFE6D8, 2.6 | FCE8F0, 7C8BB5, 1.35 |\n| noon | 6FC3E8 | BFE9F0 | FFF2D0 | 16A8B0 | 8CF0DD | FFFBE8, 3.2 | FFFFFF, A09080, 1.35 |\n| golden ✔ | 7FB7C9 | F7D9A6 | F59E6C | 1F9EA3 | 71DCCB | FFF0D0, 3.1 | FFF4E0, 8A7FA0, 1.25 |\n| dusk ✔ | 2B2F6B | A8558F | F58A5C | 24406F | C26A8C | FFB98A, 2.4 | B48AD0, 2B2F5F, 1.1 |\n\nLerp palette uniforms and lights over 3 s between steps.\n\n**Materials ✔.** `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color, gradientMap })` with a 3-step `DataTexture` ramp `[96, 178, 255]`, `NearestFilter`, `NoColorSpace`. `renderer.toneMapping = THREE.NoToneMapping` keeps the palette true. Sky and sea use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial` with a TSL `colorNode`. Anything that should get a hull outline must be a toon material.\n\n**Shadows ✔.** `renderer.shadowMap.enabled = true`, `PCFShadowMap`, one sun with a 2048 map, `shadow.bias = -0.0006`, `shadow.normalBias = 0.035`. Without those two, the toon surfaces are covered in acne.\n\n**The ink recipe ✔** (runs on WebGPU and on the WebGL2 fallback, tested in Chromium and WebKit):\n\n```js\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { mrt, output, normalView, toonOutlinePass, color, float, vec2, vec4, uv, screenSize, Fn, abs, max,\n  smoothstep, mix, uniform, perspectiveDepthToViewZ, time, length, hash, floor, oneMinus } from 'three/tsl';\n\nexport function makeInk(renderer, scene, camera) {\n  const INK = 0x1a1420;\n  const u = { hull: uniform(0.0028), width: uniform(1.4), boil: uniform(0.5), fadeNear: uniform(30), fadeFar: uniform(90),\n              grain: uniform(0.02), near: uniform(camera.near), far: uniform(camera.far), ink: uniform(new THREE.Color(INK)) };\n  const scenePass = toonOutlinePass(scene, camera, color(INK), u.hull, float(1));\n  scenePass.setMRT(mrt({ output, normal: normalView }));\n  const colorTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('output'), normalTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('normal'),\n        depthTex = scenePass.getTextureNode('depth');\n  const post = new THREE.PostProcessing(renderer);\n  post.outputNode = Fn(() => {\n    const cell = floor(uv().mul(vec2(40, 23)));\n    const wob = hash(floor(time.mul(8)).add(cell.x).add(cell.y.mul(17))).sub(0.5).mul(u.boil);   // hand-drawn line boil\n    const px = u.width.add(wob).div(screenSize);\n    const viewZ = (o) => perspectiveDepthToViewZ(depthTex.sample(uv().add(o)).r, u.near, u.far);\n    const n = (o) => normalTex.sample(uv().add(o)).xyz;\n    const dx = vec2(px.x, 0), dy = vec2(0, px.y);\n    const z0 = viewZ(vec2(0, 0)).abs();\n    const dz = max(abs(viewZ(dx).sub(viewZ(dx.negate()))), abs(viewZ(dy).sub(viewZ(dy.negate())))).div(z0.max(0.5));\n    const dn = max(length(n(dx).sub(n(dx.negate()))), length(n(dy).sub(n(dy.negate()))));\n    const e = max(smoothstep(0.05, 0.12, dz), smoothstep(0.7, 1.1, dn)).mul(oneMinus(smoothstep(u.fadeNear, u.fadeFar, z0)));\n    const c = mix(colorTex.rgb, u.ink, e);\n    const p = floor(uv().mul(screenSize));\n    const g = hash(p.x.add(p.y.mul(1571))).sub(0.5).mul(u.grain);\n    return vec4(c.add(g).mul(oneMinus(length(uv().sub(0.5)).mul(0.62).pow(2.2))), 1);\n  })();\n  return { post, u };\n}\n// Render with post.render(). Remember: camera far changes need u.far updated.\n```\nGotchas ✔:\n- TSL `hash()` takes **one float**. Give it a vec2 and you get vertical streaks over the whole image.\n- `fadeNear/fadeFar` removes depth-line noise at the horizon, which otherwise draws a thick black band. Make sure the sea disc is big (radius 590) and blends to `skyLow` with distance.\n- Do not merge vertices on hard-edged props: smooth normals smear the toon shading. Plain `BoxGeometry` looks right, and the depth lines cover the hull's corners.\n- `InstancedMesh` with `setColorAt` works with toon and the hull ✔. Use it for marbles, splash blobs, fruit crowds.\n- Glass ✔: toon material, `transparent: true, opacity: 0.16, side: DoubleSide, depthWrite: false`, colour `#E8FBFF`, with a hull outline. Good jar.\n- No TSL compute anywhere. It breaks the WebGL2 fallback. All particles are CPU-driven instances.\n\n**Sea ✔.** `length(positionWorld.xz)` into a `sin` with a small domain warp and `time`, then `step(0.86, wave)` between `sea` and `seaHi` for hard bands, mixed to `skyLow` by distance from the camera (60 to 260 units). Add a foam ring near the island by distance from its centre, plus rings that follow the tilt.\n\n**Sky ✔.** Inverted sphere radius 600, `mix` of the three colours on the view direction's y (`smoothstep(0, .14)` and `smoothstep(.1, .5)`). Add inked toon clouds as flattened spheres that drift.\n\n**Model recipes ✔ (all primitives, all parented to named joints).** Build in code from `SphereGeometry` (scaled), `CapsuleGeometry`, `CylinderGeometry`, `ConeGeometry`, `TorusGeometry`, `LatheGeometry`, `TubeGeometry` and `RoundedBoxGeometry` (from `three/addons/geometries/RoundedBoxGeometry.js`).\n\n- **Peg doll, 1.5 m.** Feet: white flattened spheres r .1 scaled (1, .55, 1.5), at x ±.13. Legs: skin capsules r .08, length .3. Hips at y .46. Shorts: capsule r .2 (scaled 1.05, 1, .85). Shirt: capsule r .21, length .24 (z scale .82). Head: sphere r .23, centre .82 above hips. Headband: torus r .228. Eyes: ink spheres r .024 at (±.08, .85, .205) from hips. Cheeks: pink flattened spheres. Arms: visible darker wooden ball joints r .075 at shoulders (±.26, .5) and r .06 at elbows, skin capsule segments, white mitten spheres r .07. Skin `#F2D3A8`, joints `#D9A86C`, shorts `#2B4A6F`. Vary with paint, hats (visor, beret, bucket hat, perm of spheres, wizard hat, tiny crown), and small height and width changes for guests.\n- **Trudy.** Rounded box (1.1, .42, .26), radius .1, `#F5B700`, on two stubby brown pegs. Dark window (.56, .2, .05) on the face holding a lime `#B9F26B` capsule vial r .075, length .36, lying on its side. Bubble: white flattened sphere with an ink pupil; it slides in the vial. Two ink marks on the vial like a level's centre lines. Round lime end vials at x ±.42 as cheeks. Ink torus arc for a mouth. She hops with squash and stretch. This design is very readable: keep it.\n- **Ms. Stillwater.** One long orange leg (cylinder r .025, height 1.05), the other tucked. Body sphere r .34 scaled (.8, .92, 1.35), `#A9C2D4`. Wings: flattened ellipsoids `#7E9CB4`. S-curve neck: `TubeGeometry` of a Catmull-Rom curve, r .062, `#D6E3EC`, rising to y 2.2. Head sphere r .115. Beak: yellow cone. Ink crest cone. Half-closed eyes as ink torus arcs. A small `#C24D6A` scarf at the base of the neck. For animation, build the neck from 5 joint segments.\n- **Coach Bolster.** Capsule r .72, length .5, scaled (1.06, 1, .9), `#B9806A`. Red sweatband torus at the top. Two pale muzzle spheres, cream cone tusks, tiny ink eyes, flattened flippers `#8D5F4F`, a white lanyard torus and a yellow whistle.\n- **Island.** Sand disc r 22 over a grass disc r 15 (flush with the sand, or dolls sink into it: all walking surfaces share y = 0), big inverted rock cone below on its one pivot rock. Toy trees: peg trunk plus ball top. Lighthouse: stacked red and cream rings with a lamp and a blue cap. Pavilions: toy-block posts and rounded slab roofs in teal and orange. Sun Deck, Bandstand, Circus Green, Yard, Pier, Jar, Mirror pavilion, Notice Board, Gazebo, Bed Hut at fixed spots around the rim and centre. Every set in section 6 appears here as a landmark, and the class itself runs in its own world (section 16).\n- **Comic touches.** Inked world-space words: CLACK!, SPLOSH!, WOBBLE. Canvas-texture sprites that pop and fade. Everyone squashes on landing. A small hit-stop on catches and big events. Gentle camera shake, off with reduce-shake.\n\n---\n\n## 10. Animation\n\nUse Three's `AnimationMixer` and `AnimationClip` as the base (TASK.md requires the engine's system). Rig each character as a hierarchy of named `THREE.Group` joints with the rigid parts parented to them: `root, hips, spine, head, shoulderL/R, elbowL/R, hipL/R, kneeL/R, ankleL/R` for dolls. No skinned meshes are needed for jointed toys. `poseClip(name, duration, keyframes)` in `src/anim/clips.js` compiles `{ t, pose: { shoulderL: [x, y, z in degrees], ... } }` into `QuaternionKeyframeTrack`s on `'shoulderL.quaternion'`. `AnimationMixer` plays them with `crossFadeTo`.\n\n- **Doll clips:** idle, breathe, walk, jog, hop, land, cheer, clap, shrug, bow, flail, sleep, one per pose (8), squat (scrubbed: `action.paused = true`, `action.time = depth × duration`), plank, sit cross-legged, lean-back tug, hip-circle (scrubbed by hoop phase), step-L, step-R, carry-tray, hold-slab, tuck, pike, dive-ready.\n- **Trudy:** hop, idle wobble, gasp, sulk, faint, read clipboard; plus procedural squash and bubble.\n- **Heron:** stand, slow demo versions of each pose, neck bow, head turn, pat. **Walrus:** bounce, clap, huge inhale, salute, catch log. **Gull:** fly, land, peck, squawk.\n- **Layering order each frame:** `mixer.update(dt)`, then apply procedural lean from the balance core at `root`, head bobble, breath on spine. Then, while ragdolled, parts follow bodies instead.\n\n**Ragdoll from the rig ✔ (10 bodies, stable).** When a doll falls, create one dynamic body per segment at the segment's world transform (body origin at the joint, collider offset along the segment). Reparent the segment's visual to the scene (`scene.attach`) and copy each body's transform to it each step. Use spherical joints with `setContactsEnabled(false)` and the ragdoll collision bits. Tested: capsules torso (hh .2, r .22, m .45), head (.02, .2, .15), upper arm (.1, .07, .05), lower arm (.1, .07, .04), upper leg (.13, .09, .08), lower leg (.13, .08, .06), linear damping .05, angular damping .6, friction .7, restitution .15. Max speed 7 m/s, no NaN, settles in about 5.5 s; end it at `recoverTime`. Start it with the core's velocity and a small impulse on the head. On recovery, `attach` the segments back and restore their local transforms.\n\nGhost lanes do not ragdoll: they play a flop clip.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Audio\n\nEverything is synthesised with Web Audio. No files. All instruments take a `ctx` argument, so the same code runs on an `AudioContext` and on an `OfflineAudioContext` for tests.\n\n**Mix.** `buses (music, sfx, voice) → master compressor → destination`. Settings 0 to 100. **Music defaults to 10** (gain 0.10), sound 80, voice 80. Start the context on the first gesture; before that the game runs silently without error. In a hub session, stop all sound on `end`.\n\n**Music engine.** Look-ahead scheduler (25 ms tick, 150 ms ahead) on `currentTime`. Reverb from a `ConvolverNode` with a generated noise-decay impulse. Tracks are data: `{ bpm, swing, chords per bar, parts }` in `src/audio/tracks.js`. Instruments: FM electric piano (sine carrier with a 14:1 tine and a 1:1 body, fast index decay), slap bass (saw through an enveloped low-pass plus a click), marimba (sine plus 4× partial, fast decay), vibes (sine plus 4× partial and tremolo), pad (three detuned saws, low-pass, slow attack), FM brass stab, breathy flute (sine plus band-passed noise), kick (sine sweep 120→45 Hz), gated snare (noise into short reverb), brushes and shaker (filtered noise), woodblock, rim.\n\n**Score.** Original smooth city-pop and bossa, nothing like the source's music.\n\n| Track | Where | Feel | Chords per bar |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Level Best | Title, menus, ending | 92 BPM light swing; e-piano, vibes lead, soft bass, brushes | D♭maj9 B♭m9 E♭m9 A♭13, twice; G♭maj9 Fm7 E♭m9 A♭13sus |\n| Island | Hub | 84 BPM bossa; marimba, upright-ish bass, shaker | Fmaj7 Am7 B♭maj7 C9, Fmaj7 Dm9 Gm9 C13 |\n| Stillness | Poses, Cairn, Still Water | 60 BPM, free; pad, bells on the breath cycle, flute | Dmaj9 Emaj9/D, slowly, Lydian |\n| Strength | Log Reps, Plank, Tug | 100 BPM funk; slap bass, clav-like e-piano, brass stabs, gated snare | Em9 A13 |\n| Cardio | Step Class, Plates, Loop | Bpm from the chart; four-on-the-floor, octave slap bass, e-piano, brass, gated snare | D♭maj7 Cm7 B♭m7 E♭sus |\n| Balance | Fruit Hat, Room Service, Springboard, Raft | 118 BPM bouncy; marimba, pizzicato, woodblock, tuba-like bass | C6 Am7 Dm7 G7 |\n| Level | The Grand Hold | Starts as bare e-piano of the title theme, then every instrument, slower, in tune | As Level Best |\n| Stings | Results, reveal, unlock | Three seconds or less, one per medal, drum roll and reveal, little fanfare | |\n\n**Title theme motif** (use as written; MIDI, on a 16-step bar, `[step, note, length]`):\n```\nBar 1 D♭maj9: [0,77,3] [3,75,1] [4,73,2] [6,68,4] [12,70,2] [14,72,2]\nBar 2 B♭m9:   [0,73,3] [3,72,1] [4,70,2] [6,65,6] [14,68,2]\nBar 3 E♭m9:   [0,70,3] [3,68,1] [4,66,2] [6,63,4] [12,65,2] [14,66,2]\nBar 4 A♭13:   [0,68,3] [3,70,1] [4,72,2] [6,75,6]\n```\n**Out-of-tune island.** While the rest tilt is above zero, detune every music note by `tilt° × 1.6 × sin(t × 0.7)` cents. At level, it is exactly in tune. The player will feel this before they notice it.\n\n**Voices.** Animalese-style gibberish. Each line is spoken from its own text: strip it to letters, walk the letters at the character's rate, and for each syllable-group fire a short blip: a pulse or saw oscillator at the character's pitch with slight random drift, through two band-pass filters at formants picked by vowel (a, e, i, o, u, each with a set F1 and F2), 60 ms envelope; consonants add a very short noise tick. Pitch rises on `?`, jumps up on `!`, falls at the end of a sentence. Occasional set exclamations in nonsense Simlish (\"Wobba-la!\", \"Oof-ah.\", \"Nee nee!\"). The text types out in time.\n\n| Voice | Base pitch | Rate | Character |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Trudy | 420 Hz | 16 syll/s | squeaky, nervous, high formants |\n| Ms. Stillwater | 170 Hz | 7 syll/s | breathy, adds noise, long gaps |\n| Coach Bolster | 95 Hz | 11 syll/s | big, saw, loud, bursts |\n| Guests | 200-320 Hz | 12 syll/s | varied per guest |\n| Gary | | | FM squawk |\n\n**SFX** (`src/audio/sfx.js`, all parameterised, with small random variation): wood clack (three resonant noise bursts, tuned per limb), toy spring (rising sine with vibrato), splash (noise with falling low-pass, big or small), marble drop and roll (short high pings, pitch random, more as the jar fills), plate hum (sine following speed), plate crash, fruit thud, urchin spike, whistle, bell, breath in and out (filtered noise), wind gust (rising band-passed noise), wooden creak (for the island: slow saw through a resonant filter), UI move, confirm and back (woodblock tones), stamp thud, drum roll, foot click.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Multiplayer\n\n**Two modes, both required.** On one screen by itself (Group Class, as the hub's own docs say couch play stays the game's own, up to four controllers), and across devices in an AI Hub session. Both use the lane system, so they cost little beyond it.\n\n### Group Class: on its own, 1-4 on one TV\n\nFrom the main menu or the Gazebo. **Lobby:** four mats. Each pad's Cross joins it, Enter or a click joins keyboard and mouse as one player, Circle or Esc leaves. Seats use the hub's colours. The first to join drives menus. **Playlist:** Quick Class (three random) or pick three. All events with `couch: true`: Poses, Cairn, Log Reps, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, Springboard, Tippy Raft, Tug of Peace, Room Service. **Points:** 4, 3, 2, 1 per event. **Final podium:** a toy-block podium, winners on top, and it tips over.\n\nAll lanes use one camera that frames everyone; each event's camera has a wide setting for this. Room Service uses four parallel boardwalks with the camera following the group, and the leader's finish starts a 15 s clock for the rest. Each lane reads its own pad by gamepad index, and prompts on that player's disc follow that device.\n\n### AI Hub session: ghost mode, 1-4 devices\n\nThe contract is `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md`. Read it. I ran a minimal WebGPU ghost game built this way through the real harness and it passed all 16 checks at 1, 2 and 4 players, on the bad network, and with the host leaving ✔.\n\n**Why ghost.** Every copy runs its own physics, and the others appear as dolls in the next lanes doing the same class: it looks exactly like a fitness class. It is robust to latency, because nobody's balance depends on the network.\n\n**Head of the page, in this order** (the hub's check reads `name` then `content`, so keep that attribute order):\n```html\n<meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n<script>…vendor/session.js, verbatim, nothing before it…</script>\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"ghost\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"3\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Stay balanced through the class: highest score wins\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Lean: stick, WASD or mouse. Feet: L2 R2, Q E or clicks. Flail: Square or F\">\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:source\" content=\"Wii Fit\">\n```\nDo not set `hub:webgpu required`: the WebGL2 fallback works ✔, so the hub can deal this game to everyone. Fetch the client from `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/session.js` into `vendor/session.js`. **The hub checks it by SHA-256** from its banner to its last line, with LF line endings: 1.4.0 is `529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a` ✔. Make the build fail if it doesn't match. If the hub has released a newer client, take its hash from `scripts/lib/hub-client.mjs` in that repo.\n\n**The boot path** (first statements of `main.js`, before anything heavy). Wrap it in an async function: an `iife` build rejects top-level `await`.\n```js\nasync function main() {\n  if (AIHub.active) {\n    const pConnect = AIHub.connect({ mode: 'ghost', seats: [1, 4], card: true });   // hello must go out within 10 s\n    await bootEngine();                       // renderer.init(), RAPIER.init(), build the world\n    const info = await pConnect;\n    if (info) runSession(info); else runStandalone();     // null: no hub after all\n  } else { await bootEngine(); runStandalone(); }\n}\nmain().catch(showBootFailure);                // shows the message, calls AIHub.fail(message)\n```\n\n**In `runSession(info)`:**\n1. No title, menus or attract. Build the class stage, line up the dolls, then `AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press and without waiting for every shader. Register `AIHub.on` handlers before `ready()`.\n2. **Your own card** (`card: true`). The class lined up on the deck, Trudy with a clipboard, the music. Overlay: the game's name, each player's name in their colour with a tick when ready (from `card.ready`), how to win, this player's controls with glyphs, time left from `card.left` (show \"Starting...\" when `card.starting`, \"Paused\" when `card.paused`). Enter or Cross or a tap: `AIHub.answer('ready')`. Triangle or M (name the key on the card) or a tap: `AIHub.answer('more')`. The harness presses Enter by dispatching a keydown on the page, so listen on `window`. Nothing on the card changes the round, and it never touches `Math.random`.\n3. **The round runs on `AIHub.time()` alone,** so everyone is in the same class at the same moment. Count down from 3 while the time is negative. Three slots picked with `AIHub.rng('deal')` from the session set (Poses, Cairn, Log Reps, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Fruit Hat, Springboard), no repeats: each is 6 s intro, 50 s play (`sessionConfig` makes each event fit exactly), 6 s score. Total 186 s, within the 270 s limit that 3 minutes gives ✔. If time jumps (hidden tab), go to the slot the clock says.\n4. **Ghosts.** From the moment `AIHub.time()` reaches 0, including during intros, about 10 times a second, `AIHub.send({ t:'g', a:slotIndex, x, z, lx, lz, c:clipIndex, f:fallen?1:0, s:score }, { reliable:false })`. Keep it well under 1,200 bytes. Show a ghost only when its `a` equals your slot. Interpolate over about 150 ms. Ghosts are visual only: name tag (set with `textContent`, never HTML), seat colour on the shirt, a flop clip for `f`. They never touch your physics. Remove on `leave`.\n5. **Scoring.** Each slot gives `round(form × 10)`. Call `AIHub.progress({ score, label: 'Fruit Hat 420' })` every second **from the moment time reaches 0, with score 0 during the first intro.** The harness checks for ghost traffic and progress about 4 seconds into play, well before the first class starts, and fails the game if there is none. `AIHub.result({ score, stats, awards })` once at the end, for example stats `{ 'Floor Exercises': 2, 'Best medal': 'Pleasant', 'Wobble Age': 41 }`, and an award from a small list (\"Suspiciously Still\"). Then a calm \"waiting for the others\" with Trudy.\n6. `time-up`: send `result` at once if not already sent (grace is 5 s ✔). `end`: stop input, sending and all sound, and keep the last frame; the hub draws its results over the game.\n7. Options opens a small overlay with volumes only, and it doesn't pause. No restart, no quit, no Dev Mode.\n\n**Session rules to keep:** no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; no other network; no chat, emotes or custom names (so no wave button in a session); names only from the hub; all gameplay random numbers from `AIHub.rng(label)`.\n\n**On its own:** `AIHub.active` is false and the game behaves as if the hub didn't exist. Pressing Enter and Space repeatedly on the title must be harmless and leave something drawn: the hub's check does exactly this.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Attract mode\n\nAfter 10 s with no input on the Title or Main menu, start a loop of bot-played vignettes, about 18 s each, using the real events with bot lanes and god mode on: island walk with the island tipping, Fruit Hat (a greedy bot that topples), The Cairn, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Springboard (a belly flop with the slow-mo replay), Tippy Raft with four bots, Room Service, Poses. Show as much as possible, and let the bots cheat. Overlay: the title, small, and \"DEMO · Press any button\" with the current device's glyph. Loop forever, returning to the title for 5 s between passes.\n\n**Any input exits and is swallowed:** a key, a button, a click, a stick past 0.3, or more than 8 px of mouse movement. It returns to the Title and does not act on that press. Audio plays if the context is already running; otherwise it is silent with no error. Never starts in a session.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Dev mode\n\nFrom the Pause menu. It is one screen with big controls, usable by pad, keyboard and mouse.\n\n**Tools:** jump to any event and tier; jump to any day or step; skip to Results or to the Ending; god mode (no falls); time scale 0.1× to 4×; single-step; free camera; physics debug lines (`world.debugRender()` into a `LineSegments`); stats (fps, step ms, bodies, draw calls); input monitor (both sticks, triggers, last device); force a gust, a fall, a fruit or a stone; play any dialogue line, music track or sting; set Wobble Age; add marbles; unlock all; toggle ink and pick palette; let the bot play; reset save; copy tuning as JSON (to the clipboard, falling back to a large on-screen text so nothing needs a dialog).\n\n**Live sliders with Reset to Defaults** (`src/data/tuning.js`: each entry `{ group, label, min, max, step, default }`; the whole game reads tuning live):\n- *Balance:* maxLean, margin, zeta, ankleMax, fall angle, reflex, flail boost, flail cooldown, sway scale, tremor scale, recover time.\n- *World:* gravity, island weight (° at rim), island rest tilt, island spring, walk speed.\n- *Events:* fruit mass, fruit interval, landing radius, stone mass, stone interval, ring size at end, gust force, plate decay, plate gain, rhythm window, rep tempo, board gain, air torque.\n- *Rewards:* marbles per minute, medal scale.\n- *Ink:* hull, line width, boil, grain.\n\n**For later designers.** TASK.md wants the rest of the game buildable with normal designer tools. So events' tiers, poses, charts, dialogue, days, unlocks, palettes and tuning are all plain data tables in `src/data/`. A designer can add a tier, a day or a line of dialogue without touching systems, and test it from Dev Mode. Keep it that way.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Touch-only devices\n\n**Detect with media queries, not `maxTouchPoints`:** `matchMedia('(pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches`. Playwright's iPhone profile reports `maxTouchPoints` 0 ✔.\n\nShow the title with \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a Settings button that works by tap. There are no touch controls anywhere else. Attract mode runs after 10 s as usual, and a tap returns to this screen. If a pad connects or a key is pressed, drop this state and carry on normally. In a session the card's Ready and More time accept taps, per the hub's rules.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Architecture and build\n\n**Stack.** `three@0.180.0` (import only from `three/webgpu` and `three/tsl`; addons from `three/addons/...` work ✔), `@dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3`, `esbuild`, `playwright@1.63.0`, `@fontsource/fredoka` (SIL OFL; put its name and licence in the credits). Web Audio and the Gamepad API directly. `npm i` these in the dev folder ✔; Playwright finds the browsers already here.\n\n```\npackage.json\nvendor/session.js\ntools/build.mjs  tools/test.mjs  tools/shots.mjs  tools/hub/{harness.html,bridge.js,round.js}\nsrc/main.js\nsrc/core/      loop.js  rng.js  save.js  script.js  tuning.js\nsrc/input/     input.js  glyphs.js  bot.js\nsrc/render/    renderer.js  ink.js  palette.js  sky.js  sea.js  cameraRig.js  particles.js  comicText.js\nsrc/art/       materials.js  doll.js  trudy.js  heron.js  walrus.js  gull.js  island.js  props.js\nsrc/anim/      clips.js  rig.js\nsrc/physics/   world.js  balancer.js  ragdoll.js  island.js\nsrc/audio/     engine.js  instruments.js  tracks.js  sequencer.js  voice.js  sfx.js\nsrc/ui/        ui.css  focus.js  hud.js  disc.js  speech.js  screens/*.js\nsrc/game/      director.js  programme.js  checkin.js  jar.js  hub.js  profiles.js  results.js\n               attract.js  devmode.js  groupclass.js  session.js  ghost.js  ending.js\nsrc/events/    base.js  poses.js  cairn.js  stillWater.js  logReps.js  plank.js  tug.js  stepClass.js\n               plates.js  islandLoop.js  fruitHat.js  roomService.js  springboard.js  raft.js  grandHold.js\nsrc/data/      config.js  events.js  programme.js  poses.js  charts.js  dialogue.js  tuning.js\n               palettes.js  unlocks.js  names.js  about.js\ntest/*.mjs\n```\n\n**The loop.** Fixed 1/120 s simulation. `frame(real)`: `acc += min(real, 0.1) × timeScale`; run `game.fixed(1/120)` up to 12 times; `game.frame(real)`; `post.render()`. Input polls inside the fixed tick. **All game logic and UI flow run on sim time, never `setTimeout`,** via a `game.after(sec, fn)` scheduler and generator scripts. This is what makes a whole-game test take a minute. Use generators for sequences: `function* dayOne(d) { yield d.say('trudy', 'intro.step_on'); yield d.wait(1); ... }`, ticked by `src/core/script.js`.\n\n**Random numbers.** `src/core/rng.js` is the only file that may touch randomness. `rng(label)` returns `AIHub.rng(label)` in a session and a mulberry32 stream outside one. Cosmetics use a private stream. Never call `Math.random` directly. In a session the client seeds it for fairness, and stray draws don't matter, but nothing should depend on it.\n\n**Renderer ✔.** `new THREE.WebGPURenderer({ antialias: true })`, `await renderer.init()`, then check `renderer.backend.isWebGPUBackend`. It falls back to WebGL2 by itself when there's no adapter ✔ (a harmless warning). Pixel ratio up to 2. Wrap boot in try/catch: if nothing starts, show the message and call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Performance is out of scope, per TASK.md: add detail freely. On this computer a simple scene ran at about 59 fps at 720p under software WebGPU ✔.\n\n**Rapier ✔.** `import RAPIER from '@dimforge/rapier3d-compat'; await RAPIER.init();`. It works inlined with no extra file. It logs one harmless warning about deprecated init parameters. Fixed step: `world.timestep = 1/120`. Free each world with `world.free()`.\n\n**Build ✔.** One self-contained HTML with no external requests.\n\n```js\n// tools/build.mjs (core; OUT is the file name and location TASK.md gives you)\nimport { build } from 'esbuild'; import { readFileSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs'; import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\nconst session = readFileSync('vendor/session.js', 'utf8').replace(/\\r\\n/g, '\\n');\nconst END = \"})(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis);\";\nconst hash = createHash('sha256').update(session.slice(session.indexOf('/*! aihub-session '), session.indexOf(END) + END.length)).digest('hex');\nif (hash !== '529eac78c54cfff0e60d375a9c63f66d1a594327ddcc262a2bdf9b1f2fb6582a') throw new Error('not the released hub client');\nconst r = await build({ entryPoints: ['src/main.js'], bundle: true, format: 'iife', target: 'es2022', minify: true,\n  write: false, legalComments: 'none', loader: { '.css': 'text' } });\nconst js = r.outputFiles[0].text.replace(/<\\/script/gi, '<\\\\/script');\nconst font = (w) => readFileSync(`node_modules/@fontsource/fredoka/files/fredoka-latin-${w}-normal.woff2`).toString('base64');\n// Assemble with an array join, not String.replace: the bundle contains $ patterns that replace would corrupt.\nwriteFileSync(OUT, ['<!doctype html><html lang=\"en\"><head><meta charset=\"utf-8\"><script>', session, '</script>',\n  METAS, `<title>${TITLE}</title><style>@font-face{…data:font/woff2;base64,${font(500)}…}…</style></head><body>`,\n  '<script>', js, '</script></body></html>'].join(''));\n```\nThe spike's bundle of Three and Rapier came to 3.2 MB ✔. Each Fredoka weight adds about 16 KB. Expect about 4-6 MB at the end. `format: 'iife'` means no dynamic imports. It opens from `file://` ✔.\n\n**Fresh world per event.** Events get a new Rapier world and a new `THREE.Group`, added under one scene that also holds sky, sea and lights. The island is hidden during events, or kept far in the background as scenery where a set wants it. Transitions hide the swap with the ink wipe.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Testing\n\nFollow TASK.md's rules: headless scripts first, one command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only what changed, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to review or judge. Keep every file in your folder. Keep to one or two browsers. If you see \"Target closed\" or \"page crashed\", memory ran out: run fewer browsers.\n\n**The command.** `node tools/test.mjs [group ...]`. Groups: `unit`, `flow`, `events`, `balance`, `input`, `audio`, `hub`, `ui`, `full`. It prints each failure with its reason, then one line: `N passed, M failed`. Rerun only the group your change touches.\n\n**Browser flags ✔ (use these exact sets):**\n```js\nconst WEBGPU = ['--enable-unsafe-webgpu', '--enable-features=Vulkan', '--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader'];  // ~60 fps; screenshots are black\nconst SHOTS  = [...WEBGPU, '--use-vulkan=swiftshader', '--use-angle=swiftshader'];                         // real screenshots at ~6 fps\n```\n- **To see the game, use `SHOTS`.** With only `WEBGPU` the canvas is black in `page.screenshot` and blank from `toDataURL`. With `SHOTS`, screenshots show the true image ✔. Use them only for art passes, because of the frame rate.\n- No flags at all: Chromium finds no adapter and falls back to WebGL2 (slowly) ✔. `channel: 'chromium'` new headless and headed Chromium under xvfb both aborted here with the WebGPU flags: don't bother.\n- WebKit: WebGL2 fallback, works, screenshots are real ✔. Use it to test the fallback. Firefox: no WebGL2 when headless (`AllowWebgl2:false`); it does have WebGL2 when headed under `xvfb-run` ✔. Not needed for routine tests.\n- On browser close you may see \"WebGPU Device Lost\" in the console: ignore it once closing has started. Also ignore Rapier's deprecated-parameters warning.\n\n**Test hooks.** With `?test=1`, the game exposes `window.__TEST__`:\n- `state()`: JSON with `screen, attract, day, step, profile, marbles, tilt, wobbleAge, event: { id, tier, phase, t, score, lanes: [{ kind, leanX, leanZ, cob, fallen, form, props }] }, input: { lastDevice, glyphSet }, audio: { state, music, sfx, voice }, render: { backend, frames }, errors`.\n- `advance(seconds)`: runs the fixed tick and the frame logic without rendering or waiting. All tests use it, except the ones checking real time and real frames.\n- `bot(skill | null)`: `sharp`, `average`, `clumsy` play through the real input path: they write to the stubbed gamepad, not into the game's state.\n- `goto(screen, args)`, `tuning`, `settings`.","numLines":345,"startLine":356,"totalLines":778}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EHUqvauzogA6uRWMXtwuXZ","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
