{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjCwBgEQWmKvJS6gZsdY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The glass and marbles look right now, so I'm adding that recipe to the plan, marking the palettes verified, and making the cel-shading and vertical-slice requirements explicit. Next up are the exit interview, cleanup, and a check of the plan against TASK.md.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2052,"cache_read_input_tokens":453983,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2052},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"5528a707-c30c-4d86-953b-a146dc2bd04c","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:11:45.181Z","request_id":"req_011CgjCwBP8G3WUAeGK8MCLn","narration_block_indexes":[0],"thinking_duration_ms":2,"thinking_display":"updates"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjCwBgEQWmKvJS6gZsdY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TFPNrb58DPqpxGUEXiihCo","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\ndef sub(old,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==1, ('COUNT %d: '%s.count(old))+old[:70]\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\nsub(\"| noon | 6FC3E8 |\",\"| noon ✔ | 6FC3E8 |\")\nsub(\"- Glass ✔: toon material, `transparent: true, opacity: 0.16, side: DoubleSide, depthWrite: false`, colour `#E8FBFF`, with a hull outline. Good jar.\",\n    \"- Glass ✔: **do not use a toon material on glass.** The hull pass draws solid ink back faces behind it and the glass turns smoky grey. Use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial({ color: 0xe8fbff, transparent: true, opacity: 0.16, side: DoubleSide, depthWrite: false })`, and draw its outline with thin toon torus rings at the base, shoulder, neck and lip, plus a couple of narrow white highlight strips. It looks like a clean inked glass jar.\")\nsub(\"with the glass material from the gotchas above. Marbles:\",\"with the glass recipe from the gotchas above; rings at (r 1.08, y .12), (1.08, 1.9), (.86, 2.2), (.95, 2.5). Marbles:\")\nsub(\"**Materials ✔.** `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color, gradientMap })`\",\"**Materials ✔.** Everything solid is cel-shaded: `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color, gradientMap })`\")\nsub(\"the look of all characters and three palettes;\",\"the look of all characters and all four palettes;\")\nsub(\"Tippy Raft's gain, the noon palette, the walrus and heron animation.\",\"Tippy Raft's gain, all character animation.\")\nsub(\"**Genre:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression.\",\"**Scope.** A feature-complete demo: a complete, polished vertical slice with every system in place, long enough to show everything the game can do. A full pass of the programme runs about 40 minutes.\\n\\n**Genre:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression.\")\nsub(\"so no port is hard-coded and no process needs stopping.\",\"so no port is hard-coded and no process needs stopping. (If you ever do start a server process, stop only that one, by its PID, as TASK.md says.)\")\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nEOF\ncat > planner-exit-interview.json <<'EOF'\n[\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"The split between open creative questions and exact, checkable facts. Genre, perspective and the whole design were mine to decide, while the 'This computer' section and the hub's protocol page with its automatic harness told me exactly what would run and how it would be judged. That let me test the plan's riskiest claims on the real machine instead of guessing. 'What this computer doesn't have', with a stand-in for each missing thing, was especially useful.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was confusing?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Three things. First, 'Follow the hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': the hub's protocol page only covers sessions and says the game is not affected outside one, so it doesn't define multiplayer on its own. I read it as couch play on one screen, from a line in the hub repo's docs/multiplayer-plan.md, but that is my inference. Second, 'the engine's native style' and 'the engine's asset library' don't map onto Three.js, which has neither. Third, TASK.md is written to the builder (file names, model line, exit interview) while STAGE.md asks for a plan, so I had to work out which parts applied at this stage. A smaller one: TASK.md lists Firefox as WebGL 2, but headless Firefox refused a WebGL2 context here (AllowWebgl2:false); it only worked headed under xvfb-run.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Translating a game whose identity is a physical balance board into something a thumb on a stick can't trivially beat, while keeping it original. My first test showed that simply parking the stick on the target scored 100%, because a thumb is far steadier than a body, and it took a slow-sway model to make holding still a real game. Close behind: writing for builders whose capability and budget I don't know, which is why the plan has a build order and a cut order. And I can't hear anything here, so the music and voices are specified by theory and only checked as signals.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"State what multiplayer on its own means, for example 'up to four controllers on one screen is required'. Add the flags that make WebGPU screenshots real to 'This computer': --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader on top of the listed ones, at about 6 fps; without them the screenshot is black and there's no way to judge the art, and new-headless and headed Chromium aborted here. Reword Art Direction for an engine with no native style or asset library. Say how capable the builders are and what budget they have, so a plan can be scoped to them. Mark which TASK.md sections apply at the plan stage. On the hub page, state the 60 and 80 character limits on goal and how-to, which are only in retrofit.md, and that progress and ghost traffic must start within the first few seconds of play, because a game with a long intro fails the harness.\"\n  }\n]\nEOF\npython3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('planner-exit-interview.json'))\nqs=[x['question'] for x in d]\nwant=['What did you like most about this prompt?','What was confusing?','What was the hardest thing to do?','What would you change in this prompt?']\nassert qs==want and all(set(x)=={'question','answer'} and x['answer'] for x in d)\nprint('exit interview parses,',len(d),'answers, questions match STAGE.md')\n\"\necho '--- what is in scratch (all made by this session):'; du -sh _scratch; ls _scratch _scratch/spike | head -40\necho '--- files in the plan that name banned things:'; grep -n -iE 'GameTitle|exit-interview\\.json|claude-|20261104|planner-exit' PLAN.md || echo none\necho '--- task coverage:'; for t in 'Three.js|three@0.180.0' 'R180' 'WebGPU' 'cel-shaded' 'ink' 'rapier' 'Web Audio' 'Gamepad API' 'gibberish' 'Animalese' 'Simlish' '10%|defaults to 10' 'Attract' '10 s' 'Dev Mode' 'Reset to Defaults' 'live|Live sliders' 'Group Class' 'AI Hub' 'hub harness|harness' 'Xbox' 'PlayStation' 'hover|Hover' 'Controller or keyboard required' 'dense' 'playthrough' 'one line|one-line' 'file://' '127.0.0.1' 'PID' 'About/Credits|About / Credits' 'Source Information' 'Testing Feedback' 'Engine Feedback' 'Prompt Feedback' 'exit-interview file|exit interview' 'vertical slice' 'No TODOs|TODO' 'single HTML|Single HTML|self-contained HTML' 'designer' 'Performance is out of scope' 'AnimationMixer' 'subagents'; do printf '%-48s %s\\n' \"$t\" \"$(grep -c -E \"$t\" PLAN.md)\"; done","description":"Finish the plan, write the exit interview, and check coverage against TASK.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2052,"cache_read_input_tokens":453983,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2052},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"7d12ead5-fb50-4ce1-9481-bd9cb4c8b411","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:12:07.307Z","request_id":"req_011CgjCwBP8G3WUAeGK8MCLn","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01TFPNrb58DPqpxGUEXiihCo":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\ndef sub(old,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==1, ('COUNT %d: '%s.count(old))+old[:70]\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\nsub(\"| noon | 6FC3E8 |\",\"| noon ✔ | 6FC3E8 |\")\nsub(\"- Glass ✔: toon material, `transparent: true, opacity: 0.16, side: DoubleSide, depthWrite: false`, colour `#E8FBFF`, with a hull outline. Good jar.\",\n    \"- Glass ✔: **do not use a toon material on glass.** The hull pass draws solid ink back faces behind it and the glass turns smoky grey. Use `MeshBasicNodeMaterial({ color: 0xe8fbff, transparent: true, opacity: 0.16, side: DoubleSide, depthWrite: false })`, and draw its outline with thin toon torus rings at the base, shoulder, neck and lip, plus a couple of narrow white highlight strips. It looks like a clean inked glass jar.\")\nsub(\"with the glass material from the gotchas above. Marbles:\",\"with the glass recipe from the gotchas above; rings at (r 1.08, y .12), (1.08, 1.9), (.86, 2.2), (.95, 2.5). Marbles:\")\nsub(\"**Materials ✔.** `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color, gradientMap })`\",\"**Materials ✔.** Everything solid is cel-shaded: `MeshToonNodeMaterial({ color, gradientMap })`\")\nsub(\"the look of all characters and three palettes;\",\"the look of all characters and all four palettes;\")\nsub(\"Tippy Raft's gain, the noon palette, the walrus and heron animation.\",\"Tippy Raft's gain, all character animation.\")\nsub(\"**Genre:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression.\",\"**Scope.** A feature-complete demo: a complete, polished vertical slice with every system in place, long enough to show everything the game can do. A full pass of the programme runs about 40 minutes.\\n\\n**Genre:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression.\")\nsub(\"so no port is hard-coded and no process needs stopping.\",\"so no port is hard-coded and no process needs stopping. (If you ever do start a server process, stop only that one, by its PID, as TASK.md says.)\")\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nEOF\ncat > planner-exit-interview.json <<'EOF'\n[\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What did you like most about this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"The split between open creative questions and exact, checkable facts. Genre, perspective and the whole design were mine to decide, while the 'This computer' section and the hub's protocol page with its automatic harness told me exactly what would run and how it would be judged. That let me test the plan's riskiest claims on the real machine instead of guessing. 'What this computer doesn't have', with a stand-in for each missing thing, was especially useful.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was confusing?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Three things. First, 'Follow the hub for multiplayer integration into ai-hub as well as for the game on its own': the hub's protocol page only covers sessions and says the game is not affected outside one, so it doesn't define multiplayer on its own. I read it as couch play on one screen, from a line in the hub repo's docs/multiplayer-plan.md, but that is my inference. Second, 'the engine's native style' and 'the engine's asset library' don't map onto Three.js, which has neither. Third, TASK.md is written to the builder (file names, model line, exit interview) while STAGE.md asks for a plan, so I had to work out which parts applied at this stage. A smaller one: TASK.md lists Firefox as WebGL 2, but headless Firefox refused a WebGL2 context here (AllowWebgl2:false); it only worked headed under xvfb-run.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What was the hardest thing to do?\",\n    \"answer\": \"Translating a game whose identity is a physical balance board into something a thumb on a stick can't trivially beat, while keeping it original. My first test showed that simply parking the stick on the target scored 100%, because a thumb is far steadier than a body, and it took a slow-sway model to make holding still a real game. Close behind: writing for builders whose capability and budget I don't know, which is why the plan has a build order and a cut order. And I can't hear anything here, so the music and voices are specified by theory and only checked as signals.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"question\": \"What would you change in this prompt?\",\n    \"answer\": \"State what multiplayer on its own means, for example 'up to four controllers on one screen is required'. Add the flags that make WebGPU screenshots real to 'This computer': --use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader on top of the listed ones, at about 6 fps; without them the screenshot is black and there's no way to judge the art, and new-headless and headed Chromium aborted here. Reword Art Direction for an engine with no native style or asset library. Say how capable the builders are and what budget they have, so a plan can be scoped to them. Mark which TASK.md sections apply at the plan stage. On the hub page, state the 60 and 80 character limits on goal and how-to, which are only in retrofit.md, and that progress and ghost traffic must start within the first few seconds of play, because a game with a long intro fails the harness.\"\n  }\n]\nEOF\npython3 -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('planner-exit-interview.json'))\nqs=[x['question'] for x in d]\nwant=['What did you like most about this prompt?','What was confusing?','What was the hardest thing to do?','What would you change in this prompt?']\nassert qs==want and all(set(x)=={'question','answer'} and x['answer'] for x in d)\nprint('exit interview parses,',len(d),'answers, questions match STAGE.md')\n\"\necho '--- what is in scratch (all made by this session):'; du -sh _scratch; ls _scratch _scratch/spike | head -40\necho '--- files in the plan that name banned things:'; grep -n -iE 'GameTitle|exit-interview\\.json|claude-|20261104|planner-exit' PLAN.md || echo none\necho '--- task coverage:'; for t in 'Three.js|three@0.180.0' 'R180' 'WebGPU' 'cel-shaded' 'ink' 'rapier' 'Web Audio' 'Gamepad API' 'gibberish' 'Animalese' 'Simlish' '10%|defaults to 10' 'Attract' '10 s' 'Dev Mode' 'Reset to Defaults' 'live|Live sliders' 'Group Class' 'AI Hub' 'hub harness|harness' 'Xbox' 'PlayStation' 'hover|Hover' 'Controller or keyboard required' 'dense' 'playthrough' 'one line|one-line' 'file://' '127.0.0.1' 'PID' 'About/Credits|About / Credits' 'Source Information' 'Testing Feedback' 'Engine Feedback' 'Prompt Feedback' 'exit-interview file|exit interview' 'vertical slice' 'No TODOs|TODO' 'single HTML|Single HTML|self-contained HTML' 'designer' 'Performance is out of scope' 'AnimationMixer' 'subagents'; do printf '%-48s %s\\n' \"$t\" \"$(grep -c -E \"$t\" PLAN.md)\"; done","description":"Finish the plan, write the exit interview, and check coverage against TASK.md"}}}
