{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjECU12z6unEnRAQhseL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01HcuUcA2HzFdweHjXscjHYJ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\ndef sub(old,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==1, ('COUNT %d: '%s.count(old))+old[:80]\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\ndef between(start,end,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(start)==1, 'START '+start[:70]\n    a=s.index(start); b=s.index(end,a+len(start))\n    s=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\n\n# ---------- section 9: base style, detail, island map\nsub(\"The look is **sun-bleached Japanese resort postcard meets wooden toy box, drawn in ink.** I rendered this cast and these materials with the recipe below and they read clearly on a TV-sized frame ✔.\",\n    \"The look is **sun-bleached Japanese resort postcard meets wooden toy box, drawn in ink.** I rendered this cast and these materials with the recipe below and they read clearly on a TV-sized frame ✔.\\n\\n**Base style.** TASK.md says to use the engine's native style as the base. Three.js has no house style and no asset library, so the base is its own toolkit: node materials, lights, shadows and TSL post-processing, with its built-in geometries and addons for generic props. Everything that makes the game feel like itself (the cast, the island, the jar, every event's props) is a new asset made in code.\\n\\n**Detail.** The performance budget is unlimited, per TASK.md. Fill the sets: lots of guests, loose props, toy trees, bunting, particles, extra lights where a scene wants them. FPS targets and optimisation are out of scope.\")\nsub(\"**Palette ✔.** Ink `#1A1420` on everything.\",\"**Palette ✔ (all four rendered and checked).** Ink `#1A1420` on everything.\")\nsub(\"- **Comic touches.** Inked world-space words:\",\n\"\"\"- **Island map** (island-local x, z; you arrive at 0, 10; the ring road for Island Loop runs at r 19):\n\n| Landmark | x, z | What's there | Landmark | x, z | What's there |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lighthouse | 2, −9 | The Grand Hold | Bandstand | 15, −3 | Step Class |\n| Jar | −3, −4 | Marble pours | Boardwalk gate | 19, 3 | Room Service |\n| Circus Green | 0, 2 | Plate Spinners | Pier | 15, 12, running out to sea | Fruit Hat, Plank & Perch, Springboard tower at its end |\n| Sun Deck | −16, −3 | Poses | Beach | 5, 17 | Tug of Peace |\n| Lily Pond | −13, 7 | Still Water | Notice Board | 1, 9 | Today's Plan, Journal |\n| Quarry Terrace | −9, −13 | The Cairn | Bed Hut | 8, 8 | End of the day |\n| The Yard | −7, 14 | Log Reps | Mirror pavilion | 9, −14 | Paint and hats |\n| Gazebo | 14, −11 | Group Class, Tippy Raft's dock | | | |\n\n  Guests wander between these spots on simple paths in island-local coordinates. They need no physics.\n- **Comic touches.** Inked world-space words:\"\"\")\n\n# ---------- section 17: rules, stand-ins, bots, tally, regressions\nsub(\"Follow TASK.md's rules: headless scripts first, one command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only what changed, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to review or judge. Keep every file in your folder. Keep to one or two browsers. If you see \\\"Target closed\\\" or \\\"page crashed\\\", memory ran out: run fewer browsers.\",\n\"\"\"Follow TASK.md's rules: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; rerun it only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\n**Sharing this computer (TASK.md).** Other agents run here at the same time.\n- Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp` (it's shared) or a parent folder. Don't read the folders beside yours.\n- Keep to one or two browsers at once and close them when done. \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug: run fewer browsers rather than retrying.\n- Don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`: the browsers are already here. `npm i playwright@1.63.0` finds them ✔. `PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE` is already set; keep it if a script spawns a browser with a clean environment. If you want a Python tool, use `uv run --with ...`; this plan's tools are all Node.\n- Prefer `file://`: every test except `hub` opens the built file directly. WebGPU only works on a secure page, `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1`. A server is only for the hub's harness, bound to 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks, and you confirm the page is your game before trusting a result.\n- Stop only processes you started, by their PID. Never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\n- There is no display. A headed browser needs `xvfb-run`, and you shouldn't need one.\n\n**Stand-ins for what this computer doesn't have.**\n| Missing | Stand-in |\n|---|---|\n| Physical controller | Stub `navigator.getGamepads` (below) |\n| Touch device | Playwright phone and tablet profiles (Pixel 7, iPhone, iPad), tapping with `page.touchscreen` |\n| Someone to listen | `OfflineAudioContext` renders and `AnalyserNode` levels on the live mix |\n| Second player | The harness's 2-4 copies for sessions, four stubbed pads for Group Class |\n| Real iPhone or iPad | WebKit with an iPhone profile; it isn't iOS Safari, so never require more than it shows |\n| TV | Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080 |\"\"\")\nsub(\"**The bots ✔ (human model, already calibrated).** A delayed correction: the bot sees the dot `delay` seconds ago and sets `wish = goal + gain × (goal − dot) + noise × sin(...)`. sharp: delay .16, gain 1.4 (use .7 under stacks). average: delay .26, gain 1.0 (.55 under stacks), noise .03. clumsy: delay .38, gain .8 (.45), noise .07. Do not raise gain under heavy load: it oscillates and tips, which is a bot failure and not a game bug.\",\n\"\"\"**The bots ✔ (the human model every skill number in this plan came from).** A delayed correction: the bot sees the dot `delay` seconds ago and sets `wish = goal + gain × (goal − dot) + noise × sin(...)`. Do not raise gain under heavy load: it oscillates and tips, which is a bot failure and not a game bug.\n\n| Skill | delay | gain | gain under load (stacks, log, tray, Lean Match) | noise | reaction lag | speed error (rad/s) | timing error sd |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| sharp | .16 s | 1.4 | .7 | 0 | .12 s | .3 | 28 ms |\n| average | .26 s | 1.0 | .55 | .03 | .28 s | .8 | 55 ms |\n| clumsy | .38 s | .8 | .45 | .07 | .5 s | 1.4 | 95 ms |\n\n**Keep a tally.** TASK.md asks how many issues testing caught and how many were critical. Append one line to `notes/issues.md` each time a test catches a real issue, as you go. Counting afterwards doesn't work.\"\"\")\nsub(\"Fruit Hat Tier 1: sharp banking at 7 has no falls and never banking has at least one. Use this test to set each `par`. This is how the plan's \\\"balance is key\\\" gets checked without a human.\",\n    \"Fruit Hat Tier 1: sharp banking at 7 has no falls and never banking has at least one. Use this test to set each `par`. This is how \\\"balance is key\\\" gets checked without a human. **Also guard against the failures I hit,** each of which looks fine until you measure it: Plate Spinners Tier 1 must separate the bots (sharp above clumsy by at least 15 points of share); Tug of Peace Tier 1 must be winnable; every Tippy Raft round ends within 90 s; Lean Match scores above .3 for the sharp bot; Log Reps doesn't give the clumsy bot above 70; Room Service Tier 1 with a brace delivers all five; a breath-synced bot beats the same bot unsynced on The Cairn Tier 2.\")\nsub(\"*audio:* music setting defaults to 10 and the bus gain is 0.10. Render each track, sting, SFX and voice with `OfflineAudioContext`: finite, non-silent (RMS above a floor), not clipped. Check detune at tilt 0 is zero.\",\n    \"*audio:* music setting defaults to 10 and the bus gain is 0.10. Render each track, sting, SFX and voice with `OfflineAudioContext`: finite, non-silent (RMS above a floor), not clipped. On the live mix, read an `AnalyserNode` on each bus. Check detune at tilt 0 is zero. No audio files are ever fetched.\")\nsub(\"Touch-only: Pixel 7 and iPhone profiles show the message and Settings, tap opens Settings, no other controls respond, attract runs, a key or pad clears the state.\",\n    \"Touch-only: Pixel 7, iPhone and iPad profiles show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and Settings, `page.touchscreen` tap opens Settings, no other controls respond, attract runs, a key or pad clears the state.\")\n\n# ---------- section 19: reporting\nbetween(\"## 19. Done\",\"---\\n\\n## 20. What was and wasn't verified\",\n\"\"\"## 19. Done and reporting\n\n**Done means** no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features: the demo is complete. Before you finish, check every line:\n\n- [ ] Single HTML where TASK.md says, opens from `file://`, no external requests, no audio files\n- [ ] WebGPU backend by default, WebGL2 fallback works, cel-shaded with the ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Three R180, Web Audio, Gamepad API\n- [ ] Original cast, events, art and music; title is your own random pick; nothing from section 2's lists\n- [ ] All thirteen events, or the ones left after cuts, each with tiers, an intro, results and bots\n- [ ] Programme through the ending, Wobble Age graph, jar, unlocks, Free Play\n- [ ] Controller-first; keyboard and mouse native on the same screens; prompts follow last device; Xbox glyph option; no dense screens\n- [ ] Attract at 10 s, any input exits; Dev Mode from Pause with tools, live sliders and reset; touch-only message and Settings\n- [ ] Group Class 1-4 on one screen; hub harness passes at 1, 2, 4 players, on the bad network, with the host leaving; game on its own passes Enter and Space\n- [ ] Music at 10% by default; all sound in code; gibberish voices with every word on screen\n- [ ] `node tools/test.mjs full` passes; a scripted whole-game playthrough is part of it\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubs in `src/`\n- [ ] About/Credits, the exit-interview file and your chat reply are all filled in as below\n\n**Three places, the same content.** TASK.md calls this critical: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in **your chat reply** and an **in-game About/Credits screen,** and into **the exit-interview file** beside the HTML. These are your own answers from your own build; nothing in this plan answers them for you. `tools/build.mjs` reads the exit-interview file into `src/data/about.js` so there is one source of truth. Write it after your tests, rebuild, rerun `ui`. During development About shows what is known so far. About is paged, in large type, and also credits Fredoka (SIL OFL), Three.js and Rapier. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n\n**Source Information.** Field by field:\n\n| Field | What to write |\n|---|---|\n| Date | The date line in your TASK.md |\n| Source | Wii Fit |\n| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given |\n| Genre and Perspective | Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island |\n| Tech | Three.js R180 on the WebGPU backend (WebGL2 fallback), TSL toon and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild, Fredoka, plus anything you added, with versions |\n| Art Direction | Cel-shaded ink filter over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code on Three.js's own materials |\n| Animation | Three.js `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean from the physics, Rapier ragdolls |\n| Audio | Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices; no audio files |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen |\n| Testing | The testing parameters TASK.md allowed, and what you ran |\n| Research | Yours, or none, beside this plan's: the hub's protocol pages and code; background on the source from Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Nintendo's Iwata Asks interviews, read as search summaries. How much more to research is your choice |\n| Environment | As written in your TASK.md |\n| Model | As written in your TASK.md |\n| Usage | Exactly `added on import`. You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it |\n\n**Testing Feedback,** answered in order (`notes/issues.md` gives you the counts):\n1. How many issues did testing catch?\n2. How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n3. What was the most helpful test you ran?\n4. What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\n**Engine Feedback,** answered in order:\n1. What did you like most about the engine?\n2. Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n3. What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n4. What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n5. What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n6. If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\n**Prompt Feedback,** answered in order:\n1. What did you like most about this prompt?\n2. What was confusing?\n3. What was the hardest thing to do?\n4. What would you change in this prompt?\n\n**The exit-interview file** is one JSON object in TASK.md's exact shape, plain text, every value filled in, and you check that it parses: `sourceInformation` (`date, source, plan, genre, perspective, tech, artDirection, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model, usage`), `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback`, `promptFeedback` (arrays, answers in order), and `aiHub`:\n\n| `aiHub` field | Suggested |\n|---|---|\n| `name` | The game's title, as on its title screen |\n| `tagline` | A few words of your own |\n| `description` | One or two sentences, such as: a wooden-toy wellness retreat on a crooked island, where you hold poses, stack stones and catch fruit with real balance physics, and straighten the whole island one class at a time |\n| `type` | Game |\n| `genre` | Sports |\n| `source` | Wii Fit |\n| `series` | Empty: the franchise is the source itself, and the hub's catalogue wants it empty then |\n| `players` | 1–4 |\n| `controls` | The same line as `hub:session-howto` |\n| `tags` | 3 to 6 lowercase, such as 3d, comedy, third-person, versus, arcade |\n\n\"\"\")\n\n# ---------- section 20\nbetween(\"**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above), with throwaway spikes:**\",\"\\n\\n## 21\" if \"\\n\\n## 21\" in s else \"\\x00\",\n\"\") if False else None\na=s.index(\"**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above), with throwaway spikes:**\")\ns=s[:a]+\"\"\"**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above), with throwaway prototypes and simulations:** versions exist on npm and jsDelivr; single-file esbuild build from `file://`; WebGPU in Chromium with SwiftShader; WebGL2 fallback in Chromium and WebKit; `toonOutlinePass` with MRT and the custom ink pass (run from this document's own code); instanced toon meshes; glass; shadow settings; the look of the cast and all four palettes; the hub harness with WebGPU, own card and ghost mode; client hash; balance model (run from this document's own code); torque limit; gusts and flail; island tilt (run from this document's own code); marble pours; ragdoll; touch media queries; Fredoka's licence and file names. **Every event's numbers were simulated with the bots:** Fruit Hat, Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Room Service, Springboard's board and air time, Tippy Raft, and the Check-In. Doing that overturned my first numbers for Plate Spinners, Tug of Peace, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Room Service, Tippy Raft, Lean Match and The Cairn's top tier, so expect the same when you build: **measure before you trust a number.**\n\n**Not tested, and these are your call:**\n- The bots are a model of a person. Their timing and tracking errors are my assumptions, so all skill results are estimates. Real thumbs may differ, and a mouse or keyboard more so: only the stick's model was simulated.\n- Step Class's and Plate Spinners' results come from logic simulations, not the whole game. Springboard's hit chances are arithmetic from a release-timing error. Island Loop is only arithmetic, and the Grand Hold reuses tested parts but wasn't simulated as a whole.\n- All character animation, and any art beyond the cast and a test scene.\n- I could not listen to anything. The music and voices are specified by theory, so check them with the audio tests and trust the arrangement.\n- Nobody has played any of this with a real controller on a real TV. Whether it is fun is yours to judge, by playing it through the bots' eyes and your own tests.\n\n---\n\n## 21. TASK.md coverage\n\nEvery requirement in TASK.md, in its order, and where this plan answers it.\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Answered in |\n|---|---|\n| Source: Wii Fit | Section 1, what we keep from it; section 2, what we don't copy |\n| Genre, picked | Section 1 |\n| Perspective, picked | Section 1; each event's camera in section 6 |\n| Title: a riff, 50 brainstormed, picked with a real random number generator | Left to you, as this plan's brief says: section 1 |\n| Research, your choice | Section 19, Research row |\n| Package: one HTML file; downloadable dependencies optional | Section 16, Build: everything inlined, no downloads |\n| Three.js R180 | Section 16, Stack |\n| WebGPU | Section 16, Renderer; tested in section 17 |\n| Stylized cel-shaded / ink filter | Section 9, Materials and the ink recipe |\n| Rapier 0.19.3 | Sections 4 and 16 |\n| Web Audio | Section 11 |\n| Gamepad API | Section 3; stubs in section 17 |\n| Rest of the stack is yours | Section 16: esbuild, Playwright, Fredoka |\n| Art Direction: engine's style as base, new assets, library for generic props | Section 9, Base style and Model recipes |\n| Animation: engine's system as base, new animations | Section 10 |\n| Audio: all in code, no files | Section 11; unit test greps in section 17 |\n| Voices: Animalese / Simlish-style gibberish | Section 11, Voices |\n| Music at 10% by default | Sections 7 and 11; `audio` test in section 17 |\n| Online: AI Hub | Section 12 |\n| Subfolder for dev files; modules bundled to one file in root | Section 16: file tree and Build |\n| External CDN allowed | Section 16: not needed, everything is inlined |\n| Don't read outside your folder; shared ports and processes | Section 17, Sharing this computer |\n| Prefer `file://` and headless tools; 127.0.0.1 on an OS-picked port; confirm the page; stop only your own PIDs | Section 17, Sharing this computer, and the `hub` test |\n| Performance budget unlimited; FPS and optimisation out of scope | Section 9, Detail; section 16, Renderer |\n| Testing: scripts that play and check state, scripted whole-game playthrough | Section 17: test hooks, `flow` test |\n| One command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes | Section 17, The command |\n| Screenshots only for what a script can't check | Section 17: art pass |\n| No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit | Section 17, first paragraph |\n| Effort, your choice | Section 18 gives an order; how far you take it is yours |\n| This computer: tools, browsers, WebGPU flags and secure pages | Sections 16 and 17, Browser flags |\n| Display, Firefox, WebKit, blank WebGPU screenshots | Section 17, Browser flags |\n| Memory and earlyoom | Section 17, Sharing this computer |\n| No controller, touch, listener, second player, iPhone or TV: use stand-ins | Section 17, Stand-ins |\n| Sharing: browsers, `/tmp`, `playwright install`, system Python and `uv` | Section 17, Sharing this computer |\n| Original game inspired by the source | Sections 1 and 2 |\n| Feature-complete demo, polished vertical slice, long enough to show everything | Section 1, Scope; section 5, the day table; section 18 |\n| Capture the source's world, characters, themes and tone in mechanics, pacing and UX | Section 1's table; sections 4, 5 and 8 |\n| Infrastructure so the rest can be built with normal designer tools | Section 14, For later designers; `src/data/` in section 16 |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, standard tools | Section 14; Pause in section 7 |\n| Live sliders for the key variables, reset to defaults | Section 14 |\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, looping AI demo, any input exits, may cheat, shows as much as possible | Section 13 |\n| Multiplayer: hub integration | Section 12, AI Hub session |\n| Multiplayer: the game on its own | Section 12, Group Class and On its own |\n| Narrative tone like the source's | Section 8 |\n| 10-foot, controller-first, DualSense or any standard pad | Sections 3 and 7 |\n| Keyboard and mouse: same game, same screens, native, expected keys | Section 3 |\n| Mouse aims, looks, clicks menus and world things; hover moves the focus | Section 3 |\n| Prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs; Xbox option; keys and mouse buttons | Section 3; `input` test in section 17 |\n| No dense, web-style data screens | Section 7, The 10-foot rules; `ui` test in section 17 |\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, \"Controller or keyboard required\", Settings, attract runs | Section 15 |\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | Sections 18 and 19; unit test greps in section 17 |\n| Source Information, all 14 fields, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19, field by field |\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19, every question listed |\n| Exit-interview file: shape, parses, `aiHub` block | Section 19 |\n\"\"\"\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nprint('words',len(s.split()))\nEOF\ngrep -n -iE 'GameTitle|exit-interview\\.json|claude-|20261104|planner-exit' PLAN.md || echo 'no banned names'; grep -n '^## ' PLAN.md","description":"Add environment rules, island map, reporting and coverage table to the plan"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17577,"cache_read_input_tokens":549344,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17577},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"7cc2186d-82f3-4d1a-a2a4-07667a626678","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:29:06.059Z","request_id":"req_011CgjECThgPjsby14duPDju","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01HcuUcA2HzFdweHjXscjHYJ":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\ndef sub(old,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==1, ('COUNT %d: '%s.count(old))+old[:80]\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\ndef between(start,end,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(start)==1, 'START '+start[:70]\n    a=s.index(start); b=s.index(end,a+len(start))\n    s=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\n\n# ---------- section 9: base style, detail, island map\nsub(\"The look is **sun-bleached Japanese resort postcard meets wooden toy box, drawn in ink.** I rendered this cast and these materials with the recipe below and they read clearly on a TV-sized frame ✔.\",\n    \"The look is **sun-bleached Japanese resort postcard meets wooden toy box, drawn in ink.** I rendered this cast and these materials with the recipe below and they read clearly on a TV-sized frame ✔.\\n\\n**Base style.** TASK.md says to use the engine's native style as the base. Three.js has no house style and no asset library, so the base is its own toolkit: node materials, lights, shadows and TSL post-processing, with its built-in geometries and addons for generic props. Everything that makes the game feel like itself (the cast, the island, the jar, every event's props) is a new asset made in code.\\n\\n**Detail.** The performance budget is unlimited, per TASK.md. Fill the sets: lots of guests, loose props, toy trees, bunting, particles, extra lights where a scene wants them. FPS targets and optimisation are out of scope.\")\nsub(\"**Palette ✔.** Ink `#1A1420` on everything.\",\"**Palette ✔ (all four rendered and checked).** Ink `#1A1420` on everything.\")\nsub(\"- **Comic touches.** Inked world-space words:\",\n\"\"\"- **Island map** (island-local x, z; you arrive at 0, 10; the ring road for Island Loop runs at r 19):\n\n| Landmark | x, z | What's there | Landmark | x, z | What's there |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Lighthouse | 2, −9 | The Grand Hold | Bandstand | 15, −3 | Step Class |\n| Jar | −3, −4 | Marble pours | Boardwalk gate | 19, 3 | Room Service |\n| Circus Green | 0, 2 | Plate Spinners | Pier | 15, 12, running out to sea | Fruit Hat, Plank & Perch, Springboard tower at its end |\n| Sun Deck | −16, −3 | Poses | Beach | 5, 17 | Tug of Peace |\n| Lily Pond | −13, 7 | Still Water | Notice Board | 1, 9 | Today's Plan, Journal |\n| Quarry Terrace | −9, −13 | The Cairn | Bed Hut | 8, 8 | End of the day |\n| The Yard | −7, 14 | Log Reps | Mirror pavilion | 9, −14 | Paint and hats |\n| Gazebo | 14, −11 | Group Class, Tippy Raft's dock | | | |\n\n  Guests wander between these spots on simple paths in island-local coordinates. They need no physics.\n- **Comic touches.** Inked world-space words:\"\"\")\n\n# ---------- section 17: rules, stand-ins, bots, tally, regressions\nsub(\"Follow TASK.md's rules: headless scripts first, one command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only what changed, screenshots only for what a script can't check, no subagents or workflows to review or judge. Keep every file in your folder. Keep to one or two browsers. If you see \\\"Target closed\\\" or \\\"page crashed\\\", memory ran out: run fewer browsers.\",\n\"\"\"Follow TASK.md's rules: scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count; rerun it only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n\n**Sharing this computer (TASK.md).** Other agents run here at the same time.\n- Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp` (it's shared) or a parent folder. Don't read the folders beside yours.\n- Keep to one or two browsers at once and close them when done. \"Target closed\", \"page crashed\" or \"browser has been closed\" means memory ran out, not a bug: run fewer browsers rather than retrying.\n- Don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`: the browsers are already here. `npm i playwright@1.63.0` finds them ✔. `PLAYWRIGHT_HOST_PLATFORM_OVERRIDE` is already set; keep it if a script spawns a browser with a clean environment. If you want a Python tool, use `uv run --with ...`; this plan's tools are all Node.\n- Prefer `file://`: every test except `hub` opens the built file directly. WebGPU only works on a secure page, `file://` or `http://127.0.0.1`. A server is only for the hub's harness, bound to 127.0.0.1 on a port the OS picks, and you confirm the page is your game before trusting a result.\n- Stop only processes you started, by their PID. Never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`, or kill whatever holds a port.\n- There is no display. A headed browser needs `xvfb-run`, and you shouldn't need one.\n\n**Stand-ins for what this computer doesn't have.**\n| Missing | Stand-in |\n|---|---|\n| Physical controller | Stub `navigator.getGamepads` (below) |\n| Touch device | Playwright phone and tablet profiles (Pixel 7, iPhone, iPad), tapping with `page.touchscreen` |\n| Someone to listen | `OfflineAudioContext` renders and `AnalyserNode` levels on the live mix |\n| Second player | The harness's 2-4 copies for sessions, four stubbed pads for Group Class |\n| Real iPhone or iPad | WebKit with an iPhone profile; it isn't iOS Safari, so never require more than it shows |\n| TV | Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080 |\"\"\")\nsub(\"**The bots ✔ (human model, already calibrated).** A delayed correction: the bot sees the dot `delay` seconds ago and sets `wish = goal + gain × (goal − dot) + noise × sin(...)`. sharp: delay .16, gain 1.4 (use .7 under stacks). average: delay .26, gain 1.0 (.55 under stacks), noise .03. clumsy: delay .38, gain .8 (.45), noise .07. Do not raise gain under heavy load: it oscillates and tips, which is a bot failure and not a game bug.\",\n\"\"\"**The bots ✔ (the human model every skill number in this plan came from).** A delayed correction: the bot sees the dot `delay` seconds ago and sets `wish = goal + gain × (goal − dot) + noise × sin(...)`. Do not raise gain under heavy load: it oscillates and tips, which is a bot failure and not a game bug.\n\n| Skill | delay | gain | gain under load (stacks, log, tray, Lean Match) | noise | reaction lag | speed error (rad/s) | timing error sd |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| sharp | .16 s | 1.4 | .7 | 0 | .12 s | .3 | 28 ms |\n| average | .26 s | 1.0 | .55 | .03 | .28 s | .8 | 55 ms |\n| clumsy | .38 s | .8 | .45 | .07 | .5 s | 1.4 | 95 ms |\n\n**Keep a tally.** TASK.md asks how many issues testing caught and how many were critical. Append one line to `notes/issues.md` each time a test catches a real issue, as you go. Counting afterwards doesn't work.\"\"\")\nsub(\"Fruit Hat Tier 1: sharp banking at 7 has no falls and never banking has at least one. Use this test to set each `par`. This is how the plan's \\\"balance is key\\\" gets checked without a human.\",\n    \"Fruit Hat Tier 1: sharp banking at 7 has no falls and never banking has at least one. Use this test to set each `par`. This is how \\\"balance is key\\\" gets checked without a human. **Also guard against the failures I hit,** each of which looks fine until you measure it: Plate Spinners Tier 1 must separate the bots (sharp above clumsy by at least 15 points of share); Tug of Peace Tier 1 must be winnable; every Tippy Raft round ends within 90 s; Lean Match scores above .3 for the sharp bot; Log Reps doesn't give the clumsy bot above 70; Room Service Tier 1 with a brace delivers all five; a breath-synced bot beats the same bot unsynced on The Cairn Tier 2.\")\nsub(\"*audio:* music setting defaults to 10 and the bus gain is 0.10. Render each track, sting, SFX and voice with `OfflineAudioContext`: finite, non-silent (RMS above a floor), not clipped. Check detune at tilt 0 is zero.\",\n    \"*audio:* music setting defaults to 10 and the bus gain is 0.10. Render each track, sting, SFX and voice with `OfflineAudioContext`: finite, non-silent (RMS above a floor), not clipped. On the live mix, read an `AnalyserNode` on each bus. Check detune at tilt 0 is zero. No audio files are ever fetched.\")\nsub(\"Touch-only: Pixel 7 and iPhone profiles show the message and Settings, tap opens Settings, no other controls respond, attract runs, a key or pad clears the state.\",\n    \"Touch-only: Pixel 7, iPhone and iPad profiles show \\\"Controller or keyboard required\\\" and Settings, `page.touchscreen` tap opens Settings, no other controls respond, attract runs, a key or pad clears the state.\")\n\n# ---------- section 19: reporting\nbetween(\"## 19. Done\",\"---\\n\\n## 20. What was and wasn't verified\",\n\"\"\"## 19. Done and reporting\n\n**Done means** no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features: the demo is complete. Before you finish, check every line:\n\n- [ ] Single HTML where TASK.md says, opens from `file://`, no external requests, no audio files\n- [ ] WebGPU backend by default, WebGL2 fallback works, cel-shaded with the ink filter, Rapier 0.19.3, Three R180, Web Audio, Gamepad API\n- [ ] Original cast, events, art and music; title is your own random pick; nothing from section 2's lists\n- [ ] All thirteen events, or the ones left after cuts, each with tiers, an intro, results and bots\n- [ ] Programme through the ending, Wobble Age graph, jar, unlocks, Free Play\n- [ ] Controller-first; keyboard and mouse native on the same screens; prompts follow last device; Xbox glyph option; no dense screens\n- [ ] Attract at 10 s, any input exits; Dev Mode from Pause with tools, live sliders and reset; touch-only message and Settings\n- [ ] Group Class 1-4 on one screen; hub harness passes at 1, 2, 4 players, on the bad network, with the host leaving; game on its own passes Enter and Space\n- [ ] Music at 10% by default; all sound in code; gibberish voices with every word on screen\n- [ ] `node tools/test.mjs full` passes; a scripted whole-game playthrough is part of it\n- [ ] No TODOs, placeholders or stubs in `src/`\n- [ ] About/Credits, the exit-interview file and your chat reply are all filled in as below\n\n**Three places, the same content.** TASK.md calls this critical: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in **your chat reply** and an **in-game About/Credits screen,** and into **the exit-interview file** beside the HTML. These are your own answers from your own build; nothing in this plan answers them for you. `tools/build.mjs` reads the exit-interview file into `src/data/about.js` so there is one source of truth. Write it after your tests, rebuild, rerun `ui`. During development About shows what is known so far. About is paged, in large type, and also credits Fredoka (SIL OFL), Three.js and Rapier. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n\n**Source Information.** Field by field:\n\n| Field | What to write |\n|---|---|\n| Date | The date line in your TASK.md |\n| Source | Wii Fit |\n| Plan | The file name of the plan you were given |\n| Genre and Perspective | Physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras per event and an orbitable chase camera on the island |\n| Tech | Three.js R180 on the WebGPU backend (WebGL2 fallback), TSL toon and ink post, Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild, Fredoka, plus anything you added, with versions |\n| Art Direction | Cel-shaded ink filter over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code on Three.js's own materials |\n| Animation | Three.js `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, procedural lean from the physics, Rapier ragdolls |\n| Audio | Code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, synthesised sound effects, Animalese and Simlish-style gibberish voices; no audio files |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1-4 devices, plus 1-4 players on one screen |\n| Testing | The testing parameters TASK.md allowed, and what you ran |\n| Research | Yours, or none, beside this plan's: the hub's protocol pages and code; background on the source from Wikipedia, a Nintendo World Report review and Nintendo's Iwata Asks interviews, read as search summaries. How much more to research is your choice |\n| Environment | As written in your TASK.md |\n| Model | As written in your TASK.md |\n| Usage | Exactly `added on import`. You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it |\n\n**Testing Feedback,** answered in order (`notes/issues.md` gives you the counts):\n1. How many issues did testing catch?\n2. How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n3. What was the most helpful test you ran?\n4. What was the least helpful test you ran?\n\n**Engine Feedback,** answered in order:\n1. What did you like most about the engine?\n2. Did you get stuck or guess wrong?\n3. What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?\n4. What was the hardest part about using the engine?\n5. What did you change in the engine, and why? (none if nothing)\n6. If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?\n\n**Prompt Feedback,** answered in order:\n1. What did you like most about this prompt?\n2. What was confusing?\n3. What was the hardest thing to do?\n4. What would you change in this prompt?\n\n**The exit-interview file** is one JSON object in TASK.md's exact shape, plain text, every value filled in, and you check that it parses: `sourceInformation` (`date, source, plan, genre, perspective, tech, artDirection, animation, audio, online, testing, research, environment, model, usage`), `testingFeedback`, `engineFeedback`, `promptFeedback` (arrays, answers in order), and `aiHub`:\n\n| `aiHub` field | Suggested |\n|---|---|\n| `name` | The game's title, as on its title screen |\n| `tagline` | A few words of your own |\n| `description` | One or two sentences, such as: a wooden-toy wellness retreat on a crooked island, where you hold poses, stack stones and catch fruit with real balance physics, and straighten the whole island one class at a time |\n| `type` | Game |\n| `genre` | Sports |\n| `source` | Wii Fit |\n| `series` | Empty: the franchise is the source itself, and the hub's catalogue wants it empty then |\n| `players` | 1–4 |\n| `controls` | The same line as `hub:session-howto` |\n| `tags` | 3 to 6 lowercase, such as 3d, comedy, third-person, versus, arcade |\n\n\"\"\")\n\n# ---------- section 20\nbetween(\"**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above), with throwaway spikes:**\",\"\\n\\n## 21\" if \"\\n\\n## 21\" in s else \"\\x00\",\n\"\") if False else None\na=s.index(\"**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above), with throwaway spikes:**\")\ns=s[:a]+\"\"\"**Tested here on 2026-11-04 (✔ above), with throwaway prototypes and simulations:** versions exist on npm and jsDelivr; single-file esbuild build from `file://`; WebGPU in Chromium with SwiftShader; WebGL2 fallback in Chromium and WebKit; `toonOutlinePass` with MRT and the custom ink pass (run from this document's own code); instanced toon meshes; glass; shadow settings; the look of the cast and all four palettes; the hub harness with WebGPU, own card and ghost mode; client hash; balance model (run from this document's own code); torque limit; gusts and flail; island tilt (run from this document's own code); marble pours; ragdoll; touch media queries; Fredoka's licence and file names. **Every event's numbers were simulated with the bots:** Fruit Hat, Poses, The Cairn, Still Water, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Tug of Peace, Step Class, Plate Spinners, Room Service, Springboard's board and air time, Tippy Raft, and the Check-In. Doing that overturned my first numbers for Plate Spinners, Tug of Peace, Log Reps, Plank & Perch, Room Service, Tippy Raft, Lean Match and The Cairn's top tier, so expect the same when you build: **measure before you trust a number.**\n\n**Not tested, and these are your call:**\n- The bots are a model of a person. Their timing and tracking errors are my assumptions, so all skill results are estimates. Real thumbs may differ, and a mouse or keyboard more so: only the stick's model was simulated.\n- Step Class's and Plate Spinners' results come from logic simulations, not the whole game. Springboard's hit chances are arithmetic from a release-timing error. Island Loop is only arithmetic, and the Grand Hold reuses tested parts but wasn't simulated as a whole.\n- All character animation, and any art beyond the cast and a test scene.\n- I could not listen to anything. The music and voices are specified by theory, so check them with the audio tests and trust the arrangement.\n- Nobody has played any of this with a real controller on a real TV. Whether it is fun is yours to judge, by playing it through the bots' eyes and your own tests.\n\n---\n\n## 21. TASK.md coverage\n\nEvery requirement in TASK.md, in its order, and where this plan answers it.\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Answered in |\n|---|---|\n| Source: Wii Fit | Section 1, what we keep from it; section 2, what we don't copy |\n| Genre, picked | Section 1 |\n| Perspective, picked | Section 1; each event's camera in section 6 |\n| Title: a riff, 50 brainstormed, picked with a real random number generator | Left to you, as this plan's brief says: section 1 |\n| Research, your choice | Section 19, Research row |\n| Package: one HTML file; downloadable dependencies optional | Section 16, Build: everything inlined, no downloads |\n| Three.js R180 | Section 16, Stack |\n| WebGPU | Section 16, Renderer; tested in section 17 |\n| Stylized cel-shaded / ink filter | Section 9, Materials and the ink recipe |\n| Rapier 0.19.3 | Sections 4 and 16 |\n| Web Audio | Section 11 |\n| Gamepad API | Section 3; stubs in section 17 |\n| Rest of the stack is yours | Section 16: esbuild, Playwright, Fredoka |\n| Art Direction: engine's style as base, new assets, library for generic props | Section 9, Base style and Model recipes |\n| Animation: engine's system as base, new animations | Section 10 |\n| Audio: all in code, no files | Section 11; unit test greps in section 17 |\n| Voices: Animalese / Simlish-style gibberish | Section 11, Voices |\n| Music at 10% by default | Sections 7 and 11; `audio` test in section 17 |\n| Online: AI Hub | Section 12 |\n| Subfolder for dev files; modules bundled to one file in root | Section 16: file tree and Build |\n| External CDN allowed | Section 16: not needed, everything is inlined |\n| Don't read outside your folder; shared ports and processes | Section 17, Sharing this computer |\n| Prefer `file://` and headless tools; 127.0.0.1 on an OS-picked port; confirm the page; stop only your own PIDs | Section 17, Sharing this computer, and the `hub` test |\n| Performance budget unlimited; FPS and optimisation out of scope | Section 9, Detail; section 16, Renderer |\n| Testing: scripts that play and check state, scripted whole-game playthrough | Section 17: test hooks, `flow` test |\n| One command, only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changes | Section 17, The command |\n| Screenshots only for what a script can't check | Section 17: art pass |\n| No subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit | Section 17, first paragraph |\n| Effort, your choice | Section 18 gives an order; how far you take it is yours |\n| This computer: tools, browsers, WebGPU flags and secure pages | Sections 16 and 17, Browser flags |\n| Display, Firefox, WebKit, blank WebGPU screenshots | Section 17, Browser flags |\n| Memory and earlyoom | Section 17, Sharing this computer |\n| No controller, touch, listener, second player, iPhone or TV: use stand-ins | Section 17, Stand-ins |\n| Sharing: browsers, `/tmp`, `playwright install`, system Python and `uv` | Section 17, Sharing this computer |\n| Original game inspired by the source | Sections 1 and 2 |\n| Feature-complete demo, polished vertical slice, long enough to show everything | Section 1, Scope; section 5, the day table; section 18 |\n| Capture the source's world, characters, themes and tone in mechanics, pacing and UX | Section 1's table; sections 4, 5 and 8 |\n| Infrastructure so the rest can be built with normal designer tools | Section 14, For later designers; `src/data/` in section 16 |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, standard tools | Section 14; Pause in section 7 |\n| Live sliders for the key variables, reset to defaults | Section 14 |\n| Attract mode: 10 s idle on title or menu, looping AI demo, any input exits, may cheat, shows as much as possible | Section 13 |\n| Multiplayer: hub integration | Section 12, AI Hub session |\n| Multiplayer: the game on its own | Section 12, Group Class and On its own |\n| Narrative tone like the source's | Section 8 |\n| 10-foot, controller-first, DualSense or any standard pad | Sections 3 and 7 |\n| Keyboard and mouse: same game, same screens, native, expected keys | Section 3 |\n| Mouse aims, looks, clicks menus and world things; hover moves the focus | Section 3 |\n| Prompts follow last input; PlayStation glyphs; Xbox option; keys and mouse buttons | Section 3; `input` test in section 17 |\n| No dense, web-style data screens | Section 7, The 10-foot rules; `ui` test in section 17 |\n| Touch-only: no touch controls, \"Controller or keyboard required\", Settings, attract runs | Section 15 |\n| Done: no TODOs, placeholders or stubs | Sections 18 and 19; unit test greps in section 17 |\n| Source Information, all 14 fields, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19, field by field |\n| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19, every question listed |\n| Exit-interview file: shape, parses, `aiHub` block | Section 19 |\n\"\"\"\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nprint('words',len(s.split()))\nEOF\ngrep -n -iE 'GameTitle|exit-interview\\.json|claude-|20261104|planner-exit' PLAN.md || echo 'no banned names'; grep -n '^## ' PLAN.md","description":"Add environment rules, island map, reporting and coverage table to the plan"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01HcuUcA2HzFdweHjXscjHYJ":{"cwd":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work"}}}
