{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WrATaaMbvKxFgTaTLswUu1","type":"tool_result","content":"176\t## 5. Programme and progress\n177\t\n178\t**Days are game days, not calendar days.** A day ends when you go to bed. The real date is only used for absence jokes and calendar stamps.\n179\t\n180\t| Day | Name | Check-In tests | Classes | What it teaches |\n181\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n182\t| 1 | Arrival | Stand Still, Lean Match, Step Time | Poses, Fruit Hat | Lean, flail, breath, the jar |\n183\t| 2 | The Second Day, Which Is Harder | Stand Still, Lean Match, Quick Lean | Log Reps, Step Class, The Cairn | Feet, rhythm, stacking |\n184\t| 3 | Service With a Wobble | One-Foot Hold, Lean Match, Step Time | Room Service, Plate Spinners, Plank & Perch | Stepping, stick circles. The Mirror opens |\n185\t| 4 | A Little Damp | Quick Lean, One-Foot Hold, Stand Still | Springboard, Still Water, Island Loop | Air control. Group Class invitation |\n186\t| 5 | Level Day | Your three best tests | Tippy Raft, Tug of Peace, The Grand Hold | The ending |\n187\t| 6+ | Maintenance | Random three | Trudy's random plan of three | Free Play continues forever |\n188\t\n189\tBuild the day list from the event registry: an event that was cut (section 18) is skipped and everything still works.\n190\t\n191\t**A day's flow:** morning line → personal question (Yes/No) → Check-In → Wobble Age reveal → Today's Plan (cards in any order: walk there or pick a card to go directly) → each class → Results → marbles → the island comes up a notch → extra Free Play if wanted → the bed hut. Press Use, the screen inks out, a stamp lands on the calendar, the next dawn. Palettes follow the day's steps (dawn, noon, golden, dusk), unless an event pins its own (Step Class at dusk, The Grand Hold at sunset).\n192\t\n193\t**The Check-In** (about 90 s) takes place on a round mat with Trudy. Three tests, each one screen with one big instruction. Each scores 0 to 1 (clamped):\n194\t- **Stand Still** (10 s, sway .1, tremor .06): keep the dot in the centre. Score `1 − meanDistance / .25` ✔ .82 / .74 / .49.\n195\t- **Lean Match** (18 s, sway .04): \"Lean 70% left.\" A bar shows your percentage. Three targets, 6 s each, ignoring the first 1.5 s of each. Score `1 − meanError / .4` ✔ .58 / .45 / .27. (My first version, hold within ±5% for 2 s, was impossible: every bot timed out.)\n196\t- **Step Time** (16 s): alternate feet with a metronome at 96 BPM. Score `1 − meanError / 120 ms` ✔ .81 / .63 / .37.\n197\t- **Quick Lean** (15 s): lean toward the lit quadrant, six prompts. Score `1 − (meanReaction − .35 s) / .9 s` ✔ .87 / .69 / .44.\n198\t- **One-Foot Hold** (12 s): keep R2 down and L2 up, which halves `margin`, while keeping the dot centred. Score as Stand Still ✔ .55 / .48 / .40.\n199\t\n200\t**Wobble Age.** `age = round(18 + 81 × (1 − mean)^1.3 + handicap)`, clamped to 18..99. The handicap is a comic head start that shrinks each day: +24, +16, +8, +3, then 0. So the number always falls, and that is what makes coming back feel good. With unchanged skill, Day 1 to Day 5 ✔: 56→32 / 66→42 / 86→62. The reveal is the comic set piece: lights dim, drum roll, numbers spin on a wooden counter, sting, Trudy's line from a band of lines by age. Stored per day and drawn as the Journal's graph. It never asks your real age (\"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\").\n201\t\n202\t**Marbles.** After each class: 2 per minute of active play (min 2) + medal bonus (1, 2, 3, 5) + 2 for a first clear. Each Check-In gives 1. They pour physically into the jar on the island, as real Rapier balls: a 200-marble pour runs fine (✔ 0.23 ms per step; 500 is 0.67 ms). Marble colour shows the medal. Thresholds unlock hats and paints (`src/data/unlocks.js`: 6, 14, 24, 36, 50, 60 marbles). A full programme pays about 15, 37, 59, 81, 103 by the end of each day, or 61 in total with all Tier B events cut, so everything unlocks either way ✔.\n203\t\n204\t**The island's tilt depends on progress, not skill.** Rest tilt = 9° × (1 − completed programme steps / total steps), so nobody can be stuck. Each step lowers it with a long wooden creak, all loose props shuffling and Trudy reacting. The last notch happens inside The Grand Hold.\n205\t\n206\t**Medals** come from form (0 to 100): ≥40 Adequate, ≥65 Pleasant, ≥85 Exemplary, ≥97 Suspicious. Below 40 is still a pass (\"Attended\"). Events that measure a share of the maximum (Poses, Still Water) use it directly. All others set a `par` so that form = 100 × raw/par, per tier, so the sharp bot lands 88 to 95 (section 17).\n207\t\n208\t**Tiers.** Every event has three: Gentle, Honest, Unreasonable. Tier 2 opens with a Pleasant on Tier 1, Tier 3 with an Exemplary on Tier 2. Days 1 to 5 use Tier 1. Maintenance days use the highest tier opened. Free Play offers all open tiers.\n209\t\n210\t**Free Play** opens each event once it has been met in the programme. Walk to its pavilion, or use the menu.\n211\t\n212\t**The ending.** The Grand Hold (section 6) ends with the island level. Trudy's bubble centres for the first time. She has only ever seen the world at nine degrees. \"Oh. So that's what the middle looks like.\" Silence, then the marbles flare, the lighthouse lights, and the credits roll over a slow pan of everything you straightened. Then: \"It's quite flat, isn't it? ...Same time tomorrow?\" Save the \"levelled\" state, unlock Free Play for everything, and carry on into Maintenance days.\n213\t\n214\t**Profiles.** Up to four, each with a name, paint, hat, days, ages, medals, marbles and counts. Name is a spinner over generated names (no text entry; `src/data/names.js`). Save JSON in `localStorage` with a version number, read inside try/catch, and fall back to memory if storage is unavailable.\n215\t\n216\t---\n217\t\n218\t## 6. Events\n219\t\n220\t### Common rules\n221\t\n222\t**Flow.** Intro Card (name, one sentence, one diagram, this device's prompts; Use to begin) → instructor counts 3-2-1 → play → whistle or bell → Results (ink-stamp medal, one big number, one line of stats, a line of dialogue, retry or continue) → marbles in the programme.\n223\t\n224\t**Lanes.** Every event is written for an array of lanes from the first line: `for (const lane of this.lanes)`. A lane is `{ id, kind: 'local' | 'bot' | 'ghost', input, doll, balancer, props, score, name, colour }`. Solo is one lane. Group Class is up to four local lanes. A hub session is one local lane plus ghost lanes. Attract mode uses bot lanes. Lanes sit 3.2 m apart on x. Each has its own collision bits. Ghost lanes have no physics at all (section 12). The camera frames all lanes in Group Class, and your own lane with neighbours partly visible in a session. Field of view 38° ✔ (the look tests used it). Pull back to fit lanes: `d = max(base, (spread / 2 + 2.5) / tan(hfov / 2))`. Chase camera: about (0, 3.4, 9) behind the doll, damped at 6 /s.\n225\t\n226\t**Each event provides:** `buildSet()`, `start()`, `fixed(dt)`, `frame(dt)`, `laneResult(i)`, `botInput(i, t, skill)`, `dispose()`, plus static `id, title, discipline, instructor, tiers, couch, session, sessionConfig`. A fresh Rapier world per event, freed in `dispose()`: no state leaks. Gameplay randomness comes only from `rng('event-id')` (section 16).\n227\t\n228\t**Non-standing poses reuse the balance core:** in Plank & Perch, Still Water and Tug of Peace the core stays upright and invisible, and the visible doll plays the pose and rocks with it. Nothing in the rules knows the difference.\n229\t\n230\t### STILLNESS, with Ms. Stillwater\n231\t\n232\t**Poses** (Sun Deck, a round teak deck on stilts over the water; low three-quarter camera, heron beside you)\n233\t- *Play:* The heron demonstrates, then the target ring glides from centre to the pose's spot on the disc (3 s). Follow it, then hold while the ring shrinks. Breath ring runs throughout.\n234\t- *Tiers ✔:* maxLean 14°, margin 0.45. Tier 1: 3 poses, hold 8 s, ring 0.30→0.16, sway 0.08→0.22, tremor 0.08→0.20. Tier 2: 4 poses, hold 10 s, ring 0.30→0.13, sway 0.12→0.30, tremor 0.10→0.30. Tier 3: 5 poses, hold 12 s, ring 0.30→0.10, sway 0.15→0.38, tremor 0.15→0.40.\n235\t- *Scoring ✔:* each tick in the ring earns `0.30 / ringRadius`; form is the share of the maximum, capped at 100. Without breath: Tier 1 95 / 66 / 54, Tier 2 74 / 58 / 46, Tier 3 55 / 41 / 33. In sync throughout: Tier 1 100 / 91 / 78, Tier 2 100 / 85 / 71, Tier 3 96 / 62 / 57. Tiers 2 and 3 are hard on purpose, and breath is how you get there.\n236\t- *Pose table* (`src/data/poses.js`, spot on the disc): The Contented Heron (−.35, 0), The Apologetic Pine (0, .4), Half-Folded Deck Chair (0, −.35), The Lighthouse Keeper (.4, 0), Startled Flamingo (.35, .2), The Long Goodbye (−.2, .45), Someone Else's Umbrella (.45, −.1), The Quiet Regret (−.4, −.2). All standing poses.\n237\t- *Bot:* follow the ring with a delayed correction; breathe in sync.\n238\t\n239\t**The Cairn** (Quarry Terrace, pale stone over the cliff; side three-quarter camera)\n240\t- *Play:* You hold a slab overhead. Ms. Stillwater lowers stones onto the stack with her beak. Keep the slab level as the stack gets taller and more alive. Then a final hold.\n241\t- *Physics ✔:* margin 0.8, ankleMax 6, solver iterations 10. Slab: cuboid (.55, .03, .55) on the doll's body at local y .95, friction 1, mass .05. Stones: **cuboid collider** half-extents (r×.8, hh, r×.8), mass .02, friction .95, restitution 0, linear damping .1, angular damping .6. Sizes (r, hh) from the bottom: (.26,.09) (.24,.085) (.22,.08) (.2,.078) (.18,.074) (.165,.07) (.15,.066) (.14,.062) (.125,.058) (.11,.054). Release each stone 1.2 cm above the current top stone's centre with a small random offset.\n242\t- *Tiers ✔:* Tier 1: 6 stones every 5.5 s, hold 8 s, sway .1→.3, tremor .05→.15, offset ±1.5 cm. Every bot finished with all six up: it teaches. Tier 2: 8 stones every 5 s, hold 10 s, sway .15→.4, tremor .08→.2, offset ±2 cm: 5.3 / 4.8 / 2 stones left without breath, 8 / 8 / 6.3 in sync. Tier 3: 10 **wider** stones, (.28,.09) (.27,.085) (.26,.08) (.25,.078) (.24,.074) (.23,.07) (.22,.066) (.21,.062) (.2,.058) (.19,.054), every 4.5 s, hold 10 s, sway .15→.4, tremor .08→.2, offset ±2 cm: 1.3 / 1 / 2 without breath, 10 / 5.8 / 1.5 in sync. With the normal stones and more sway, Tier 3 collapsed whatever you did, breath or not: unfair, so don't use that.\n243\t- *Scoring:* 100 per stone on the slab at the end + height bonus + steadiness. Dropped stones clatter and the next one still comes.\n244\t- *Bot:* correct the lean with gain 0.7, delay 0.16 s. Do not use high gains: a delayed correction with gain 1.4 oscillates and tips under this load ✔.\n245\t\n246\t**Still Water** (Lily Pond; high three-quarter camera over the water)\n247\t- *Play:* Sit on a lily pad for 60 s. Stay in the small calm zone (r .18). Calm fills a meter, and creatures arrive at thresholds: dragonfly on your nose, frog on your head, koi circling, turtle, and at full calm Ms. Stillwater tiptoes over and pats your head. Move too much and they scatter. Ripples spread from the pad in proportion to how much you move: this is a beautiful TSL shader on the pond. Distractions: Trudy whispering (\"Don't think about your left knee\"), a sneeze (telegraphed \"Ah... ah...\": press Flail to stifle it, or take a big shove).\n248\t- *Tiers ✔, 60 s:* calm zone r .15, .12, .10; sway .07→.18, .1→.22, .12→.26; tremor .05; 2, 3, 4 sneezes (1.2 s warning; a missed one is a 2.2 N·m shove for .3 s). Calm rises 1/30 per s in the zone and falls 1/8 per s outside it, and creatures arrive at .2, .4, .6, .8, 1. Time in zone: Tier 1 100% / 95% / 80% (peak calm 1 / 1 / .67), Tier 2 86 / 71 / 48, Tier 3 62 / 49 / 31.\n249\t- *Scoring:* form = mean of (share of time in the zone, calm at the end) ✔ Tier 1 100 / 89 / 48. *Bot:* hold centre, stifle sneezes.\n250\t\n251\t### STRENGTH, with Coach Bolster\n252\t\n253\t**Log Reps** (The Yard; front three-quarter camera, Coach beside you shouting encouragement)\n254\t- *Play:* A log across your shoulders. A tempo ribbon moves down 2 s, pauses 0.5 s, up 2 s, rests 0.5 s. Squeeze both triggers to sink (depth = average of L2 and R2; on keyboard and mouse, holding both ramps depth down at the tempo's speed and releasing ramps up). Stay in the ribbon's band and keep the dot centred. Fatigue grows each rep: depth shake from .015 to .07, a narrower band (half-width .16→.10), stronger rumble.\n255\t- *Tiers ✔:* margin .8, ankleMax 6, dot zone r .30. 6, 9, 12 reps, with sway .08→.22, .12→.3, .15→.38 and tremor .04→.12, .06→.16, .08→.2. Coach's gag at the end: \"That's the last one! ...THIS one's the last one.\" Two bonus reps worth double.\n256\t- *Scoring ✔:* a rep scores (share of time in the band) × (.5 + .5 × share of time in the zone). Form is the mean: 97 / 77 / 50 on every tier, assuming tracking error .05 / .12 / .2. Reps alone don't make it harder, so tighten the band by .02 on Tier 2 and .04 on Tier 3. (My first band, .24→.15 with a zone of .45, scored everyone above 90.)\n257\t- *The log is a rule, not a contact ✔.* Friction doesn't work here. At normal friction the log barely moves (.03 m at 16° of lean in 6 s). At very low friction it is past .5 m within 1.3-2 s at any lean. There is no useful middle. So attach the log to the shoulders and keep a number: `offset` grows by .05 m/s per degree of sideways lean past 6°, and comes back when you lean the other way. It adds `.06 × 9.81 × offset` to `nx`, so it feeds on itself. At .5 m it becomes a dynamic body and falls, Coach catches it with a flipper and hands it back, and that rep is lost.\n258\t- *Bot:* follow the ribbon with a delay.\n259\t\n260\t**Plank & Perch** (The Beam, on the pier; side camera)\n261\t- *Play:* Hold a plank pose on a low beam. Gary the gull and friends land on your head, back and feet. Each adds a steady push on the dot. Counter it with your weight. Flail shakes them all off for a form penalty and a look from Coach. More than 2 s outside the zone: belly flop into the sand, comedic, resume.\n262\t- *Tiers ✔:* zone r .28, sway .1. 30 s with a gull every 5 s, pushes .2-.4 N·m. 45 s, every 4.5 s, same pushes. 60 s, every 4 s, pushes .25-.45. Each gull lands on the same side as the last 70% of the time, or the pushes cancel out and it's too easy. Time in zone: Tier 1 80% / 62% / 53%, Tier 2 62 / 51 / 53, Tier 3 54 / 48 / 50, with up to two falls. Tiers 2 and 3 separate players less, because a flail resets everything. Consider a stiffer flail penalty there.\n263\t- *Scoring:* time in zone + a bonus per gull still on you at the end. Being covered in gulls is a win condition. Set `par` near 85, 65, 58. *Bot:* counter the net push, flail after 1.2 s outside at a big lean.\n264\t\n265\t**Tug of Peace** (The Beach; side camera; versus)\n266\t- *Play:* Best of three pulls. A caller shouts \"Heave\" every 1.2 s. On the call, squeeze both feet and lean back (down on the stick). Lean back too far when the other side eases off and you sit down hard (ragdoll, rope leaps .3 against you). Solo: you and Retired Kevin against Both Sandras. Group Class: seats 1 and 3 against 2 and 4, or 1 against 2.\n267\t- *Rules ✔:* The rope is one number from −1 to 1 on a line with sand markings. The rope pulls you forward: `extZ = .1 + .3 × theirPower`. (At my first .5 + .8 × theirPower, the pull cancelled all your lean and nobody could ever win.) Your power = timing × share, where timing = `1 − |error| / .3 s` and share = your actual backward lean / maxLean. Each call moves the rope by `(yours − theirs) × .33`. Their power is .35, .5, .54 by tier, ±20%. On Tiers 2 and 3 they ease off every 12 s or 9 s (roughly): tension drops to 0 for .8 s, and if your share is above .72 at that moment you get 3.2 N·m backward for .5 s, which sits you down unless you flail.\n268\t- *What that gives ✔:* Tier 1: everyone wins with the stick at .9, in 25-48 s. Tier 2: .85 is safe but slow (wins 100% / 88% / 0%); full stick also wins, with 3-4 falls. Tier 3: .85 only wins for the sharp bot; .95 wins 100% / 88% / 0%; full stick means 6-7 falls, and only the sharp bot still wins. So the choice of how far to lean is the game.\n269\t- *Scoring:* form = 40 + 30 per pull won, minus 5 per fall. *Bot:* hit the call with error, stick at .9. Not in the hub's set.\n270\t\n271\t### CARDIO, with Coach Bolster and the class\n272\t\n273\t**Step Class** (The Bandstand at dusk; front camera, class of eight dolls behind you following your moves)\n274\t- *Play:* Footprints slide along the deck toward your feet: left, right, both, and lean ribbons you hold for a beat. The class gets more in sync as your combo grows. At 16 the spotlights swing and confetti falls. A miss makes someone in the class trip.\n275\t- *Windows (starting values):* Perfect ±45 ms, Good ±90, OK ±135. Audio-offset setting in Settings. Step timing comes from `AudioContext.currentTime` minus `outputLatency` when audio runs, otherwise the sim clock. Foot clicks are on the SFX bus: **music is only 10% by default, so the beat must be readable without it.** Every note also has a visual pulse on the beat.\n276\t- *Chart format* (`src/data/charts.js`): bars of 8 cells. `L R B` feet, `< > ^ v` lean, `.` rest. Patterns: `L...R...`, `L.R.L.R.`, `L.R.B...`, `L.L.R.R.`, `<...>...`, `L.R.<...`, `^...v...`, `LRL.RLR.`, `B.B.LRB.`, `L.R.LRLR`. Form: intro 4 bars (empty), A 8, B 8, A′ 8, break 4 (lean only), B′ 8, outro 4. Tier 1: first three patterns plus `<...>...` at 104 BPM. Tier 2: adds the next three at 116. Tier 3: all at 124. Session version: 24 bars at 116.\n277\t- *Scoring ✔:* Perfect 1, Good .7, OK .4; form is the share of the maximum. With timing error sd 28 / 55 / 95 ms and a small miss rate that grows by tier: Tier 1 96 / 82 / 62 (106 notes in 102 s), Tier 2 96 / 81 / 58 (108 in 91 s), Tier 3 96 / 77 / 54 (126 in 85 s). The session version takes 49.7 s and fits its 50 s slot.\n278\t- *Bot:* hits every note with its skill's timing error.\n279\t\n280\t**Plate Spinners** (The Circus Green; high front camera)\n281\t- *Play:* You stand in a ring of poles with spinning plates on them and a big hoop around your waist. Circle the stick (or the mouse, or roll W-D-S-A) to drive the hoop. Plates need speed: Large 3.5-5 rad/s, Medium 6.5-8, Small 9.5-11, with dead zones between. A plate gains energy only while the hoop is in its band, and everything else decays. You keep them alive by changing speed. Neglected plates wobble wider and wider, then fall, smash into six Rapier shards with a clatter, and return after 6 s.\n282\t- *Measure:* hoop ω = smoothed angular velocity of the weight vector when |weight| > 0.5; otherwise it decays. **The hoop has inertia,** easing to that speed with τ = .5 s, so changing speed takes time. A colour ring around your waist shows the current band. Each plate has a big arc gauge. No falling in this event.\n283\t- *Tiers ✔, 60 s:* start energy .7, gain .45 per s. Tier 1: L M S, decay .11. Tier 2: L M M S, decay .13. Tier 3: L L M M S S, decay .16. Share of plate-time alive: Tier 1 100% / 87% / 73%, Tier 2 90 / 80 / 67, Tier 3 79 / 70 / 61. Crashes a minute: 0 / 4 / 8 on Tier 1, more above. (My first version, wide bands and slow decay with no hoop inertia, gave every bot 100% and no crashes, so nobody could lose.)\n284\t- *Scoring:* form = `200 × share − 100`, floored at 0 ✔ Tier 1 100 / 74 / 46. *Bot:* pick the band of the lowest plate and rotate at the band's middle, with its skill's reaction time and speed error.\n285\t\n286\t**Island Loop** (the island's ring road; chase camera)\n287\t- *Play:* Jog two laps of the ring road (r 19, 119 m a lap, about 34 s at the target 3.5 m/s). Alternate feet to run; speed = 1.4 m per step, so the target is 2.5 steps a second with a band of ±15%. A pace band (not too fast, not too slow) follows Retired Kevin, who runs ahead. The stick steers. Shells on the path. The island tilts under your weight as usual, so uphill and downhill are real. Trudy rides on your shoulder and asks questions at the worst time.\n288\t- *Scoring:* time in the band + shells. *Bot:* steady cadence, steer the racing line. Solo only.\n289\t\n290\t### BALANCE PLAY, with Trudy\n291\t\n292\t**Fruit Hat** (end of the pier; barge offshore firing fruit; camera low from the sea side) — **build this event first. It is the game's soul and the proof of the model.**\n293\t- *Play:* You wear a wide basket hat. A barge fires fruit in high arcs. A shadow ring marks each landing spot. Lean to put the basket under it. **Fruit in the hat is not yet yours and it makes you top-heavy. Press Hop to toss everything into the crate at your feet:** that banks it, resets the load, and takes 1 s. Push your luck: more fruit before banking means a multiplier, but you wobble worse and may spill it all. A fall spills everything unbanked. Spiky urchins knock the basket and cost points. Old boots are heavy. A golden pineapple is worth five and very heavy. Flail shakes off whatever is on top.\n294\t- *Physics ✔:* Basket on the doll's body: cylinder (half-height .02, radius .36) at local y .75, mass .01, friction .9, plus 8 rim cuboids (.02, .1, .16) at radius .36, local y .85, mass .001. Fruit: ball radius .075, friction .8, restitution .1, CCD on. Fly 1.5 s from 7 m out to height 1.75 at the landing spot.\n295\t- *Tiers ✔, 60 s:* Tier 1: landing radius .45, one fruit per 1.6 s, mass .015, ankleMax 4.6, margin .45. Tier 2: radius .55, per 1.3 s, mass .012, ankleMax 5, margin .6. Tier 3: radius .65, per 1.0 s, same body.\n296\t- *What I measured on Tier 1 ✔:* a sharp bot banking at 7 banks about 28 fruit with no falls. **The same bot never banking falls twice a minute.** A clumsy bot banking at 5 banks 25-30 with no falls; banking at 9 it sometimes falls. That is the whole design working. Lean only for a fruit in its last 0.95 s of flight: chasing every fruit at full lean makes you whip and fall.\n297\t- *Scoring:* bank value × (1 + 0.1 × (fruit in that bank − 1)), so bigger banks pay more. Unbanked fruit at the whistle counts half. *Bot:* as measured, with `skill` setting lag and noise.\n298\t\n299\t**Room Service** (the long boardwalk to the guest gazebo; chase camera from behind and above)\n300\t- *Play:* Carry a tray of tumblers along a narrow boardwalk. **Alternate feet to step** (same foot twice is a stumble). Each step rocks you a little. Leaning steers sideways too. Wind gusts are telegraphed for a full second by ink streaks and a rising sound, **and the Balance Disc shows a green zone where to lean.** Gary swoops for a glass on Tier 2+. Wobbly planks on Tier 2+. Off the edge: splash, respawn at the last buoy with one fewer glass. Deliver what is left to the table. The lesson the game teaches: **small early leans keep the glasses, sudden big ones throw them.**\n301\t- *Physics ✔:* margin .8, ankleMax 6, **reflex 5** (slower than the default, so moves are smooth). Tray: cuboid (.3, .02, .3) at local (0, .5, .3), mass .04, friction 1, on the doll's body. Five tumblers: cylinder half-height .06, radius .05, mass .012, friction .9, restitution 0, angular damping .5, linear damping .1. Foot: steps set a target speed of .55 m / .42 s, the foot low-passes to it with τ = .5 s, and it stops when no step has come for 1.6 cadences. Each step adds a rock of .2 N·m for .18 s to `nx`, and moves you sideways by `leanX × .25 × .55`. The boardwalk is ±.8 wide.\n302\t- *Tiers ✔:* 30, 45, 60 m (24, 35, 47 s at a steady pace). Gusts 1.2, 2.0, 2.3 N·m for .5 s, about every 7, 6, 5 s. Glasses delivered of 5 with a brace of `.2 × gust` in stick units: Tier 1 5 / 5 / 5, Tier 2 3.8 / 4 / 4.5, Tier 3 2 / 4 / 3.5. With no brace: Tier 1 3.5 / 2.5 / 4, Tiers 2 and 3 none. **Gusts of 2.8 and up emptied the tray at every brace I tried, and over-bracing spills too:** a .6 brace on a 2 N·m gust leaves 2-4.8. No bot fell at these gusts. Do not raise them much.\n303\t- *Scoring:* 100 per glass + time bonus. *Bot:* steady steps, keep centred, brace at `.2 × gust` on the telegraph.\n304\t\n305\t**Springboard** (the toy-block dive tower at the end of the pier; side camera, slow-mo side replay)\n306\t- *Play:* Pump the board by squeezing both feet as it goes down. Press Hop near the top to launch. In the air the stick rotates you, holding Hop tucks and spins you faster. Hit the water. **Two ways to win:** a clean entry (head or feet down within 12°) makes barely a ripple and earns Grace. A perfect belly flop (front flat within 20°) sends up a giant column of water, knocks Gary out of the sky, and earns Splash. Score is the better of the two times height. Anything else is \"Technically a Dive.\"\n307\t- *Board ✔:* an analytic spring, not Rapier: `y'' = −ω²y − 2ζωy' − (pumping ? 8 : 0)`, with ω = 2π / .9 s, ζ = .035, starting from a small bounce. Upward speed after 3 / 5 / 7 s of pumping: 4.7 / 6.5 / 7.6 m/s with good timing, 3.6 / 4.6 / 5.2 a little late. A quarter period late (.26 s), it barely moves (1.5 m/s). Pumping by reaction doesn't work, so give it a visible rhythm and a click. Launch speed = `max(3, upward speed at the press)`, so nobody is stuck.\n308\t- *Air ✔:* air time 1.5 s from a 5 m tower at 4 m/s, 2 s at 7 m/s, 2.3 s from 9 m at 7 m/s. The stick sets a target spin up to 3.4 rad/s (195°/s), reached at 9 /s; tuck multiplies it by 1.8. Free the balance core in the air: remove the joint and apply this as torque. Hit chance if you stop by eye with a timing error of 80 / 150 / 250 ms: Grace 56% / 32% / 19% at full stick, 83 / 53 / 33 at half stick, 33 / 18 / 12 tucked; Splash 80 / 51 / 32, 98 / 78 / 53, 52 / 29 / 18. So slow spins are safer, and tucks are for showing off.\n309\t- *Tiers and scoring:* three dives, best two count. Towers 5, 7, 9 m. Grace = `100 × (1 − error / 12°)`, Splash = `100 × (1 − error / 20°)`, each × `(1 + (height − 5) / 10)`.\n310\t- *On entry:* swap to the ragdoll with a simple upward force below y = 0. Keep a 2 s ring buffer of the doll's transform and replay the best flop at ¼ speed.\n311\t- *Bot:* pump in phase, then pick a flop or a dive. Good attract-mode comedy.\n312\t\n313\t**Tippy Raft** (a round raft at sea; high wide camera; party event) — the one event where players physically fight, and Group Class's reason to exist.\n314\t- *Play:* 2-4 dolls on a raft. They walk with the stick. Flail shoves in a short cone. Hop hops. The raft tilts toward the sum of everyone's weight using the island's model (below), so everyone is on the same seesaw. **Swells** rock it from random directions, each warned by a rising wave. Sections float away over time. Off the edge: splash, out. Last doll standing wins the round; best of three. Solo in Free Play against three bots.\n315\t- *Numbers ✔:* radius 6, losing 1 m every 12 s down to 2. Tilt model K 6, C 3.5, 14° for one doll at the rim, clamped to 26°. Slope push `tilt × 10`. Walk 3 m/s. Swell about every 6 s: adds 2.5 rad/s to the tilt velocity. Shove: within 1.3 m, gives the target 6 m/s, decaying at 3 /s, with a 2 s cooldown. Over 16 rounds with one sharp, two average and one clumsy bot: rounds lasted 10-55 s, 29 s on average; wins 6 / 8 (between two bots) / 2. So skill helps and doesn't decide it. (Without swells and at a slow shrink, rounds never ended: everyone just stands in the middle.)\n316\t- *Not in the hub's set.* It needs a shared world, and the session is ghost mode. *Bot:* stay toward the centre and the high side, go at whoever is close, shove when in reach.\n317\t\n318\t### THE FINALE\n319\t\n320\t**The Grand Hold** (lighthouse gallery at sunset; camera circles slowly, the whole cast and island below)\n321\t- *Play:* Your balance is now tied directly to the island: your lean tips everything below you. Four 20-second phases, each a remix: a Pose with breath; four stones on your head; storm gusts with Gary; a short Step Class in the wind with the class cheering from below. Then the last hold: 15 s, the ring shrinking to the centre, sway fading as you succeed, the final notch coming up.\n322\t- *Rules:* It cannot be failed. A fall means the class catches you with a stretched bedsheet and the phase restarts. The ending plays on completion. Medals are given anyway (\"Exemplary, I suppose\").\n323\t\n324\t### Island physics (hub, Island Loop, Tippy Raft, finale) ✔\n325\t\n326\t**Do not move the island. Tilt gravity.** Rapier sees a fixed flat disc; the visual island group rotates; gravity is set to match each step. All the physics stays in island-local coordinates, with no moving-platform problems to solve. Up to 11° of tilt, a walker's height stayed within 0.004 m, and 60 props slid to the low side with none lost. (A kinematic platform also worked, but it is more to manage: I only took it to 1.8° and props rolled off.)\n327\t\n328\t```js\n329\t// tx, tz = tilt in radians, toward +x and +z (that side goes down). dt = 1/120.\n330\tvx += (K*restX + W*p.x - K*tx - C*vx) * dt;  tx += vx * dt;   // p = walker position; K 2.2, C 1.8\n331\tvz += (K*restZ + W*p.z - K*tz - C*vz) * dt;  tz += vz * dt;   // W = K * rad(4) / 22 → 4° at the rim\n332\tworld.gravity = { x: 9.81*Math.sin(tx), y: -9.81*Math.cos(Math.hypot(tx,tz)), z: 9.81*Math.sin(tz) };\n333\tconst a = Math.hypot(tx,tz) || 1e-9, s = Math.sin(a/2);\n334\tislandGroup.quaternion.set(tz/a*s, 0, -tx/a*s, Math.cos(a/2));     // +x side down is about -z\n335\t```\n336\t- Everything on the island is a child of `islandGroup`, including the player and props, using island-local coordinates. Keep the doll visually upright by counter-rotating most of the tilt.\n337\t- Disc collider radius 22, friction .9. A low toy fence around the rim, physically 40 cuboids of half-size (.15, 1, 1.9), keeps props on. Props pile up against the fence on the low side: this is a good joke, so leave it.\n338\t- Walker ✔: capsule (.5, .3), mass 1, `lockRotations()`, friction 0 with Min combine rule, `setCanSleep(false)`. Each step `setLinvel(wantedX + tx*3, v.y, wantedZ + tz*3)`, so the slope pushes you a little. Walk speed 4 m/s.\n339\t- Props must be `setCanSleep(false)`, or they will not notice gravity changing.\n340\t- The sea and horizon stay level; only the island tilts. The level horizon against the crooked lighthouse is what sells it.\n341\t\n342\t---\n343\t\n344\t## 7. Screens\n345\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"7eed2c12-4fe6-43cb-a39a-45aeec978848","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:30:04.580Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work/PLAN.md","content":"## 5. Programme and progress\n\n**Days are game days, not calendar days.** A day ends when you go to bed. The real date is only used for absence jokes and calendar stamps.\n\n| Day | Name | Check-In tests | Classes | What it teaches |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Arrival | Stand Still, Lean Match, Step Time | Poses, Fruit Hat | Lean, flail, breath, the jar |\n| 2 | The Second Day, Which Is Harder | Stand Still, Lean Match, Quick Lean | Log Reps, Step Class, The Cairn | Feet, rhythm, stacking |\n| 3 | Service With a Wobble | One-Foot Hold, Lean Match, Step Time | Room Service, Plate Spinners, Plank & Perch | Stepping, stick circles. The Mirror opens |\n| 4 | A Little Damp | Quick Lean, One-Foot Hold, Stand Still | Springboard, Still Water, Island Loop | Air control. Group Class invitation |\n| 5 | Level Day | Your three best tests | Tippy Raft, Tug of Peace, The Grand Hold | The ending |\n| 6+ | Maintenance | Random three | Trudy's random plan of three | Free Play continues forever |\n\nBuild the day list from the event registry: an event that was cut (section 18) is skipped and everything still works.\n\n**A day's flow:** morning line → personal question (Yes/No) → Check-In → Wobble Age reveal → Today's Plan (cards in any order: walk there or pick a card to go directly) → each class → Results → marbles → the island comes up a notch → extra Free Play if wanted → the bed hut. Press Use, the screen inks out, a stamp lands on the calendar, the next dawn. Palettes follow the day's steps (dawn, noon, golden, dusk), unless an event pins its own (Step Class at dusk, The Grand Hold at sunset).\n\n**The Check-In** (about 90 s) takes place on a round mat with Trudy. Three tests, each one screen with one big instruction. Each scores 0 to 1 (clamped):\n- **Stand Still** (10 s, sway .1, tremor .06): keep the dot in the centre. Score `1 − meanDistance / .25` ✔ .82 / .74 / .49.\n- **Lean Match** (18 s, sway .04): \"Lean 70% left.\" A bar shows your percentage. Three targets, 6 s each, ignoring the first 1.5 s of each. Score `1 − meanError / .4` ✔ .58 / .45 / .27. (My first version, hold within ±5% for 2 s, was impossible: every bot timed out.)\n- **Step Time** (16 s): alternate feet with a metronome at 96 BPM. Score `1 − meanError / 120 ms` ✔ .81 / .63 / .37.\n- **Quick Lean** (15 s): lean toward the lit quadrant, six prompts. Score `1 − (meanReaction − .35 s) / .9 s` ✔ .87 / .69 / .44.\n- **One-Foot Hold** (12 s): keep R2 down and L2 up, which halves `margin`, while keeping the dot centred. Score as Stand Still ✔ .55 / .48 / .40.\n\n**Wobble Age.** `age = round(18 + 81 × (1 − mean)^1.3 + handicap)`, clamped to 18..99. The handicap is a comic head start that shrinks each day: +24, +16, +8, +3, then 0. So the number always falls, and that is what makes coming back feel good. With unchanged skill, Day 1 to Day 5 ✔: 56→32 / 66→42 / 86→62. The reveal is the comic set piece: lights dim, drum roll, numbers spin on a wooden counter, sting, Trudy's line from a band of lines by age. Stored per day and drawn as the Journal's graph. It never asks your real age (\"I didn't ask how old you are. I asked your knees.\").\n\n**Marbles.** After each class: 2 per minute of active play (min 2) + medal bonus (1, 2, 3, 5) + 2 for a first clear. Each Check-In gives 1. They pour physically into the jar on the island, as real Rapier balls: a 200-marble pour runs fine (✔ 0.23 ms per step; 500 is 0.67 ms). Marble colour shows the medal. Thresholds unlock hats and paints (`src/data/unlocks.js`: 6, 14, 24, 36, 50, 60 marbles). A full programme pays about 15, 37, 59, 81, 103 by the end of each day, or 61 in total with all Tier B events cut, so everything unlocks either way ✔.\n\n**The island's tilt depends on progress, not skill.** Rest tilt = 9° × (1 − completed programme steps / total steps), so nobody can be stuck. Each step lowers it with a long wooden creak, all loose props shuffling and Trudy reacting. The last notch happens inside The Grand Hold.\n\n**Medals** come from form (0 to 100): ≥40 Adequate, ≥65 Pleasant, ≥85 Exemplary, ≥97 Suspicious. Below 40 is still a pass (\"Attended\"). Events that measure a share of the maximum (Poses, Still Water) use it directly. All others set a `par` so that form = 100 × raw/par, per tier, so the sharp bot lands 88 to 95 (section 17).\n\n**Tiers.** Every event has three: Gentle, Honest, Unreasonable. Tier 2 opens with a Pleasant on Tier 1, Tier 3 with an Exemplary on Tier 2. Days 1 to 5 use Tier 1. Maintenance days use the highest tier opened. Free Play offers all open tiers.\n\n**Free Play** opens each event once it has been met in the programme. Walk to its pavilion, or use the menu.\n\n**The ending.** The Grand Hold (section 6) ends with the island level. Trudy's bubble centres for the first time. She has only ever seen the world at nine degrees. \"Oh. So that's what the middle looks like.\" Silence, then the marbles flare, the lighthouse lights, and the credits roll over a slow pan of everything you straightened. Then: \"It's quite flat, isn't it? ...Same time tomorrow?\" Save the \"levelled\" state, unlock Free Play for everything, and carry on into Maintenance days.\n\n**Profiles.** Up to four, each with a name, paint, hat, days, ages, medals, marbles and counts. Name is a spinner over generated names (no text entry; `src/data/names.js`). Save JSON in `localStorage` with a version number, read inside try/catch, and fall back to memory if storage is unavailable.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Events\n\n### Common rules\n\n**Flow.** Intro Card (name, one sentence, one diagram, this device's prompts; Use to begin) → instructor counts 3-2-1 → play → whistle or bell → Results (ink-stamp medal, one big number, one line of stats, a line of dialogue, retry or continue) → marbles in the programme.\n\n**Lanes.** Every event is written for an array of lanes from the first line: `for (const lane of this.lanes)`. A lane is `{ id, kind: 'local' | 'bot' | 'ghost', input, doll, balancer, props, score, name, colour }`. Solo is one lane. Group Class is up to four local lanes. A hub session is one local lane plus ghost lanes. Attract mode uses bot lanes. Lanes sit 3.2 m apart on x. Each has its own collision bits. Ghost lanes have no physics at all (section 12). The camera frames all lanes in Group Class, and your own lane with neighbours partly visible in a session. Field of view 38° ✔ (the look tests used it). Pull back to fit lanes: `d = max(base, (spread / 2 + 2.5) / tan(hfov / 2))`. Chase camera: about (0, 3.4, 9) behind the doll, damped at 6 /s.\n\n**Each event provides:** `buildSet()`, `start()`, `fixed(dt)`, `frame(dt)`, `laneResult(i)`, `botInput(i, t, skill)`, `dispose()`, plus static `id, title, discipline, instructor, tiers, couch, session, sessionConfig`. A fresh Rapier world per event, freed in `dispose()`: no state leaks. Gameplay randomness comes only from `rng('event-id')` (section 16).\n\n**Non-standing poses reuse the balance core:** in Plank & Perch, Still Water and Tug of Peace the core stays upright and invisible, and the visible doll plays the pose and rocks with it. Nothing in the rules knows the difference.\n\n### STILLNESS, with Ms. Stillwater\n\n**Poses** (Sun Deck, a round teak deck on stilts over the water; low three-quarter camera, heron beside you)\n- *Play:* The heron demonstrates, then the target ring glides from centre to the pose's spot on the disc (3 s). Follow it, then hold while the ring shrinks. Breath ring runs throughout.\n- *Tiers ✔:* maxLean 14°, margin 0.45. Tier 1: 3 poses, hold 8 s, ring 0.30→0.16, sway 0.08→0.22, tremor 0.08→0.20. Tier 2: 4 poses, hold 10 s, ring 0.30→0.13, sway 0.12→0.30, tremor 0.10→0.30. Tier 3: 5 poses, hold 12 s, ring 0.30→0.10, sway 0.15→0.38, tremor 0.15→0.40.\n- *Scoring ✔:* each tick in the ring earns `0.30 / ringRadius`; form is the share of the maximum, capped at 100. Without breath: Tier 1 95 / 66 / 54, Tier 2 74 / 58 / 46, Tier 3 55 / 41 / 33. In sync throughout: Tier 1 100 / 91 / 78, Tier 2 100 / 85 / 71, Tier 3 96 / 62 / 57. Tiers 2 and 3 are hard on purpose, and breath is how you get there.\n- *Pose table* (`src/data/poses.js`, spot on the disc): The Contented Heron (−.35, 0), The Apologetic Pine (0, .4), Half-Folded Deck Chair (0, −.35), The Lighthouse Keeper (.4, 0), Startled Flamingo (.35, .2), The Long Goodbye (−.2, .45), Someone Else's Umbrella (.45, −.1), The Quiet Regret (−.4, −.2). All standing poses.\n- *Bot:* follow the ring with a delayed correction; breathe in sync.\n\n**The Cairn** (Quarry Terrace, pale stone over the cliff; side three-quarter camera)\n- *Play:* You hold a slab overhead. Ms. Stillwater lowers stones onto the stack with her beak. Keep the slab level as the stack gets taller and more alive. Then a final hold.\n- *Physics ✔:* margin 0.8, ankleMax 6, solver iterations 10. Slab: cuboid (.55, .03, .55) on the doll's body at local y .95, friction 1, mass .05. Stones: **cuboid collider** half-extents (r×.8, hh, r×.8), mass .02, friction .95, restitution 0, linear damping .1, angular damping .6. Sizes (r, hh) from the bottom: (.26,.09) (.24,.085) (.22,.08) (.2,.078) (.18,.074) (.165,.07) (.15,.066) (.14,.062) (.125,.058) (.11,.054). Release each stone 1.2 cm above the current top stone's centre with a small random offset.\n- *Tiers ✔:* Tier 1: 6 stones every 5.5 s, hold 8 s, sway .1→.3, tremor .05→.15, offset ±1.5 cm. Every bot finished with all six up: it teaches. Tier 2: 8 stones every 5 s, hold 10 s, sway .15→.4, tremor .08→.2, offset ±2 cm: 5.3 / 4.8 / 2 stones left without breath, 8 / 8 / 6.3 in sync. Tier 3: 10 **wider** stones, (.28,.09) (.27,.085) (.26,.08) (.25,.078) (.24,.074) (.23,.07) (.22,.066) (.21,.062) (.2,.058) (.19,.054), every 4.5 s, hold 10 s, sway .15→.4, tremor .08→.2, offset ±2 cm: 1.3 / 1 / 2 without breath, 10 / 5.8 / 1.5 in sync. With the normal stones and more sway, Tier 3 collapsed whatever you did, breath or not: unfair, so don't use that.\n- *Scoring:* 100 per stone on the slab at the end + height bonus + steadiness. Dropped stones clatter and the next one still comes.\n- *Bot:* correct the lean with gain 0.7, delay 0.16 s. Do not use high gains: a delayed correction with gain 1.4 oscillates and tips under this load ✔.\n\n**Still Water** (Lily Pond; high three-quarter camera over the water)\n- *Play:* Sit on a lily pad for 60 s. Stay in the small calm zone (r .18). Calm fills a meter, and creatures arrive at thresholds: dragonfly on your nose, frog on your head, koi circling, turtle, and at full calm Ms. Stillwater tiptoes over and pats your head. Move too much and they scatter. Ripples spread from the pad in proportion to how much you move: this is a beautiful TSL shader on the pond. Distractions: Trudy whispering (\"Don't think about your left knee\"), a sneeze (telegraphed \"Ah... ah...\": press Flail to stifle it, or take a big shove).\n- *Tiers ✔, 60 s:* calm zone r .15, .12, .10; sway .07→.18, .1→.22, .12→.26; tremor .05; 2, 3, 4 sneezes (1.2 s warning; a missed one is a 2.2 N·m shove for .3 s). Calm rises 1/30 per s in the zone and falls 1/8 per s outside it, and creatures arrive at .2, .4, .6, .8, 1. Time in zone: Tier 1 100% / 95% / 80% (peak calm 1 / 1 / .67), Tier 2 86 / 71 / 48, Tier 3 62 / 49 / 31.\n- *Scoring:* form = mean of (share of time in the zone, calm at the end) ✔ Tier 1 100 / 89 / 48. *Bot:* hold centre, stifle sneezes.\n\n### STRENGTH, with Coach Bolster\n\n**Log Reps** (The Yard; front three-quarter camera, Coach beside you shouting encouragement)\n- *Play:* A log across your shoulders. A tempo ribbon moves down 2 s, pauses 0.5 s, up 2 s, rests 0.5 s. Squeeze both triggers to sink (depth = average of L2 and R2; on keyboard and mouse, holding both ramps depth down at the tempo's speed and releasing ramps up). Stay in the ribbon's band and keep the dot centred. Fatigue grows each rep: depth shake from .015 to .07, a narrower band (half-width .16→.10), stronger rumble.\n- *Tiers ✔:* margin .8, ankleMax 6, dot zone r .30. 6, 9, 12 reps, with sway .08→.22, .12→.3, .15→.38 and tremor .04→.12, .06→.16, .08→.2. Coach's gag at the end: \"That's the last one! ...THIS one's the last one.\" Two bonus reps worth double.\n- *Scoring ✔:* a rep scores (share of time in the band) × (.5 + .5 × share of time in the zone). Form is the mean: 97 / 77 / 50 on every tier, assuming tracking error .05 / .12 / .2. Reps alone don't make it harder, so tighten the band by .02 on Tier 2 and .04 on Tier 3. (My first band, .24→.15 with a zone of .45, scored everyone above 90.)\n- *The log is a rule, not a contact ✔.* Friction doesn't work here. At normal friction the log barely moves (.03 m at 16° of lean in 6 s). At very low friction it is past .5 m within 1.3-2 s at any lean. There is no useful middle. So attach the log to the shoulders and keep a number: `offset` grows by .05 m/s per degree of sideways lean past 6°, and comes back when you lean the other way. It adds `.06 × 9.81 × offset` to `nx`, so it feeds on itself. At .5 m it becomes a dynamic body and falls, Coach catches it with a flipper and hands it back, and that rep is lost.\n- *Bot:* follow the ribbon with a delay.\n\n**Plank & Perch** (The Beam, on the pier; side camera)\n- *Play:* Hold a plank pose on a low beam. Gary the gull and friends land on your head, back and feet. Each adds a steady push on the dot. Counter it with your weight. Flail shakes them all off for a form penalty and a look from Coach. More than 2 s outside the zone: belly flop into the sand, comedic, resume.\n- *Tiers ✔:* zone r .28, sway .1. 30 s with a gull every 5 s, pushes .2-.4 N·m. 45 s, every 4.5 s, same pushes. 60 s, every 4 s, pushes .25-.45. Each gull lands on the same side as the last 70% of the time, or the pushes cancel out and it's too easy. Time in zone: Tier 1 80% / 62% / 53%, Tier 2 62 / 51 / 53, Tier 3 54 / 48 / 50, with up to two falls. Tiers 2 and 3 separate players less, because a flail resets everything. Consider a stiffer flail penalty there.\n- *Scoring:* time in zone + a bonus per gull still on you at the end. Being covered in gulls is a win condition. Set `par` near 85, 65, 58. *Bot:* counter the net push, flail after 1.2 s outside at a big lean.\n\n**Tug of Peace** (The Beach; side camera; versus)\n- *Play:* Best of three pulls. A caller shouts \"Heave\" every 1.2 s. On the call, squeeze both feet and lean back (down on the stick). Lean back too far when the other side eases off and you sit down hard (ragdoll, rope leaps .3 against you). Solo: you and Retired Kevin against Both Sandras. Group Class: seats 1 and 3 against 2 and 4, or 1 against 2.\n- *Rules ✔:* The rope is one number from −1 to 1 on a line with sand markings. The rope pulls you forward: `extZ = .1 + .3 × theirPower`. (At my first .5 + .8 × theirPower, the pull cancelled all your lean and nobody could ever win.) Your power = timing × share, where timing = `1 − |error| / .3 s` and share = your actual backward lean / maxLean. Each call moves the rope by `(yours − theirs) × .33`. Their power is .35, .5, .54 by tier, ±20%. On Tiers 2 and 3 they ease off every 12 s or 9 s (roughly): tension drops to 0 for .8 s, and if your share is above .72 at that moment you get 3.2 N·m backward for .5 s, which sits you down unless you flail.\n- *What that gives ✔:* Tier 1: everyone wins with the stick at .9, in 25-48 s. Tier 2: .85 is safe but slow (wins 100% / 88% / 0%); full stick also wins, with 3-4 falls. Tier 3: .85 only wins for the sharp bot; .95 wins 100% / 88% / 0%; full stick means 6-7 falls, and only the sharp bot still wins. So the choice of how far to lean is the game.\n- *Scoring:* form = 40 + 30 per pull won, minus 5 per fall. *Bot:* hit the call with error, stick at .9. Not in the hub's set.\n\n### CARDIO, with Coach Bolster and the class\n\n**Step Class** (The Bandstand at dusk; front camera, class of eight dolls behind you following your moves)\n- *Play:* Footprints slide along the deck toward your feet: left, right, both, and lean ribbons you hold for a beat. The class gets more in sync as your combo grows. At 16 the spotlights swing and confetti falls. A miss makes someone in the class trip.\n- *Windows (starting values):* Perfect ±45 ms, Good ±90, OK ±135. Audio-offset setting in Settings. Step timing comes from `AudioContext.currentTime` minus `outputLatency` when audio runs, otherwise the sim clock. Foot clicks are on the SFX bus: **music is only 10% by default, so the beat must be readable without it.** Every note also has a visual pulse on the beat.\n- *Chart format* (`src/data/charts.js`): bars of 8 cells. `L R B` feet, `< > ^ v` lean, `.` rest. Patterns: `L...R...`, `L.R.L.R.`, `L.R.B...`, `L.L.R.R.`, `<...>...`, `L.R.<...`, `^...v...`, `LRL.RLR.`, `B.B.LRB.`, `L.R.LRLR`. Form: intro 4 bars (empty), A 8, B 8, A′ 8, break 4 (lean only), B′ 8, outro 4. Tier 1: first three patterns plus `<...>...` at 104 BPM. Tier 2: adds the next three at 116. Tier 3: all at 124. Session version: 24 bars at 116.\n- *Scoring ✔:* Perfect 1, Good .7, OK .4; form is the share of the maximum. With timing error sd 28 / 55 / 95 ms and a small miss rate that grows by tier: Tier 1 96 / 82 / 62 (106 notes in 102 s), Tier 2 96 / 81 / 58 (108 in 91 s), Tier 3 96 / 77 / 54 (126 in 85 s). The session version takes 49.7 s and fits its 50 s slot.\n- *Bot:* hits every note with its skill's timing error.\n\n**Plate Spinners** (The Circus Green; high front camera)\n- *Play:* You stand in a ring of poles with spinning plates on them and a big hoop around your waist. Circle the stick (or the mouse, or roll W-D-S-A) to drive the hoop. Plates need speed: Large 3.5-5 rad/s, Medium 6.5-8, Small 9.5-11, with dead zones between. A plate gains energy only while the hoop is in its band, and everything else decays. You keep them alive by changing speed. Neglected plates wobble wider and wider, then fall, smash into six Rapier shards with a clatter, and return after 6 s.\n- *Measure:* hoop ω = smoothed angular velocity of the weight vector when |weight| > 0.5; otherwise it decays. **The hoop has inertia,** easing to that speed with τ = .5 s, so changing speed takes time. A colour ring around your waist shows the current band. Each plate has a big arc gauge. No falling in this event.\n- *Tiers ✔, 60 s:* start energy .7, gain .45 per s. Tier 1: L M S, decay .11. Tier 2: L M M S, decay .13. Tier 3: L L M M S S, decay .16. Share of plate-time alive: Tier 1 100% / 87% / 73%, Tier 2 90 / 80 / 67, Tier 3 79 / 70 / 61. Crashes a minute: 0 / 4 / 8 on Tier 1, more above. (My first version, wide bands and slow decay with no hoop inertia, gave every bot 100% and no crashes, so nobody could lose.)\n- *Scoring:* form = `200 × share − 100`, floored at 0 ✔ Tier 1 100 / 74 / 46. *Bot:* pick the band of the lowest plate and rotate at the band's middle, with its skill's reaction time and speed error.\n\n**Island Loop** (the island's ring road; chase camera)\n- *Play:* Jog two laps of the ring road (r 19, 119 m a lap, about 34 s at the target 3.5 m/s). Alternate feet to run; speed = 1.4 m per step, so the target is 2.5 steps a second with a band of ±15%. A pace band (not too fast, not too slow) follows Retired Kevin, who runs ahead. The stick steers. Shells on the path. The island tilts under your weight as usual, so uphill and downhill are real. Trudy rides on your shoulder and asks questions at the worst time.\n- *Scoring:* time in the band + shells. *Bot:* steady cadence, steer the racing line. Solo only.\n\n### BALANCE PLAY, with Trudy\n\n**Fruit Hat** (end of the pier; barge offshore firing fruit; camera low from the sea side) — **build this event first. It is the game's soul and the proof of the model.**\n- *Play:* You wear a wide basket hat. A barge fires fruit in high arcs. A shadow ring marks each landing spot. Lean to put the basket under it. **Fruit in the hat is not yet yours and it makes you top-heavy. Press Hop to toss everything into the crate at your feet:** that banks it, resets the load, and takes 1 s. Push your luck: more fruit before banking means a multiplier, but you wobble worse and may spill it all. A fall spills everything unbanked. Spiky urchins knock the basket and cost points. Old boots are heavy. A golden pineapple is worth five and very heavy. Flail shakes off whatever is on top.\n- *Physics ✔:* Basket on the doll's body: cylinder (half-height .02, radius .36) at local y .75, mass .01, friction .9, plus 8 rim cuboids (.02, .1, .16) at radius .36, local y .85, mass .001. Fruit: ball radius .075, friction .8, restitution .1, CCD on. Fly 1.5 s from 7 m out to height 1.75 at the landing spot.\n- *Tiers ✔, 60 s:* Tier 1: landing radius .45, one fruit per 1.6 s, mass .015, ankleMax 4.6, margin .45. Tier 2: radius .55, per 1.3 s, mass .012, ankleMax 5, margin .6. Tier 3: radius .65, per 1.0 s, same body.\n- *What I measured on Tier 1 ✔:* a sharp bot banking at 7 banks about 28 fruit with no falls. **The same bot never banking falls twice a minute.** A clumsy bot banking at 5 banks 25-30 with no falls; banking at 9 it sometimes falls. That is the whole design working. Lean only for a fruit in its last 0.95 s of flight: chasing every fruit at full lean makes you whip and fall.\n- *Scoring:* bank value × (1 + 0.1 × (fruit in that bank − 1)), so bigger banks pay more. Unbanked fruit at the whistle counts half. *Bot:* as measured, with `skill` setting lag and noise.\n\n**Room Service** (the long boardwalk to the guest gazebo; chase camera from behind and above)\n- *Play:* Carry a tray of tumblers along a narrow boardwalk. **Alternate feet to step** (same foot twice is a stumble). Each step rocks you a little. Leaning steers sideways too. Wind gusts are telegraphed for a full second by ink streaks and a rising sound, **and the Balance Disc shows a green zone where to lean.** Gary swoops for a glass on Tier 2+. Wobbly planks on Tier 2+. Off the edge: splash, respawn at the last buoy with one fewer glass. Deliver what is left to the table. The lesson the game teaches: **small early leans keep the glasses, sudden big ones throw them.**\n- *Physics ✔:* margin .8, ankleMax 6, **reflex 5** (slower than the default, so moves are smooth). Tray: cuboid (.3, .02, .3) at local (0, .5, .3), mass .04, friction 1, on the doll's body. Five tumblers: cylinder half-height .06, radius .05, mass .012, friction .9, restitution 0, angular damping .5, linear damping .1. Foot: steps set a target speed of .55 m / .42 s, the foot low-passes to it with τ = .5 s, and it stops when no step has come for 1.6 cadences. Each step adds a rock of .2 N·m for .18 s to `nx`, and moves you sideways by `leanX × .25 × .55`. The boardwalk is ±.8 wide.\n- *Tiers ✔:* 30, 45, 60 m (24, 35, 47 s at a steady pace). Gusts 1.2, 2.0, 2.3 N·m for .5 s, about every 7, 6, 5 s. Glasses delivered of 5 with a brace of `.2 × gust` in stick units: Tier 1 5 / 5 / 5, Tier 2 3.8 / 4 / 4.5, Tier 3 2 / 4 / 3.5. With no brace: Tier 1 3.5 / 2.5 / 4, Tiers 2 and 3 none. **Gusts of 2.8 and up emptied the tray at every brace I tried, and over-bracing spills too:** a .6 brace on a 2 N·m gust leaves 2-4.8. No bot fell at these gusts. Do not raise them much.\n- *Scoring:* 100 per glass + time bonus. *Bot:* steady steps, keep centred, brace at `.2 × gust` on the telegraph.\n\n**Springboard** (the toy-block dive tower at the end of the pier; side camera, slow-mo side replay)\n- *Play:* Pump the board by squeezing both feet as it goes down. Press Hop near the top to launch. In the air the stick rotates you, holding Hop tucks and spins you faster. Hit the water. **Two ways to win:** a clean entry (head or feet down within 12°) makes barely a ripple and earns Grace. A perfect belly flop (front flat within 20°) sends up a giant column of water, knocks Gary out of the sky, and earns Splash. Score is the better of the two times height. Anything else is \"Technically a Dive.\"\n- *Board ✔:* an analytic spring, not Rapier: `y'' = −ω²y − 2ζωy' − (pumping ? 8 : 0)`, with ω = 2π / .9 s, ζ = .035, starting from a small bounce. Upward speed after 3 / 5 / 7 s of pumping: 4.7 / 6.5 / 7.6 m/s with good timing, 3.6 / 4.6 / 5.2 a little late. A quarter period late (.26 s), it barely moves (1.5 m/s). Pumping by reaction doesn't work, so give it a visible rhythm and a click. Launch speed = `max(3, upward speed at the press)`, so nobody is stuck.\n- *Air ✔:* air time 1.5 s from a 5 m tower at 4 m/s, 2 s at 7 m/s, 2.3 s from 9 m at 7 m/s. The stick sets a target spin up to 3.4 rad/s (195°/s), reached at 9 /s; tuck multiplies it by 1.8. Free the balance core in the air: remove the joint and apply this as torque. Hit chance if you stop by eye with a timing error of 80 / 150 / 250 ms: Grace 56% / 32% / 19% at full stick, 83 / 53 / 33 at half stick, 33 / 18 / 12 tucked; Splash 80 / 51 / 32, 98 / 78 / 53, 52 / 29 / 18. So slow spins are safer, and tucks are for showing off.\n- *Tiers and scoring:* three dives, best two count. Towers 5, 7, 9 m. Grace = `100 × (1 − error / 12°)`, Splash = `100 × (1 − error / 20°)`, each × `(1 + (height − 5) / 10)`.\n- *On entry:* swap to the ragdoll with a simple upward force below y = 0. Keep a 2 s ring buffer of the doll's transform and replay the best flop at ¼ speed.\n- *Bot:* pump in phase, then pick a flop or a dive. Good attract-mode comedy.\n\n**Tippy Raft** (a round raft at sea; high wide camera; party event) — the one event where players physically fight, and Group Class's reason to exist.\n- *Play:* 2-4 dolls on a raft. They walk with the stick. Flail shoves in a short cone. Hop hops. The raft tilts toward the sum of everyone's weight using the island's model (below), so everyone is on the same seesaw. **Swells** rock it from random directions, each warned by a rising wave. Sections float away over time. Off the edge: splash, out. Last doll standing wins the round; best of three. Solo in Free Play against three bots.\n- *Numbers ✔:* radius 6, losing 1 m every 12 s down to 2. Tilt model K 6, C 3.5, 14° for one doll at the rim, clamped to 26°. Slope push `tilt × 10`. Walk 3 m/s. Swell about every 6 s: adds 2.5 rad/s to the tilt velocity. Shove: within 1.3 m, gives the target 6 m/s, decaying at 3 /s, with a 2 s cooldown. Over 16 rounds with one sharp, two average and one clumsy bot: rounds lasted 10-55 s, 29 s on average; wins 6 / 8 (between two bots) / 2. So skill helps and doesn't decide it. (Without swells and at a slow shrink, rounds never ended: everyone just stands in the middle.)\n- *Not in the hub's set.* It needs a shared world, and the session is ghost mode. *Bot:* stay toward the centre and the high side, go at whoever is close, shove when in reach.\n\n### THE FINALE\n\n**The Grand Hold** (lighthouse gallery at sunset; camera circles slowly, the whole cast and island below)\n- *Play:* Your balance is now tied directly to the island: your lean tips everything below you. Four 20-second phases, each a remix: a Pose with breath; four stones on your head; storm gusts with Gary; a short Step Class in the wind with the class cheering from below. Then the last hold: 15 s, the ring shrinking to the centre, sway fading as you succeed, the final notch coming up.\n- *Rules:* It cannot be failed. A fall means the class catches you with a stretched bedsheet and the phase restarts. The ending plays on completion. Medals are given anyway (\"Exemplary, I suppose\").\n\n### Island physics (hub, Island Loop, Tippy Raft, finale) ✔\n\n**Do not move the island. Tilt gravity.** Rapier sees a fixed flat disc; the visual island group rotates; gravity is set to match each step. All the physics stays in island-local coordinates, with no moving-platform problems to solve. Up to 11° of tilt, a walker's height stayed within 0.004 m, and 60 props slid to the low side with none lost. (A kinematic platform also worked, but it is more to manage: I only took it to 1.8° and props rolled off.)\n\n```js\n// tx, tz = tilt in radians, toward +x and +z (that side goes down). dt = 1/120.\nvx += (K*restX + W*p.x - K*tx - C*vx) * dt;  tx += vx * dt;   // p = walker position; K 2.2, C 1.8\nvz += (K*restZ + W*p.z - K*tz - C*vz) * dt;  tz += vz * dt;   // W = K * rad(4) / 22 → 4° at the rim\nworld.gravity = { x: 9.81*Math.sin(tx), y: -9.81*Math.cos(Math.hypot(tx,tz)), z: 9.81*Math.sin(tz) };\nconst a = Math.hypot(tx,tz) || 1e-9, s = Math.sin(a/2);\nislandGroup.quaternion.set(tz/a*s, 0, -tx/a*s, Math.cos(a/2));     // +x side down is about -z\n```\n- Everything on the island is a child of `islandGroup`, including the player and props, using island-local coordinates. Keep the doll visually upright by counter-rotating most of the tilt.\n- Disc collider radius 22, friction .9. A low toy fence around the rim, physically 40 cuboids of half-size (.15, 1, 1.9), keeps props on. Props pile up against the fence on the low side: this is a good joke, so leave it.\n- Walker ✔: capsule (.5, .3), mass 1, `lockRotations()`, friction 0 with Min combine rule, `setCanSleep(false)`. Each step `setLinvel(wantedX + tx*3, v.y, wantedZ + tz*3)`, so the slope pushes you a little. Walk speed 4 m/s.\n- Props must be `setCanSleep(false)`, or they will not notice gravity changing.\n- The sea and horizon stay level; only the island tilts. The level horizon against the crooked lighthouse is what sells it.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Screens\n","numLines":170,"startLine":176,"totalLines":958}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01WrATaaMbvKxFgTaTLswUu1","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
