{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjGVKXVxYWeiqMvcv1PH","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01WjiZUg4GKae7XpCjXJSVDU","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\ndef sub(old,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==1, ('COUNT %d: '%s.count(old))+old[:90]\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\ndef between(start,end,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(start)==1, 'START '+start[:70]\n    a=s.index(start); b=s.index(end,a+len(start))\n    s=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\n\n# ---------- header\nsub(\"· 19 Done and reporting · 20 What was and wasn't verified · 21 TASK.md coverage\",\"· 19 Done and reporting · 20 What was and wasn't verified · 21 TASK.md coverage and proof · 22 Planning-stage report\")\n\n# ---------- section 3 controls\nsub(\"| Shift weight (events) / walk (island) | Left stick, axes 0 and 1 | W A S D or arrow keys | Pointer's offset from screen centre, normalised by 33% of screen height, clamped to the unit disc |\",\n    \"| Shift weight (events) / walk (island) | Left stick, axes 0 and 1 | W A S D or arrow keys (see below); C recentres | Pointer's offset from screen centre, normalised by 33% of screen height, clamped to the unit disc |\")\nsub(\"- **Keyboard feel:** weight keys ramp to full over 0.45 s and fall back over 0.25 s, so tapping gives small corrections. Foot keys ramp 0 to 1 in 0.12 s.\",\n\"\"\"- **Keys in balance events ✔: they move the asking ring, and it stays where you leave it.** Holding a key moves the ring at 1.6 units/s; releasing leaves it there, and C recentres. The ring must be clearly visible on the Balance Disc, because a player steering by keys watches the ring, not the dot. I tested three models with a person-like bot. Keys that spring back on release needed 9.6 presses a second just to hold a pose. A tap-then-accelerate model scored worst (Poses Tier 1: 68 / 75 / 38). This one holds up against the stick: Poses Tier 1 98 / 62 / 54 (stick 100 / 76 / 63), Tier 2 86 / 58 / 34 (90 / 64 / 53), Still Water Tier 1 100 / 85 / 28 (100 / 93 / 24), Fruit Hat Tier 1 21 / 22 / 20 banked with no falls (28 / 33 / 28), Room Service Tier 1 all five glasses. Use a move speed of about 1.6 and don't add acceleration.\n- **Keys when you're moving, not balancing: direct.** Walking on the island, Tippy Raft, steering in Island Loop and spinning in Springboard's air use the raw key direction, as PC players expect. Plate Spinners has its own scheme ✔, below.\n- **Foot keys** ramp 0 to 1 in 0.12 s.\n- **Latch presses ✔.** A key or mouse button pressed and released between two input polls must still count, or quick taps vanish at low frame rates. Normalise key codes too: the hub's harness sends synthetic events whose `code` is just `a`, so accept `a` and `KeyA` alike.\n- **Hover means the pointer moved ✔.** Move focus only on a `pointermove` whose coordinates changed. A browser also fires `pointerenter` when a menu appears under a parked mouse, and that made focus jump by itself under a controller player.\"\"\")\nsub(\"- **Haptics:** `pad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on falls, catches, rising fatigue and near-tip. Guard it, with a Settings toggle.\",\n    \"- **Haptics:** `pad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on falls, catches, rising fatigue and near-tip. Guard it, with a Settings toggle.\\n- **Audio on a controller ✔.** Call `AudioContext.resume()` on pad input too, not only on keys and clicks. My first wiring left a controller-only player in silence. I can't confirm here that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio, so if the context is still suspended a few seconds after pad input, show a small tag asking for a key or click.\")\n\n# ---------- section 4 traps and falling\nsub(\"- Use `world.integrationParameters.numSolverIterations = 8` to `10` for events with props.\",\n\"\"\"- Use `world.integrationParameters.numSolverIterations = 8` to `10` for events with props.\n- **A Rapier panic kills the physics for the whole page ✔.** It surfaces as `RuntimeError: unreachable` from the WASM module, and no later call recovers. I hit two causes, both of them easy to write by accident:\n  1. **Touching a body after removing it, or removing it twice.** Drop every reference when you remove a body: remove it from your lists in the same line.\n  2. **Removing a prop and the collider it rested on in the same step, or the support first and the prop later.** `world.step()` panics about half a second later, once the body moves. Even one stone does it. It looks fine if nothing moves, so a quick test can miss it. **Safe: remove the prop, step once, then remove the support.** `collider.setEnabled(false)` and `body.setEnabled(false)` are safe too, and so is freeing the whole world.\n  Everything else the plan does soaked clean ✔: ragdoll spawn and remove 30 times with the doll swaying, four ragdolls at once, fruit loaded and removed 40 times, removing and re-creating the foot joint 30 times, plate shards 40 times, tumblers respawned 30 times, 60 fresh worlds in a row, props removed on a gravity-tilted island. If a panic still happens: out of a session, save and reload the page; in a session, call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Never catch it and carry on.\"\"\")\nsub(\"After `recoverTime` (2.2 s) the parts snap back with a spring noise and the doll bounces upright. Only the current hold or rep is lost.\",\n    \"After `recoverTime` (2.2 s) the parts snap back with a spring noise and the doll bounces upright. Only the current hold or rep is lost. Disable the core while the ragdoll runs (`body.setEnabled(false)`). On recovery reset its pose, velocities and **your cached `leanX`, `leanZ`** ✔: if you forget, the stale lean makes the doll fall again at once, for ever.\")\n\n# ---------- plates\nsub(\"Circle the stick (or the mouse, or roll W-D-S-A) to drive the hoop.\",\"Circle the stick or the mouse to drive the hoop. **On keys, alternate A and D: each press is half a turn, so your tapping speed sets the hoop.**\")\nsub(\"- *Scoring:* form = `200 × share − 100`, floored at 0 ✔ Tier 1 100 / 74 / 46. *Bot:*\",\n    \"- *On keys ✔:* the asking ring is far too slow to reach any band, so this scheme is needed. Tapping at 1.35, 2.3 and 3.2 presses a second hits the three bands. Share alive: Tier 1 97% / 84% / 76%, Tier 2 91 / 79 / 74, Tier 3 79 / 66 / 60, in line with the stick.\\n- *Scoring:* form = `200 × share − 100`, floored at 0 ✔ Tier 1 100 / 74 / 46. *Bot:*\")\n\n# ---------- island loop\nsub(\"- *Scoring:* time in the band + shells. *Bot:* steady cadence, steer the racing line. Solo only.\",\n    \"- *Whole run ✔,* real walker on the tilted disc at 3.6° of rest tilt: two laps in 69-74 s, never more than .4 m off the road. Time in the band 99% / 97% / 57%, assuming cadence error 3% / 8% / 16%.\\n- *Scoring:* time in the band + shells. *Bot:* steady cadence, steer the racing line. Solo only.\")\n\n# ---------- fruit hat\nsub(\"That is the whole design working. Lean only for a fruit in its last 0.95 s of flight: chasing every fruit at full lean makes you whip and fall.\",\n    \"That is the whole design working. Lean only for a fruit in its last 0.95 s of flight: chasing every fruit at full lean makes you whip and fall. **I also ran this through a whole integrated build, with the bot pressing a stubbed pad ✔:** the sharp bot banking at 7 scored 47.8 raw in 4 banks with no falls, never banking fell exactly twice, and the clumsy bot banking at 5 scored 43.5 with no falls. So the numbers survive the real input path. Set `par` near 52 on Tier 1.\")\n\n# ---------- springboard\nsub(\"So slow spins are safer, and tucks are for showing off.\",\n    \"So slow spins are safer, and tucks are for showing off.\\n- *Whole run ✔,* pump then launch then a real spinning body, over 40 dives each: launch speed 5.4 / 3.8 / 3 m/s, height from a 5 m tower 6.5 / 5.7 / 5.5 m. Belly flop hit rate 43% / 35% / 13% at full stick, 85 / 68 / 45 at half stick. Head first 35 / 20 / 5 at full stick. **Half stick is too slow to get head first from the 5 m tower** (0% for everyone); it works from 9 m (40 / 0 / 0). Letting go, the spin coasts on for about 22° at full stick, so release early.\")\n\n# ---------- grand hold\nsub(\"- *Rules:* It cannot be failed. A fall means the class catches you with a stretched bedsheet and the phase restarts. The ending plays on completion. Medals are given anyway (\\\"Exemplary, I suppose\\\").\",\n    \"- *Rules:* It cannot be failed. A fall means the class catches you with a stretched bedsheet and the phase restarts. The ending plays on completion. Medals are given anyway (\\\"Exemplary, I suppose\\\").\\n- *Whole run ✔:* pose at (.3, .1) with sway .1; four stones on a head plate with sway .12; gusts of 3 N·m with a telegraph; lean cues; last hold with sway fading from .2 and the ring from .3 to .08. No bot needed a single restart, and it takes 95 s. Time in the ring on the last hold: 98-100% / 47-93% / 30-44%. **At the end of the stones phase, remove the stones, step once, then remove the head plate** (section 4): doing both together is what crashed Rapier for me.\")\n\n# ---------- audio\nsub(\"Start the context on the first gesture; before that the game runs silently without error. In a hub session, stop all sound on `end`.\",\n    \"Start the context on the first gesture, including pad input (section 3); before that the game runs silently without error. In a hub session, stop all sound on `end`.\\n\\n**What I could check without ears ✔** (TASK.md's stand-in: render it and read the signal). I wrote the FM e-piano, bass, foot click and voices from this section and rendered them with `OfflineAudioContext`. All 21 notes of the title motif came out at the written pitch class (19 exact; two read an octave low by autocorrelation, likely the detector). With backing, the peak is −2.1 dB with nothing clipped. **With music at 10 and sound at 80, a foot click stands +17.6 dB above the music just before it (weakest +7 dB),** so Step Class's beat is readable. In a real-time run an `AnalyserNode` on the music bus heard the motif, at bus gain .10.\")\nsub(\"Occasional set exclamations in nonsense Simlish (\\\"Wobba-la!\\\", \\\"Oof-ah.\\\", \\\"Nee nee!\\\"). The text types out in time.\",\n    \"Occasional set exclamations in nonsense Simlish (\\\"Wobba-la!\\\", \\\"Oof-ah.\\\", \\\"Nee nee!\\\"). The text types out in time. **Balance the voices ✔:** at equal settings Trudy's narrow formant filters peaked at +1 dB (clipping, rms −20 dB), while Ms. Stillwater and Coach Bolster sat near −30 dB rms. Give each voice its own gain so all peak around −6 dB. Spaces and punctuation bring the real rate down to about 12.7, 5.5 and 8.7 blips a second for the rates below.\")\n\n# ---------- section 16\nsub(\"Pixel ratio up to 2. Wrap boot in try/catch: if nothing starts, show the message and call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Performance is out of scope, per TASK.md: add detail freely. On this computer a simple scene ran at about 59 fps at 720p under software WebGPU ✔.\",\n    \"Pixel ratio up to 2. Wrap boot in try/catch: if nothing starts, show the message and call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Watch `renderer.backend.device.lost` and record it, because a lost device gives you no error and a loop that seems to run (section 17). Performance is out of scope, per TASK.md: add detail freely. On this computer, with flags that really render, a simple scene runs at about 6 fps at 720p under software WebGPU ✔.\")\nsub(\"**The loop.** Fixed 1/120 s simulation. `frame(real)`: `acc += min(real, 0.1) × timeScale`; run `game.fixed(1/120)` up to 12 times; `game.frame(real)`; `post.render()`.\",\n    \"**The loop.** Fixed 1/120 s simulation. `frame(real)`: `acc += min(real, 0.25) × timeScale`; run `game.fixed(1/120)` up to 30 times; `game.frame(real)`; `post.render()`. That keeps the game on real time down to 4 fps ✔: with a 0.1 s cap at this computer's 6 fps, everything including attract's 10 seconds ran slow.\")\n\n# ---------- section 17 flags\nbetween(\"**Browser flags ✔ (use these exact sets):**\",\"**Test hooks.**\",\n\"\"\"**Browser flags ✔ (use this set for anything that needs the renderer):**\n```js\nconst RENDER = ['--enable-unsafe-webgpu', '--enable-features=Vulkan', '--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader', '--use-vulkan=swiftshader', '--use-angle=swiftshader'];\n```\n- **Do not use TASK.md's three WebGPU flags alone. With them, Three's device is lost 0.2 s after startup ✔** (\"A valid external Instance reference no longer exists\"). `backend === 'webgpu'` stays true and the frame counter climbs at about 60 fps, but nothing is being drawn, and that is why the screenshots are black. It fooled me: I first reported that speed as real. With the two extra flags, which the hub's own `hub-check` docs also list, the device stays alive, frames are real at about 6 fps, and `page.screenshot` shows the true image ✔.\n- **Every test that renders must assert `render.lost` is null.** \"Frames rising\" proves nothing by itself. Also read the screenshot back as pixels and check it has ink and a range of colours ✔.\n- Logic tests don't need any of this: `?test=1&render=0` runs with no renderer at all, on plain Chromium.\n- No flags at all: Chromium finds no adapter and falls back to WebGL2 (slowly) ✔. `channel: 'chromium'` new headless and headed Chromium under xvfb both aborted here with the WebGPU flags: don't bother.\n- WebKit: WebGL2 fallback, works, screenshots are real ✔. Use it to test the fallback. Firefox: no WebGL2 when headless (`AllowWebgl2:false`); it does have WebGL2 when headed under `xvfb-run` ✔. Not needed for routine tests.\n- Rapier's deprecated-parameters warning is harmless. **Treat any \"Device Lost\" message as a failure.** There are none with the flags above.\n\n\"\"\")\nsub(\"With `?render=0`, the game skips creating a renderer, which makes logic tests fast. Rapier and all game logic still run.\",\n    \"With `?render=0`, the game skips creating a renderer, which makes logic tests fast. Rapier and all game logic still run. With `?manual=1`, the game's own loop doesn't run at all, so only `advance()` moves time and tests never race the frame loop ✔. Add `render: { lost }` to `state()`.\")\nsub(\"Use `page.keyboard` and `page.mouse` for the rest.\",\"Use `page.keyboard` and `page.mouse` for the rest. When a bot stops, zero the stub: a stick left pushed scrolls every menu ✔.\")\nsub(\"One run with rendering on for each event for 2 s with `WEBGPU` flags: `backend === 'webgpu'`, frames rising, no console errors.\",\n    \"One run with rendering on for each event for 2 s with `RENDER` flags: `backend === 'webgpu'`, `render.lost` null, frames rising, no console errors. Also soak the removal paths with the doll swaying, because Rapier's panic only shows once something moves (section 4).\")\nsub(\"Open `harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=N&auto=1` with `WEBGPU` plus `--no-sandbox --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`,\",\n    \"Open `harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=N&auto=1` with `RENDER` plus `--no-sandbox --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`,\")\nsub(\"**Art pass, by eye, at the end.** `tools/shots.mjs` with `SHOTS` at 1920×1080:\",\"**Art pass, by eye, at the end.** `tools/shots.mjs` with `RENDER` at 1920×1080:\")\nsub(\"sharp above clumsy by at least 15 points of share\",\"sharp above clumsy by at least 15 points of share, stick and keys alike\")\n\n# ---------- section 19: Testing row filled in\nsub(\"| Testing | The testing parameters TASK.md allowed, and what you ran |\",\n    \"| Testing | The parameters TASK.md allows: as much headless testing as you like, with scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Then what you actually ran |\")\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nprint('ok words',len(s.split()))\nEOF\ngrep -n \"SHOTS\\|WEBGPU\\b\" PLAN.md | cut -c1-120","description":"Write integration findings and corrections into the plan"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2480,"cache_read_input_tokens":758064,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2480},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"196c42b3-8c7a-43e3-a29e-a7231e853a6a","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:59:11.924Z","request_id":"req_011CgjGVJwXbVN3CeJEFfYTT","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01WjiZUg4GKae7XpCjXJSVDU":{"command":"cd /home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-211905__xe6f2a4/work && python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\ndef sub(old,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==1, ('COUNT %d: '%s.count(old))+old[:90]\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\ndef between(start,end,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(start)==1, 'START '+start[:70]\n    a=s.index(start); b=s.index(end,a+len(start))\n    s=s[:a]+new+s[b:]\n\n# ---------- header\nsub(\"· 19 Done and reporting · 20 What was and wasn't verified · 21 TASK.md coverage\",\"· 19 Done and reporting · 20 What was and wasn't verified · 21 TASK.md coverage and proof · 22 Planning-stage report\")\n\n# ---------- section 3 controls\nsub(\"| Shift weight (events) / walk (island) | Left stick, axes 0 and 1 | W A S D or arrow keys | Pointer's offset from screen centre, normalised by 33% of screen height, clamped to the unit disc |\",\n    \"| Shift weight (events) / walk (island) | Left stick, axes 0 and 1 | W A S D or arrow keys (see below); C recentres | Pointer's offset from screen centre, normalised by 33% of screen height, clamped to the unit disc |\")\nsub(\"- **Keyboard feel:** weight keys ramp to full over 0.45 s and fall back over 0.25 s, so tapping gives small corrections. Foot keys ramp 0 to 1 in 0.12 s.\",\n\"\"\"- **Keys in balance events ✔: they move the asking ring, and it stays where you leave it.** Holding a key moves the ring at 1.6 units/s; releasing leaves it there, and C recentres. The ring must be clearly visible on the Balance Disc, because a player steering by keys watches the ring, not the dot. I tested three models with a person-like bot. Keys that spring back on release needed 9.6 presses a second just to hold a pose. A tap-then-accelerate model scored worst (Poses Tier 1: 68 / 75 / 38). This one holds up against the stick: Poses Tier 1 98 / 62 / 54 (stick 100 / 76 / 63), Tier 2 86 / 58 / 34 (90 / 64 / 53), Still Water Tier 1 100 / 85 / 28 (100 / 93 / 24), Fruit Hat Tier 1 21 / 22 / 20 banked with no falls (28 / 33 / 28), Room Service Tier 1 all five glasses. Use a move speed of about 1.6 and don't add acceleration.\n- **Keys when you're moving, not balancing: direct.** Walking on the island, Tippy Raft, steering in Island Loop and spinning in Springboard's air use the raw key direction, as PC players expect. Plate Spinners has its own scheme ✔, below.\n- **Foot keys** ramp 0 to 1 in 0.12 s.\n- **Latch presses ✔.** A key or mouse button pressed and released between two input polls must still count, or quick taps vanish at low frame rates. Normalise key codes too: the hub's harness sends synthetic events whose `code` is just `a`, so accept `a` and `KeyA` alike.\n- **Hover means the pointer moved ✔.** Move focus only on a `pointermove` whose coordinates changed. A browser also fires `pointerenter` when a menu appears under a parked mouse, and that made focus jump by itself under a controller player.\"\"\")\nsub(\"- **Haptics:** `pad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on falls, catches, rising fatigue and near-tip. Guard it, with a Settings toggle.\",\n    \"- **Haptics:** `pad.vibrationActuator?.playEffect('dual-rumble', …)` on falls, catches, rising fatigue and near-tip. Guard it, with a Settings toggle.\\n- **Audio on a controller ✔.** Call `AudioContext.resume()` on pad input too, not only on keys and clicks. My first wiring left a controller-only player in silence. I can't confirm here that every browser accepts a gamepad press as the gesture that unlocks audio, so if the context is still suspended a few seconds after pad input, show a small tag asking for a key or click.\")\n\n# ---------- section 4 traps and falling\nsub(\"- Use `world.integrationParameters.numSolverIterations = 8` to `10` for events with props.\",\n\"\"\"- Use `world.integrationParameters.numSolverIterations = 8` to `10` for events with props.\n- **A Rapier panic kills the physics for the whole page ✔.** It surfaces as `RuntimeError: unreachable` from the WASM module, and no later call recovers. I hit two causes, both of them easy to write by accident:\n  1. **Touching a body after removing it, or removing it twice.** Drop every reference when you remove a body: remove it from your lists in the same line.\n  2. **Removing a prop and the collider it rested on in the same step, or the support first and the prop later.** `world.step()` panics about half a second later, once the body moves. Even one stone does it. It looks fine if nothing moves, so a quick test can miss it. **Safe: remove the prop, step once, then remove the support.** `collider.setEnabled(false)` and `body.setEnabled(false)` are safe too, and so is freeing the whole world.\n  Everything else the plan does soaked clean ✔: ragdoll spawn and remove 30 times with the doll swaying, four ragdolls at once, fruit loaded and removed 40 times, removing and re-creating the foot joint 30 times, plate shards 40 times, tumblers respawned 30 times, 60 fresh worlds in a row, props removed on a gravity-tilted island. If a panic still happens: out of a session, save and reload the page; in a session, call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Never catch it and carry on.\"\"\")\nsub(\"After `recoverTime` (2.2 s) the parts snap back with a spring noise and the doll bounces upright. Only the current hold or rep is lost.\",\n    \"After `recoverTime` (2.2 s) the parts snap back with a spring noise and the doll bounces upright. Only the current hold or rep is lost. Disable the core while the ragdoll runs (`body.setEnabled(false)`). On recovery reset its pose, velocities and **your cached `leanX`, `leanZ`** ✔: if you forget, the stale lean makes the doll fall again at once, for ever.\")\n\n# ---------- plates\nsub(\"Circle the stick (or the mouse, or roll W-D-S-A) to drive the hoop.\",\"Circle the stick or the mouse to drive the hoop. **On keys, alternate A and D: each press is half a turn, so your tapping speed sets the hoop.**\")\nsub(\"- *Scoring:* form = `200 × share − 100`, floored at 0 ✔ Tier 1 100 / 74 / 46. *Bot:*\",\n    \"- *On keys ✔:* the asking ring is far too slow to reach any band, so this scheme is needed. Tapping at 1.35, 2.3 and 3.2 presses a second hits the three bands. Share alive: Tier 1 97% / 84% / 76%, Tier 2 91 / 79 / 74, Tier 3 79 / 66 / 60, in line with the stick.\\n- *Scoring:* form = `200 × share − 100`, floored at 0 ✔ Tier 1 100 / 74 / 46. *Bot:*\")\n\n# ---------- island loop\nsub(\"- *Scoring:* time in the band + shells. *Bot:* steady cadence, steer the racing line. Solo only.\",\n    \"- *Whole run ✔,* real walker on the tilted disc at 3.6° of rest tilt: two laps in 69-74 s, never more than .4 m off the road. Time in the band 99% / 97% / 57%, assuming cadence error 3% / 8% / 16%.\\n- *Scoring:* time in the band + shells. *Bot:* steady cadence, steer the racing line. Solo only.\")\n\n# ---------- fruit hat\nsub(\"That is the whole design working. Lean only for a fruit in its last 0.95 s of flight: chasing every fruit at full lean makes you whip and fall.\",\n    \"That is the whole design working. Lean only for a fruit in its last 0.95 s of flight: chasing every fruit at full lean makes you whip and fall. **I also ran this through a whole integrated build, with the bot pressing a stubbed pad ✔:** the sharp bot banking at 7 scored 47.8 raw in 4 banks with no falls, never banking fell exactly twice, and the clumsy bot banking at 5 scored 43.5 with no falls. So the numbers survive the real input path. Set `par` near 52 on Tier 1.\")\n\n# ---------- springboard\nsub(\"So slow spins are safer, and tucks are for showing off.\",\n    \"So slow spins are safer, and tucks are for showing off.\\n- *Whole run ✔,* pump then launch then a real spinning body, over 40 dives each: launch speed 5.4 / 3.8 / 3 m/s, height from a 5 m tower 6.5 / 5.7 / 5.5 m. Belly flop hit rate 43% / 35% / 13% at full stick, 85 / 68 / 45 at half stick. Head first 35 / 20 / 5 at full stick. **Half stick is too slow to get head first from the 5 m tower** (0% for everyone); it works from 9 m (40 / 0 / 0). Letting go, the spin coasts on for about 22° at full stick, so release early.\")\n\n# ---------- grand hold\nsub(\"- *Rules:* It cannot be failed. A fall means the class catches you with a stretched bedsheet and the phase restarts. The ending plays on completion. Medals are given anyway (\\\"Exemplary, I suppose\\\").\",\n    \"- *Rules:* It cannot be failed. A fall means the class catches you with a stretched bedsheet and the phase restarts. The ending plays on completion. Medals are given anyway (\\\"Exemplary, I suppose\\\").\\n- *Whole run ✔:* pose at (.3, .1) with sway .1; four stones on a head plate with sway .12; gusts of 3 N·m with a telegraph; lean cues; last hold with sway fading from .2 and the ring from .3 to .08. No bot needed a single restart, and it takes 95 s. Time in the ring on the last hold: 98-100% / 47-93% / 30-44%. **At the end of the stones phase, remove the stones, step once, then remove the head plate** (section 4): doing both together is what crashed Rapier for me.\")\n\n# ---------- audio\nsub(\"Start the context on the first gesture; before that the game runs silently without error. In a hub session, stop all sound on `end`.\",\n    \"Start the context on the first gesture, including pad input (section 3); before that the game runs silently without error. In a hub session, stop all sound on `end`.\\n\\n**What I could check without ears ✔** (TASK.md's stand-in: render it and read the signal). I wrote the FM e-piano, bass, foot click and voices from this section and rendered them with `OfflineAudioContext`. All 21 notes of the title motif came out at the written pitch class (19 exact; two read an octave low by autocorrelation, likely the detector). With backing, the peak is −2.1 dB with nothing clipped. **With music at 10 and sound at 80, a foot click stands +17.6 dB above the music just before it (weakest +7 dB),** so Step Class's beat is readable. In a real-time run an `AnalyserNode` on the music bus heard the motif, at bus gain .10.\")\nsub(\"Occasional set exclamations in nonsense Simlish (\\\"Wobba-la!\\\", \\\"Oof-ah.\\\", \\\"Nee nee!\\\"). The text types out in time.\",\n    \"Occasional set exclamations in nonsense Simlish (\\\"Wobba-la!\\\", \\\"Oof-ah.\\\", \\\"Nee nee!\\\"). The text types out in time. **Balance the voices ✔:** at equal settings Trudy's narrow formant filters peaked at +1 dB (clipping, rms −20 dB), while Ms. Stillwater and Coach Bolster sat near −30 dB rms. Give each voice its own gain so all peak around −6 dB. Spaces and punctuation bring the real rate down to about 12.7, 5.5 and 8.7 blips a second for the rates below.\")\n\n# ---------- section 16\nsub(\"Pixel ratio up to 2. Wrap boot in try/catch: if nothing starts, show the message and call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Performance is out of scope, per TASK.md: add detail freely. On this computer a simple scene ran at about 59 fps at 720p under software WebGPU ✔.\",\n    \"Pixel ratio up to 2. Wrap boot in try/catch: if nothing starts, show the message and call `AIHub.fail(message)`. Watch `renderer.backend.device.lost` and record it, because a lost device gives you no error and a loop that seems to run (section 17). Performance is out of scope, per TASK.md: add detail freely. On this computer, with flags that really render, a simple scene runs at about 6 fps at 720p under software WebGPU ✔.\")\nsub(\"**The loop.** Fixed 1/120 s simulation. `frame(real)`: `acc += min(real, 0.1) × timeScale`; run `game.fixed(1/120)` up to 12 times; `game.frame(real)`; `post.render()`.\",\n    \"**The loop.** Fixed 1/120 s simulation. `frame(real)`: `acc += min(real, 0.25) × timeScale`; run `game.fixed(1/120)` up to 30 times; `game.frame(real)`; `post.render()`. That keeps the game on real time down to 4 fps ✔: with a 0.1 s cap at this computer's 6 fps, everything including attract's 10 seconds ran slow.\")\n\n# ---------- section 17 flags\nbetween(\"**Browser flags ✔ (use these exact sets):**\",\"**Test hooks.**\",\n\"\"\"**Browser flags ✔ (use this set for anything that needs the renderer):**\n```js\nconst RENDER = ['--enable-unsafe-webgpu', '--enable-features=Vulkan', '--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader', '--use-vulkan=swiftshader', '--use-angle=swiftshader'];\n```\n- **Do not use TASK.md's three WebGPU flags alone. With them, Three's device is lost 0.2 s after startup ✔** (\"A valid external Instance reference no longer exists\"). `backend === 'webgpu'` stays true and the frame counter climbs at about 60 fps, but nothing is being drawn, and that is why the screenshots are black. It fooled me: I first reported that speed as real. With the two extra flags, which the hub's own `hub-check` docs also list, the device stays alive, frames are real at about 6 fps, and `page.screenshot` shows the true image ✔.\n- **Every test that renders must assert `render.lost` is null.** \"Frames rising\" proves nothing by itself. Also read the screenshot back as pixels and check it has ink and a range of colours ✔.\n- Logic tests don't need any of this: `?test=1&render=0` runs with no renderer at all, on plain Chromium.\n- No flags at all: Chromium finds no adapter and falls back to WebGL2 (slowly) ✔. `channel: 'chromium'` new headless and headed Chromium under xvfb both aborted here with the WebGPU flags: don't bother.\n- WebKit: WebGL2 fallback, works, screenshots are real ✔. Use it to test the fallback. Firefox: no WebGL2 when headless (`AllowWebgl2:false`); it does have WebGL2 when headed under `xvfb-run` ✔. Not needed for routine tests.\n- Rapier's deprecated-parameters warning is harmless. **Treat any \"Device Lost\" message as a failure.** There are none with the flags above.\n\n\"\"\")\nsub(\"With `?render=0`, the game skips creating a renderer, which makes logic tests fast. Rapier and all game logic still run.\",\n    \"With `?render=0`, the game skips creating a renderer, which makes logic tests fast. Rapier and all game logic still run. With `?manual=1`, the game's own loop doesn't run at all, so only `advance()` moves time and tests never race the frame loop ✔. Add `render: { lost }` to `state()`.\")\nsub(\"Use `page.keyboard` and `page.mouse` for the rest.\",\"Use `page.keyboard` and `page.mouse` for the rest. When a bot stops, zero the stub: a stick left pushed scrolls every menu ✔.\")\nsub(\"One run with rendering on for each event for 2 s with `WEBGPU` flags: `backend === 'webgpu'`, frames rising, no console errors.\",\n    \"One run with rendering on for each event for 2 s with `RENDER` flags: `backend === 'webgpu'`, `render.lost` null, frames rising, no console errors. Also soak the removal paths with the doll swaying, because Rapier's panic only shows once something moves (section 4).\")\nsub(\"Open `harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=N&auto=1` with `WEBGPU` plus `--no-sandbox --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`,\",\n    \"Open `harness.html?game=GAME.html&players=N&auto=1` with `RENDER` plus `--no-sandbox --autoplay-policy=no-user-gesture-required`,\")\nsub(\"**Art pass, by eye, at the end.** `tools/shots.mjs` with `SHOTS` at 1920×1080:\",\"**Art pass, by eye, at the end.** `tools/shots.mjs` with `RENDER` at 1920×1080:\")\nsub(\"sharp above clumsy by at least 15 points of share\",\"sharp above clumsy by at least 15 points of share, stick and keys alike\")\n\n# ---------- section 19: Testing row filled in\nsub(\"| Testing | The testing parameters TASK.md allowed, and what you ran |\",\n    \"| Testing | The parameters TASK.md allows: as much headless testing as you like, with scripts that play the game and check its state, including a scripted playthrough of the whole game; one command that prints only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after changing the code it covers; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit. Then what you actually ran |\")\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\nprint('ok words',len(s.split()))\nEOF\ngrep -n \"SHOTS\\|WEBGPU\\b\" PLAN.md | cut -c1-120","description":"Write integration findings and corrections into the plan"}}}
