{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjH2X8dVz5bW9LBChoSV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`PLAN.md` covers everything `TASK.md` asks for, and coverage no longer rests on keyword checks. Each of its 54 requirements now names what proves it at build time, and most of those checks already ran on a working prototype built from the plan's own recipes. `planner-exit-interview.json` is updated too, nothing is committed, and my scratch files are deleted.\n\n**Correction:** the \"about 59 fps\" I reported earlier was wrong. With the WebGPU flags listed in `TASK.md`, the device is lost 0.2 s after startup while the frame counter keeps climbing, so nothing is drawn. Two extra flags, `--use-vulkan=swiftshader --use-angle=swiftshader`, give real frames at about 6 fps. The plan now uses those flags and fails any test where the device is lost. Please fix that line in `TASK.md`, because it would have every builder testing nothing.\n\n## The hook's objections\n| Objection | Status |\n|---|---|\n| Coverage rests on keyword checks | **Fixed.** Section 21 has a \"proven by\" column; ✔ means the check ran on the prototype, which integrates one event of 13 |\n| Testing Feedback isn't in the plan | **Fixed.** Section 22 has the full planning-stage report |\n| The \"Testing\" field isn't filled in | **Fixed.** It now lists the actual allowed parameters |\n| Simulations are partial | **Mostly fixed.** Springboard, Grand Hold and Island Loop now run as whole simulations, and keyboard play was simulated for five events. Step Class and Plate Spinners still run as logic only |\n| Audio not heard | **Checked as far as possible.** Signal checks are the stand-in `TASK.md` names; nobody has actually listened |\n| No controller or TV play | **Can't be done here.** `TASK.md` says this is \"checked as far as it can be here, never required\" |\n| No About screen or exit file for a build | **Mechanism proven, not written.** The prototype's About screen shows every field from a JSON file, and section 22 has the file's exact shape. A real one belongs to the build stage, and creating it now could pollute the run's records |\n\n## The prototype\nI built Fruit Hat from the plan's recipes, with its balance, tilt and ink code extracted straight from `PLAN.md`. It covers all three input devices, attract, dev sliders, touch-only, About, audio and a hub session. One command ran the suite: **209 passed, 0 failed**, including all 16 hub harness checks in five scenarios with real rendering.\n\nFruit Hat's balance numbers held up through the full build: a bot that never banks falls exactly twice a minute, as simulated.\n\n## New problems found, now in the plan\n- **Rapier crash:** removing a prop and its support in the same step kills physics for the whole page, and so does touching a removed body. The plan gives the safe order.\n- **Keyboard:** springing keys needed 9.6 presses a second to hold a pose, and Plate Spinners couldn't be played on keys at all. Both now have tested schemes.\n- **Input wiring:**\n  - A parked mouse stole menu focus.\n  - Holding both mouse buttons never registered.\n  - Controller-only play was silent.\n  - Quick key taps were lost at low frame rates.\n- **Other:** the doll could fall forever after recovering, Trudy's voice clipped, and attract ran slow on slow machines.\n\n## Still unverified\n- **Fun and music quality:** the bots are a model of a person, and no one has played the game or listened to it.\n- **The other 12 events:** they are simulated, not built.\n- **Controller audio:** I can't confirm every browser lets a controller press unlock sound, so the plan includes a fallback.\n\n## Reporting from the planning stage\n**Source Information**\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 21:19:05\n- **Source:** Wii Fit\n- **Plan:** none\n- **Genre and Perspective:** physics-balance party sports with a daily-ritual progression; third person, with framed stage cameras and an orbitable chase camera\n- **Tech:** Three.js R180 (WebGPU, WebGL2 fallback), Rapier 0.19.3, Web Audio, Gamepad API, esbuild 0.28.2, Playwright 1.63.0, Fredoka 5.3.0\n- **Art Direction:** cel-shaded ink over a wooden-toy resort, all models made in code\n- **Animation:** `AnimationMixer` clips on jointed rigs, physics-driven lean, Rapier ragdolls (prototyped with test clips)\n- **Audio:** code-synthesised city-pop and bossa at 10% by default, effects, Animalese and Simlish-style voices (only the title theme, voices and foot click prototyped)\n- **Online:** AI Hub Session Protocol v1 in ghost mode for 1–4 devices, plus 1–4 on one screen\n- **Testing:** as much headless testing as wanted, using scripts that play the game and check state, including a whole-game playthrough; one command printing only failures and a one-line count, rerun only after code changes; screenshots only for what a script can't check; no subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit\n- **Research:** hub protocol pages and code; source background from search summaries\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort max\n- **Usage:** added on import\n\n**Testing Feedback**\n1. **Issues caught:** about 38.\n2. **Critical:** 13, listed in section 22.\n3. **Most helpful:** building one event through everything from the plan's own recipes.\n4. **Least helpful:** the Firefox WebGL2 checks, which changed nothing in the game.\n\n**Engine Feedback**\n1. **Liked most:** `toonOutlinePass` plus a short TSL pass gave the whole ink look, unchanged on WebGPU and WebGL2.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong:** yes. I took a lost device for 60 fps, and convex-hull stones collapsed on their own.\n3. **Most important thing built:** the balance core, a torque-limited controller on a ball joint.\n4. **Hardest part:** knowing what was really happening. A lost device gives no JavaScript error and the loop keeps running, and a Rapier crash can surface half a second after its cause.\n5. **Changed in the engine:** none.\n6. **One change:** make Rapier's panic a catchable error.\n\n**Prompt Feedback**\n1. **Liked most:** open creative choices combined with exact, checkable facts.\n2. **Confusing:** what \"the game on its own\" means for multiplayer, \"native style\" for an engine with none, and which parts of `TASK.md` apply at the plan stage.\n3. **Hardest:** making a balance game a steady thumb can't trivially beat, while staying original.\n4. **Would change:** fix the WebGPU flags, define standalone multiplayer, and state how capable builders are.\n\n## Files are in the run's `work` folder\n- `PLAN.md`\n- `planner-exit-interview.json`\n\nSources (read as search summaries, not full pages):\n- [Wii Fit, Wikipedia](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wii_Fit)\n- [Iwata Asks: Wii Fit, part 3, page 2](https://iwataasks.nintendo.com/interviews/wii/wii_fit/3/2)\n- [Iwata Asks: Wii Fit, part 3, page 4](https://iwataasks.nintendo.com/interviews/wii/wii_fit/3/4)\n- [Nintendo World Report review](https://nintendoworldreport.com/review/16177/wii-fit-wii)\n- [AI Hub Session Protocol](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/README.md)"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2472,"cache_read_input_tokens":786341,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2472},"output_tokens":716,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"1d2b112a-8c14-45b1-8731-cb86e60007b4","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:05:21.147Z","request_id":"req_011CgjH2WccbQ9o4VwJZ9VTW"}
