fari\n[77] - **No TV.** Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080.\n     §17: Judge the 10-foot view from screenshots at 1920×1080\n[79] - Other agents run here at the same time.\n     §17: Other agents run here at the same time\n[80] Keep one or two browsers open at once, and close them when you're done.\n     §17: Keep to one or two browsers at once and close them when done\n[81] - Keep every file you make inside your own folder, not /tmp or a parent folder: /tmp is shar\n     §17: Keep every file inside your own folder, not `/tmp` (it's shared) or a parent folder\n[82] - The browsers are already downloaded: don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install\n     §17: Don't run `playwright install` or `playwright install-deps`\n[84] For one (Pillow, numpy …), use uv:\n     §17: If you want a Python tool, use `uv run --with ...`\n[87] Build an original game inspired by the source.\n     §2: This is an original game in the source's genre\n[89] Create a feature-complete demo.\n     §1: A feature-complete demo\n[90] Capture what makes the source distinctive (its world, characters, themes and tone) and turn \n     §1: What makes the source itself\n     §8: **Writing style.**\n[91] The demo must be long enough to show ALL features and gameplay mechanics so a player can cle\n     §1: long enough to show everything the game can do\n     §6: say what you can do here and, in one line, what you can't\n[92] Treat the demo as a complete, polished vertical slice.\n     §1: a complete, polished vertical slice\n[93] It should contain all the infrastructure, systems, and polish needed so the remainder of the\n     §14: TASK.md wants the rest of the game buildable with normal designer tools\n[94] Include a dev mode, reachable from the pause menu, with the standard tools for testing the g\n     §14: From the Pause menu.\n     §14: **Tools:** jump to any event and tier\n[95] Balance is key, so expose the most important gameplay variables as live sliders, with a rese\n     §14: **Live sliders with Reset to Defaults**\n[97] After 10 seconds without input on the title/menu screen, start a looping AI-played demo or s\n     §13: After 10 s with no input on the Title or Main menu, start a loop of bot-played vignettes\n     §13: **Any input exits and is swallowed:**\n[98] The AI (or replay) can be simple and may cheat freely (infinite health, ammo, abilities, no \n     §13: god mode on\n     §13: let the bots cheat\n[99] Its only job is to show as much of the demo as possible, the attract mode is not meant to ba\n     §13: Show as much as possible\n[101] Follow https://github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub for multiplayer integratio\n     §12: **Two modes, both required.**\n     §12: AI Hub session: ghost mode\n[103] Use a writing style similar to the source’s.\n     §8: **Writing style.** Short sentences. Polite. Second person.\n[105] Design the game around a PS5 DualSense (or any pad the browser reports in its standard mappi\n     §3: Design for a PS5 DualSense, or any pad the browser reports with `mapping === 'standard'`, on a TV at 10 \n[106] With keyboard and mouse it's the same game on the same screens, not a separate desktop layou\n     §3: Keyboard and mouse play the same game on the same screens, with no separate desktop layout, and they sho\n[107] The keyboard uses the keys PC players expect for the genre.\n     §3: W A S D or arrow keys\n     §3: Left Shift\n[108] The mouse aims, looks, and clicks menu items and things in the world (hover moves the focus)\n     §3: Pointer's offset from screen centre\n     §3: Hold right button and drag\n[109] On-screen prompts follow the input used last:\n     §3: **Prompts follow the last input.**\n[110] PlayStation glyphs for the controller (with a Settings option to show Xbox glyphs, A B X Y, \n     §3: PlayStation shapes and Xbox letters (A B X Y\n     §3: Keyboard prompts show keycaps, mouse prompts show a mouse with the button lit\n[111] Do not use overly dense, web-style data screens.\n     §7: No tables, no scrolling lists of data\n[112] Touch-only devices: no touch controls; show \"Controller or keyboard required\" and the settin\n     §15: \"Controller or keyboard required\" and a Settings button\n     §15: There are no touch controls anywhere else\n[113] Let the attract mode run.\n     §15: Attract mode runs after 10 s as usual\n[115] No TODOs, placeholders, or stubbed features.\n     §19: no TODOs, placeholders or stubbed features\n[116] The demo is complete.\n     §19: the demo is complete\n[119] Put Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback in your chat r\n     §19: go in **your chat reply** and an **in-game About/Credits screen,**\n[120] Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n     §19: Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm\n[121] - Date: 2026-11-04 21:19:05\n     §19: | Date | The date line in your TASK.md |\n[122] - Source: the work it's based on\n     §19: | Source | Wii Fit |\n[123] - Plan: the file name of the plan you were given to build from, or none\n     §19: | Plan | The file name of the plan you were given |\n[124] - Genre and Perspective: genre and camera perspective used\n     §19: | Genre and Perspective |\n[125] - Tech: stack, libraries/engines, with versions if applicable\n     §19: | Tech | Three.js R180\n[126] - Art Direction: style used\n     §19: | Art Direction |\n[127] - Animation: system used\n     §19: | Animation |\n[128] - Audio: music, sound effects and voice used\n     §19: | Audio |\n[129] - Online: network features used, or none\n     §19: | Online |\n[130] - Testing: testing parameters allowed\n     §19: | Testing | The parameters TASK.md allows\n[131] - Research: research done, or none\n     §19: | Research | Yours, or none\n[132] - Environment:\n     §19: | Environment | As written in your TASK.md |\n[135] - Model:\n     §19: | Model | As written in your TASK.md |\n[137] - Usage: write \"added on import\".\n     §19: | Usage | Exactly `added on import`\n[138] You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it: the runner records the real cost, tokens \n     §19: You can't see your own cost, so don't estimate it\n[139] Exit interview: before you finish, also save these answers as exit-interview.json in this fo\n     §19: **the exit-interview file** beside the HTML\n     §19: one JSON object in TASK.md's exact shape\n[140] Tools read it (the game's run record and the AI Hub catalogue), so write plain text, and che\n     §19: plain text, every value filled in, and you check that it parses\n[142] \"sourceInformation\": {\"date\":\n     §22: \"sourceInformation\"\n[158] \"testingFeedback\":\n     §22: \"testingFeedback\"\n[160] \"engineFeedback\":\n     §22: \"engineFeedback\"\n[162] \"promptFeedback\":\n     §22: \"promptFeedback\"\n[164] \"aiHub\": {\n     §22: \"aiHub\"\n[166] \"the game's title, as on its title screen\",\n     §19: | `name` | The game's title, as on its title screen |\n[168] \"a few words to go under the title\",\n     §19: | `tagline` |\n[170] \"one or two sentences for the catalogue\",\n     §19: | `description` |\n[172] \"Game, Tech demo or Tool\",\n     §19: | `type` | Game |\n[174] \"one of Action, Adventure, Fighting, Flight, Horror, Kids & Learning, Life Sim, Platformer, \n     §19: | `genre` | Sports |\n[175] \"source\":\n     §19: | `source` | Wii Fit |\n[180] Star Fox, or empty\",\n     §19: | `series` | Empty\n[182] \"how many can play: 1, 1–2 or 1–4\",\n     §19: | `players` | 1–4 |\n[184] \"the controls, in one line\",\n     §19: | `controls` |\n[186] [\"3 to 6 lowercase tags, e.g. 2d, 3d, arcade, story, co-op, versus, first-person, third-pers\n     §19: | `tags` | 3 to 6 lowercase\n[191] - How many issues did testing catch?\n     §19: How many issues did testing catch?\n[192] - How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n     §19: How many would be considered critical issues / game breaking?\n[193] - What was the most helpful test you ran?\n     §19: Wh