{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjHSm2TmRTrvDeu73Ary","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The hook has a point about my wording. I conflated two different things, and in doing so conceded something that isn't actually a gap:\n\n- **Coverage** is what the stop condition asks for: does the plan address the requirement?\n- **The artifacts existing** is what happens at build time. `TASK.md` asks the *builder* to write the About screen, exit file and chat report at the end of its build.\n\nSo \"not covered\" was wrong. The real question is whether section 19 covers them fully enough that a builder can't get them wrong. Reading it again, it doesn't quite: it lists the content, but there's no page layout for the About screen, no chat-reply format, no end-of-build sequence, and no test that the exit file matches the required shape. I'll fix that."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2569,"cache_read_input_tokens":833782,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2569},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"19307802-7215-43fb-a84b-6c94bbc636c5","uuid":"8413974a-03e7-44aa-93ee-0575322360e8","timestamp":"2026-11-05T06:10:53.639Z","request_id":"req_011CgjHSkfskA6aqGMHonZxg"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjHSm2TmRTrvDeu73Ary","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01YELqWcDGnnUzSk5VLSd2Th","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'EOF'\np='PLAN.md'\ns=open(p,encoding='utf-8').read()\ndef sub(old,new):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old)==1, ('COUNT %d: '%s.count(old))+old[:90]\n    s=s.replace(old,new)\n\n# the end-of-build procedure and the About layout, right where the reporting starts\na=s.index(\"**Three places, the same content.**\")\nb=s.index(\"**Source Information.** Field by field:\")\ns=s[:a]+\"\"\"**Three places, the same content.** TASK.md calls this critical: Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback go in **your chat reply** and an **in-game About/Credits screen,** and into **the exit-interview file** beside the HTML. These are your own answers from your own build. Nothing in this plan answers them for you, and section 22 is only an example. Write \"unknown\" for anything you can't confirm.\n\n**The last thing you do, in this order:**\n1. Finish the game and run `node tools/test.mjs full`.\n2. Write the exit-interview file beside the HTML, using `notes/issues.md` for the counts.\n3. Run `node tools/build.mjs`. It reads that file into `src/data/about.js`, so there is one source of truth and the three places can't disagree.\n4. Run `node tools/test.mjs unit ui`. These fail if the file doesn't parse, doesn't match TASK.md's shape, or isn't on the About screen word for word.\n5. Paste the same content into your final chat reply, in the format below.\n\nDuring development, About shows what is known so far. The final build must have no gaps: \"unknown\" is allowed only for what you truly can't confirm.\n\n**The About/Credits screen.** Reached from the main menu, and in touch-only and attract-free states the same way. It follows the 10-foot rules: large type, one page at a time, moved with left and right or clicks, never a scrolling wall of text. It is also the one screen where long answers may take several pages: split them rather than shrink them.\n\n| Page | Shows |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Source Information: Date, Source, Plan, Genre and Perspective |\n| 2 | Source Information: Tech, Art Direction, Animation |\n| 3 | Source Information: Audio, Online, Testing |\n| 4 | Source Information: Research, Environment, Model, Usage |\n| 5 | Testing Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\n| 6 | Engine Feedback: the 6 questions with your answers |\n| 7 | Prompt Feedback: the 4 questions with your answers |\n| 8 | Credits: Fredoka (SIL OFL), Three.js, Rapier, and the title |\n\nUsage must read exactly `added on import`. Keep that text as one plain string, because the agent that imports the game into AI Hub replaces it with the real cost, tokens and time. I ran this mechanism on the prototype ✔: an About screen fed from a JSON file, with a test confirming every value and answer appears on it.\n\n**Your final chat reply** has this shape, with your own answers:\n\n```\n## Source Information\n- Date: ...          (and so on for all 14 fields, in TASK.md's order)\n## Testing Feedback\n1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\n## Engine Feedback\n1. ...               (6 answers, in order)\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. ...               (4 answers, in order)\n```\n\n\"\"\"+s[b:]\n\n# the build test for the exit file's shape\nsub(\"the session client hash; the built HTML has the metas in `name`, `content` order,\",\n    \"the session client hash; **the exit-interview file**, once it exists: parses, has exactly TASK.md's keys, 4, 6 and 4 feedback answers, no empty value except `series`, `usage` reading `added on import`, 3 to 6 lowercase tags, `genre` from TASK.md's list, `name` equal to the title constant; the built HTML has the metas in `name`, `content` order,\")\n\n# coverage table: say plainly how these are covered\nsub(\"| Source Information, all 14 fields, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19, field by field | `ui`: About shows every value from the file ✔. Your chat reply |\",\n    \"| Source Information, all 14 fields, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: field by field, the About pages, the chat format, and the order to do it in | `ui`: About shows every value from the file ✔. `unit`: the file's shape. Your chat reply |\")\nsub(\"| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19, every question listed; section 17's tally | `ui`: About shows every answer ✔. Your chat reply |\",\n    \"| Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback, in chat and About/Credits | Section 19: every question listed, the About pages, the chat format; section 17's tally for the counts | `ui`: About shows every answer ✔. `unit`: 4, 6 and 4 answers. Your chat reply |\")\nsub(\"| Exit-interview file: shape, parses, `aiHub` block | Section 19; shape in section 22 | `unit`: parses and has every key |\",\n    \"| Exit-interview file: beside the HTML, exact shape, plain text, parses, `aiHub` block | Section 19: what to write and when; shape in section 22 | `unit`: parses, exact keys, every value filled |\")\nsub(\"| No controller, touch, listener, second player, iPhone or TV: use stand-ins | Section 17, Stand-ins | `input`, `ui`, `audio`, `hub` ✔ |\",\n    \"| No controller, touch, listener, second player, iPhone or TV: test with the stand-ins, \\\"never required\\\" to have the real thing | Section 17, Stand-ins: these stand-ins are what TASK.md asks for | `input`, `ui`, `audio`, `hub` ✔ |\")\n\n# the intro of section 21: what coverage means\nsub(\"Every requirement in TASK.md, in its order: where this plan answers it, and what proves it was met when you build. Keyword matches prove nothing about a game, so the last column is what counts. Test groups are in section 17.\",\n    \"Every requirement in TASK.md, in its order: where this plan answers it, and what proves it was met when you build. Test groups are in section 17. Some of what TASK.md asks for can only exist once there is a build: the game itself, its About/Credits screen, the exit-interview file and your final report. This plan covers those by saying exactly what to produce, when, and how it gets checked. Producing them is the build's job, and yours.\")\nsub(\"TASK.md's reporting belongs to whoever builds the game, and only your answers count for your build. Here is the same report for this planning stage, so you can see the format filled in and what was learned. There is no game at this stage, so there is no About screen of its own: the mechanism was proven on the prototype (section 20).\",\n    \"TASK.md's reporting belongs to whoever builds the game, and only your answers count for your build (section 19 says how). Here is the same report for this planning stage, so you can see the format filled in and what was learned.\")\nopen(p,'w',encoding='utf-8').write(s)\n\nimport re, json\nparts=re.split(r'^## (\\d+)\\. ',s,flags=re.M); secs={int(parts[i]):parts[i+1] for i in range(1,len(parts),2)}\nmine=json.loads(re.search(r'```json\\n(.*?)```',secs[22],flags=re.S).group(1))\nprint('section 22 JSON parses:',bool(mine),'| About page table rows:',len(re.findall(r'^\\| \\d \\|',secs[19],flags=re.M)))\nfields=['Date','Source','Plan','Genre and Perspective','Tech','Art Direction','Animation','Audio','Online','Testing','Research','Environment','Model','Usage']\ntable=' '.join(re.findall(r'^\\| \\d \\| (.*) \\|$',secs[19],flags=re.M))\nprint('all 14 fields placed on an About page:',all(f in table for f in fields))\nprint('banned names:',[b for b in ['GameTitle','exit-interview.json','claude-opus-5-5-max','20261104','planner-exit'] if b in s] or 'none')\nEOF\nsed -n '/^\\*\\*The last thing you do/,/^\\*\\*Your final chat reply/p' PLAN.md | cut -c1-130 | head -32; ls -la; wc -w PLAN.md","description":"Make the plan's reporting section complete and verify it"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"us